AB  - In an attempt to respond more effectively to the problems of methadone maintenance patients with persistently drug-positive urines, two long term psychotherapy groups were established at a community based, university affiliated methadone clinic. Patients with poor urine profiles were offered participation as an alternative to clinic termination or referral to inpatient treatment. The groups were run by counselors from the clinic, and incorporated emotional support, psycho education, relapse prevention, social skills development, and encouragement of 12-step group participation. A chart review was conducted covering the first 18 months of group treatment. Average attendance was 66% for Group 1 and 71% for Group 2. The percentage of drug positive urines was reduced from 64% at baseline to 40% at 18 month follow-up for Group 1, and from 70% at baseline to 33% at 18 month follow-up for Group 2. This experience suggests a cost effective approach to refractory methadone patients based on established treatment principles.
