AB  - This symposium will generate discussion of behavioral addictions among Addiction Psychiatrists, and provide a foothold on the application of SUD treatment principles to addictive behaviors. Many people report struggling with what they term "behavioral addictions", but few seek-out an Addiction Psychiatrist. Many SUD patients have comorbid behaviors that are as concerning as the substance use. We will discuss some examples of addictive behaviors, review treatment, and propose limits on the proper use of the term "addiction."Opioid use disorder is a major public health problem across North America and many other parts of the world with increasing prevalence and mortality from overdoses. Fortunately, effective medications exist, including agonist substitution strategies with the full agonist methadone and partial agonist buprenorphine. The antagonist naltrexone was of limited effectiveness when only available in pill form due to poor adherence, but recently developed long-acting injectable naltrexone, as well as long-acting implants in some countries, have shown clear effectiveness compared to counseling without medication, placebo or oral naltrexone. Educational Objectives: At the conclusion of this activity, participants should be able to: 1) Outline the evidence from clinical trials supporting the effectiveness of buprenorphine and injection naltrexone for treatment of opioid use disorder. 2) Review the findings from the first two trials directly comparing the effectiveness of buprenorphine and injection naltrexone for treatment of opioid use disorder 3) Identify patient characteristics that would influence patient-treatment matching and the choice of which medication is most likely to be effective for a given patient. Background and Objective: The need for effective opioid use disorder (OUD) treatment is urgent. The objective of this symposium is to review the findings of two recent randomized controlled trials directly comparing long-acting injection naltrexone and buprenorphine-naloxone. Method(s): Two open-label multi-site randomized controlled trials compared the effectiveness of daily sublingual buprenorphine-naloxone and monthly long-acting injection naltrexone in adult OUD patients. In a 24-week U.S. trial 287 participants received buprenorphine-naloxone while 283 received long-acting injection naltrexone. The primary outcome was relapse-free survival. In a 12-week Norwegian trial 79 participants received buprenorphine-naloxone while 80 received long-acting injection naltrexone. Primary endpoints were trial completion, proportion of weekly opioidnegative urine tests, and self-reported number of days of illicit opioid use. A 9 month Norwegian follow-up trial allowed participants to choose their treatment; their opioid use was then monitored. Result(s): In the U.S. trial, significantly fewer participants completed initiation with long-acting injection naltrexone than buprenorphine-naloxone. Intent-to-treat (ITT) analyses showed significantly higher relapse rates in the long-acting injection naltrexone group than the buprenorphine-naloxone group mostly due to initiation failure; per protocol analyses (analyses restricted to participants who completed initiation of medication) showed comparable relapse rates. Adverse events were comparable. In the Norwegian trial completion and proportion of opioid negative urine tests were comparable, but the long-acting injection naltrexone group reported significantly less days of illicit opioid use. The long-acting injection naltrexone group had more adverse events primarily due to increased withdrawal events. Interestingly, in the follow up trial 117 participants chose long-acting injection naltrexone, including some who had previously received buprenorphinenaloxone. Opioid use was low among patients who remained in treatment. Heroin use was also reduced among patients who had switched from buprenorphine-naloxone to long-acting injection naltrexone. 37 participants reported adverse events, most of which were withdrawal symptoms in participants who had switched from buprenorphine-naloxone to long-acting injection naltrexone. Conclusions and Discussion: Overall, the findings suggest that long-acting injection naltrexone is comparable in effectiveness to sublingual buprenorphine-naloxone particularly among patients who succeed in initiating long-acting injection naltrexone. Initiation of long-acting injection naltrexone remains challenging because patients must endure some degree of opioid withdrawal, and some will then drop out before completing initiation. More research is needed to improve the process of initiating long-acting injection naltrexone. Once long-acting injection naltrexone is initiated, these trials show that it is well tolerated. Taken together, findings from the two trials suggest that long-acting injection naltrexone may be the better treatment for some patients while for others buprenorphine may be better. Selecting the best treatment for an individual patient remains another challenge for future research. Another important point is that about 50% of patients drop out from treatment on buprenorphine or long-acting injection naltrexone by 3 to 6 months. More research is needed on how to improve adherence to these effective medications over the long term, since the risk of relapse is high if a patient discontinues their medication. An important next step for research on long-acting injection naltrexone is to begin studying how this drug can be integrated into clinical treatment beyond strict research protocols. It will be important to see how and when long-acting injection naltrexone can be applied across the many different types of clinical settings in which patients with opioid use disorder present. It is important as well to broaden the view of outcome, from examining the outcome of a single trial of medication, as was done in these randomized trials, to examining long term course, where patients with opioid use disorder often stop treatment, relapse, and resume treatment in various patterns over time. A chronic disease model was advocated, similar to the approach to chronic mental illness, wherein treatment success is viewed as not only preventing relapses but also minimizing the time spent in relapse and successfully lifting patients from relapse back into remission. It will also be important to consider how the medications for opioid use disorder, including buprenorphine and longacting injection naltrexone, can best be incorporated into clinical settings where they have not traditionally been offered, ranging from primary care medical practices to counseling-based addiction treatment programs. Such settings will need to adopt the use of these medications in order to reach all patients afflicted with opioid use disorder and to have an effective public health response to the opioid epidemic. Lastly, more research on medication treatments for opioid use disorder is needed in rural areas, which present unique healthcare access challenges. Scientific Significance: Long-acting injection naltrexone is an effective treatment for OUD and can join our armamentarium of effective treatments along with buprenorphine and methadone. More work is needed on how to improve successful initiation onto long-acting injection naltrexone, how to improve adherence over time to medication treatments for OUD, and how to best integrate effective OUD medications into established clinical protocols across various treatment centers and clinical settings.
