AB  - Evaluates and contrasts 2 culturally sensitive AIDS-education programs delivered to 213 impoverished Latina homeless or drug-addicted women (aged 18–75 yrs). Ss were randomly assigned to a traditional intervention group that provided AIDS education and community resources or a specialized intervention group that added reinforcement of risk-reduction skills and enhancement of self-esteem and control. Ss from both groups received HIV testing and returned for a 2-wk follow-up. Measures of cognitive, behavioral, psychological, sociodemographic, and acculturation variables were administered. Ss in both groups improved significantly in cognitive, behavioral, and psychologic outcomes, except for problem-focused coping. Ss showed decreased appraisal of threat, current concerns, distress, and depression and reported less drug use and sex with multiple partners. Ss' knowledge of and attitudes toward AIDS also improved. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
