Y1  - 2022///
AB  - The present study experimentally examined the effects of a heart rate variability (HRV) biofeedback intervention among individuals attending a Partial Hospitalization Program (PHP) or Intensive Outpatient Program (IOP) for substance abuse or co-occurring disorders. Substance abuse is a major public health issue with widespread consequences, and can be difficult to treat and difficult for individuals to maintain their recovery. Emerging research demonstrates that HRV biofeedback can be effective at reducing substance cravings and is likely to serve as a helpful adjunct to substance abuse treatment. Substance abuse is known to disrupt autonomic nervous system functioning and activity in neural networks responsible for stress processing and emotion regulation, and emotional dysregulation and the presence of cravings are common precipitants of substance abuse relapse. Improvement in stress processing and affect regulation utilizing HRV biofeedback may aid individuals in treatment for substance abuse to reduce cravings and improve treatment outcomes. Multiple studies have demonstrated that HRV biofeedback contributes to a reduction of cravings within an inpatient or residential treatment setting, however no studies to date have explored the use of HRV biofeedback in an outpatient setting. Adult participants of any gender (N = 58) were randomly assigned to either the PHP/IOP treatment-as-usual (TAU) condition consisting primarily of group therapy, or the TAU plus five, once weekly sessions of HRV biofeedback condition. Primary outcomes included validated self-report measures of alcohol or drug cravings, stress and anxiety symptoms, and symptoms of depression. Participants in both conditions additionally underwent an assessment of their autonomic nervous system functioning at study Week 1 and study Week 5 to explore the relationship between change in HRV and reduction in cravings. It was hypothesized that participants in both conditions would demonstrate reductions in cravings, stress, and depression scores over time. Furthermore, it was hypothesized that the HRV biofeedback condition would demonstrate greater reductions in cravings than the TAU comparison condition, and that the HRV biofeedback group would demonstrate reductions in stress and depression symptoms equal to or greater than the TAU condition. Finally, it was hypothesized that the HRV biofeedback group would experience a significant increase in HRV compared to the TAU condition and that change in HRV would mediate reductions in craving scores. Although change in HRV was not found to explain the degree of reduction in cravings observed in the experimental condition, a statistically significant reduction in cravings was found among the HRV biofeedback group with a moderate effect size, while participants in the TAU comparison condition did not demonstrate a significant reduction in cravings. The findings suggest a possibility that factors other than change in HRV associated with the use of HRV biofeedback contribute to reductions in cravings beyond TAU alone. Results of the study demonstrate the clinical utility of HRV biofeedback in the outpatient treatment of substance abuse as a potential tool for reducing cravings. Future research is needed to determine whether this reduction in cravings contributes to longer-term improvement in treatment outcomes such as a reduction in the rate of substance abuse relapse. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
