AB  - Background: WANDAS is the only service providing this model in Western Australia. WANDAS is a midwifery led multi-disciplinary service providing specialist pregnancy care to women dealing with alcohol and other drug (AOD) use and related issues. It comprises of team members from the Medical, Midwifery and Allied Health professions, NGO provision of counselling and Addiction Specialist from Next Step. WANDAS is based at King Edward Memorial Hospital. It is the largest obstetric AOD service in Australia. The WANDAS model includes the provision of outreach service to pregnant prisoners. WANDAS provides a Post Natal follow up service for 3 to 4 months postnatally as well as psychiatric medical and social work follow up for 6 months. The 4 month pathway is for women and infants who have increased alcohol use in pregnancy. This is a new initiative for WANDAS and the challenge includes ongoing funding to secure the follow-up and early intervention for Fetal Alcohol Spectrum Disorder (FASD). Aim(s): The aim and objective of the audit was to review the service model in relation to Access to care, breastfeeding and contraception rates, number of pregnant prisoners and challenges of provision of care. The audit also included the rates of iron infusions given to pregnant women attending WANDAS. Method(s): A retrospective audit of 210 pregnant women attending WANDAS in 2017 was conducted. The setting was specialist drug and alcohol pregnancy tertiary service in Perth WA. Pregnant women attending the specialist drug and alcohol service (N = 210) participated in the study. Result(s): The number of referrals put in place for continuing health care will be presented and we will suggest ways by which gaps in the system could be closed,enabling the woman to make maximum use of contact with the health care professional during the care with the service. Implications for practice: Births from women using Alcohol and other Drug Use (AOD) are associated with a range of poor obstetric and neonatal outcomes. Attendance for antenatal care for mothers and their babies is seen as the best outcome in order to gain access to timely diagnostic expertise, psychosocial support, and referral to services (i.e. referral to paediatric services, and parenting support services).Copyright © 2018
