AB  - The influence of Alcoholics Anonymous (AA) is ubiquitous in the treatment of alcohol use disorders in the United States. Twelve step therapy, one of the prevailing approaches in the U.S., fully embraces AA ideology and practice while non-12 step therapies such as CBT encourage AA attendance during and after treatment (Morgenstern et. al. (2002). Little is known, however, about how working the 12-steps in treatment enhances engagement in 12-step behavior after treatment nor how it may improve longer-term outcome. The goal of the present study is to investigate if individuals who worked steps in treatment would engage in recommended AA related behaviors such as: working steps, attending meetings, and obtaining an AA sponsor at a higher rate than those who did not work steps in treatment. Further, as these behaviors have been associated with reductions in alcohol use, we evaluated the differences between these two groups on percent days abstinent (PDA) and percent drinking days (PDD). This study was conducted in the context of a larger two-year longitudinal study investigating AA related behavior change (R01AA014197). Measures key to this investigation included the Form 90, Alcoholics Anonymous Inventory (AAI), and General Alcoholics Anonymous Tools of Recovery (GAATOR), all of which have strong psychometric properties. Study participants (N = 253) with limited AA exposure (i.e. less than 16 lifetime weeks) were evaluated in three-month increments for 12-months. Results indicate that individuals who reported "working the steps" in treatment were more likely to work additional AA steps after treatment, get a sponsor, and attend AA meetings relative to individuals without within treatment experiences. Further, those who worked steps in treatment were significantly more likely to reduce their drinking over 12-months relative to persons without prior 12-step work. This study offers initial support for the practice of including AA step work in formal treatment for AUD when AA engagement is desired.
