Y1  - 2018///
AB  - Introduction: Adherence to pulmonary rehabilitation (PR) is low. Previous studies have focused on clinical predictors of PR completion. This mixed methods research aimed to identify social determinants of adherence to PR. Method(s): Sequential explanatory design was used, with a qualitative phase following the quantitative phase to increase the interpretability of findings. Quantitative phase: A clinical database of COPD patients (N=440) in an outpatient PR program was geocoded. Using exploratory factor analysis, a socioeconomic disadvantage index was constructed from 15 U.S. Census variables aggregated to Census tracts. Patient measures included age, sex, race, BMI, smoking status, pack years, baseline 6-minute walk distance (6MWD: <150, 150-249, >=250), comorbidities, depression, and prescribed PR sessions (<=20, 21-30, >30). Adherence, a ratio of attended-to-prescribed sessions, was coded as low (<35%), moderate (35-85%), and high (>85%). Multivariate regression models were adjusted for clustering on Census tracts. Qualitative phase: Focus groups with COPD patients who had attended PR were conducted to identify barriers and facilitators of PR adherence. Sessions were recorded, transcribed verbatim, and coded independently by two coders, who then jointly decided on the final coding scheme. Data were summarized across groups. NVivo 11 was used for analyses. Result(s): Quantitative: 26% of patients had low adherence, 23% were moderately adherent, 51% were highly adherent. In the best fitted full model, each decile increase in the socioeconomic disadvantage index increased the risk of moderate vs high adherence by 14% (p<0.01). Smoking tripled the risk of low vs high adherence (p<0.01), while each next 6MWD category decreased that risk by 72% (p<0.01) and 84% (p<0.001), respectively. Results highlighted different pathways to suboptimal adherence, whereby low adherence is associated with smoking and poor health, while moderate adherence is associated with socioeconomic disadvantage. Qualitative: Five focus groups with a total of 24 participants were conducted. Mean age was 62 years, 50% male, 67% Black, 52% with income <$20,000, and 17% current smokers; 54% had low, 21% moderate, and 25% high adherence. The most discussed themes were personal determination, physical health, transportation, and program features. Barriers to completion of PR included transportation and parking, physical limitations, and lack of determination. Facilitators included motivation and supportive staff. Proposed solutions were assistance with transportation, flexible program scheduling, incentives to maintain interest, education about the disease, and smoking cessation. Conclusion(s): Socioeconomic disadvantage and health independently impact patients' ability to attend PR. Interventions tailored to the needs of specific subgroups of patients should be tested.
