AB  - The authors answer this question by assessing whether two measures of health — self-reported health status and functional disability — affect an array of electoral and nonelectoral forms of participation, political trust and efficacy, satisfaction with democracy, political knowledge, political ideology, and policy preferences. [...]Finland’s status as a Nordic welfare state with a robust system of universal health care and moderately high levels of political participation make it a “least likely case” for where we would expect poor health to have pernicious effects on political behavior. Prior research (full disclosure, my prior research 5,6 ) has identified large negative effects of depression on turnout, while others have shown positive effects of breast cancer on political activity. 10 Even the prior research of these authors is more adventurous when it comes to the measurement of health (e.g., sick leave, heart disease, alcoholism, etc.). 10 S. King, “ Pink Ribbons Inc: Breast cancer activism and the politics of philanthropy,” International Journal of Qualitative Studies in Education, 2004, 17 (4): 473–492.
