AB  - Sought to develop a measure to assess patients' response to civil commitment, to test this measure on 2 groups of dually diagnosed patients (medically ill alcoholics and patients with dual mental and substance use disorders), and to identify patient characteristics associated with a positive response to commitment. The outcome of 38 male veterans (aged 22–72 yrs) civilly committed to inpatient substance abuse treatment was rated by their treating clinicians. Raters used the Commitment Response Form (CRF), a scale anchored to behavioral descriptions that was developed for the study. Ss' medical records were reviewed by clinical staff members who made independent retrospective ratings and a joint rating using the CRF, and also rated whether the S was a positive- or non-responder to civil commitment. The CRF showed superior reliability when compared with the dichotomous rating of outcome and demonstrated reasonable psychometric properties. Mean scale scores did not differ significantly by patient group; slightly more than half were rated as having a good to excellent overall response. Better outcome was associated with longer periods of previous abstinence from alcohol and a higher level of education. Civil commitment resulted in good to excellent outcome in many but not all Ss. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
