AB  - A model of ongoing consultation was implemented in a community group home for 8 adults with severe and profound mental retardation. Two consultants taught 9 staff members to use a token reinforcement system, engage the adults in a variety of activities, and improve the content and style of staff members' interactions with the adults. The consultants taught through workshops, direct observation of staff members' use of skills during regular activities in the home, and individual verbal feedback. Evaluation of the consultation process showed it to be effective in improving staff members' performance and in changing behaviors of the adults. Continued implementation of the process, however, appeared necessary for staff members' behavior changes to be maintained at high levels. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
