AB  - Changes in Vascular Nitric Oxide/Cyclic Guanosine Monophosphate System in Salt‐Loaded Stroke‐Prone Spontaneously Hypertensive Rats.Satomi Kagota and Masaru Kunitomo. Department of Pharmacology, School of Pharmaceutical Sciences, Mukogawa Women's University, Nishinomiya, Hyogo 663‐8179, Japan. Excessive dietary salt causes downregulation of soluble guanylate cyclase (sGC) in aortas of spontaneously hypertensive rats (SHR) followed by reduced cyclic guanosine monophosphate (cGMP) production leading to impairment of vascular relaxation in response to nitric oxide (NO) (Br J Pharmacol 2001; 134:737–744). Furthermore, dysfunction of this NO/cGMP system has been found to result from a direct effect of salt itself not from increased blood pressure due to high salt intake (J Pharmacol Exp Ther 2002; 302:344–351). Therefore, the present study was designed to determine whether salt loading alters the NO/cGMP system in aortas from stroke‐prone spontaneously hypertensive rats (SHRSP), in which salt is known to accelerate the development of hypertension. Male Wistar Kyoto rats (WKY/Izm) (n = 6) and SHRSP/Izm (n = 6) at 8 weeks of age were divided into two groups each. One group of each rat strain received normal drinking water ad libitum for 4 weeks, and the other group received 1% NaCl as drinking water. All animals were maintained on a regular diet (RC‐4, Japan SLC, Hamamatsu, Japan). At the end of the experiment, blood samples were taken from the abdominal aorta under anesthesia with pentobarbital sodium. The thoracic aortas were immediately removed and placed in Krebs‐Henseleit solution. The aortic rings were prepared and fixed vertically under a resting tension of 1.0 g in a 10‐mL organ bath filled with the solution described above. The rings were preconstricted with phenylephrine (0.1–0.3 µM), and then acetylcholine, adenosine diphosphate, sodium nitroprusside or nitroglycerin was cumulatively added to the bath medium. The isometric tension change was measured with a force–displacement transducer (Model t‐7, NEC San‐Ei, Tokyo, Japan) coupled to a dual channel chart recorder (Model 8K21, NEC San‐Ei). The protein levels of endothelial NO synthase (eNOS) and sGC in the aortas were determined by western blot analysis. The plasma NO2− plus NO3− level were determined using a commercial kit (NO2/NO3 Assay Kit‐C, Dojindo Laboratories, Kumamoto, Japan) based on the Griess reaction. In aortic rings from the salt‐loaded SHRSP, endothelium‐dependent relaxations in response to acetylcholine and adenosine diphosphate were significantly impaired as compared to those in the control SHRSP. Endothelium‐independent relaxations in response to sodium nitroprusside and nitroglycerin were also significantly impaired. However, no significant differences were found in the protein levels of eNOS or sGC between aortas from the salt‐loaded SHRSP and control groups. Plasma NO2− plus NO3− levels were significantly increased in the salt‐loaded SHRSP as compared to those in the control. In contrast, these changes by salt loading were not observed in WKY. These results indicate that in aortas of SHRSP, as found with SHR, dietary salt leads to impairment of relaxation responses to NO, which may be due to dysfunction of the NO/cGMP system in smooth muscle cells. Thus, the impairment may not be due to a decrease in sGC protein expression as observed in SHR, but to reduced activity of sGC or accelerated degradation of cGMP. Non‐invasive Measurement of Oxidative Stress in Spontaneously Hypertension Rat (SHR) and Stroke‐Prone SHR (SHRSP) Brain Using In Vivo Electron Spin Resonance (ESR) Spectroscopy—A Possibility of In Vivo Assessment for Antioxidant Property of Novel Drugs and Foods.Masaichi‐Chang‐il Lee,1Hirofumi Shoji,1Hiroyuki Miyazaki,1Kazunori Anzai,2and Toshihiko Ozawa.21Department of Pharmacology and ESR Laboratories, Kanagawa Dental College, Yokosuka, Japan 238‐8580. 2National Institute of Radiological Sciences, Anagawa, Chiba 263‐8555, Japan. Free radical reactions are associated with oxidative stress‐mediated alterations under pathophysiological conditions, and particularly in brain ischemia, brain tumor, and neurodegenerative diseases. Therefore, we need to understand the physiological and pathophysiological role of free radical generation in the brain. This study examine two nitroxyl compounds, blood–brain‐barrier (BBB)‐permeable 3‐methoxycarbonyl‐2,2,5,5‐tetramethyl‐pyrrolidine‐1‐yloxy (MC‐PROXYL) and BBB‐impermeable carbamoyl‐PROXYL, as spin probes for in vivo ESR technique. Preliminary comparisons were made by ESR imaging of the heads of live mice and isolated rat brains using MC‐PROXYL and the carbamoyl‐PROXYL. The results showed that MC‐PROXYL, but not carbamolyl‐PROXYL, was widely distributed in the brain. These methods were also applied for the imaging of isolated brains from SHR and SHRSP. The rapid decay of 2D ESR images of MC‐PROXYL in isolated SHR‐brain and SHRSP‐brain was observed, compared to Wistar‐Kyoto rats (WKYs), using the ESR imaging system. Furthermore, we provided evidence that the decay rate constant of MC‐PROXYL in the head region was faster in live SHRs and SHRSPs than in live WKYs, by using L‐band ESR non‐invasively. Taken together, the high oxidative stress sustained by oxygen radical generation in SHR and SHRSP may cause the alteration of MC‐PROXYL metabolism in the brain. Our results suggest that in vivo ESR could be applied to the assessment of antioxidant effects on oxidative stress in the brain of animal disease models, such as the SHR and SHRSP. Furthermore, after screening test of antioxidant effects of drugs and foods using in vivo ESR technique, we will be able to develop and find drugs and food with novel antioxidant property in the near future. cDNA Microarray Analysis of Gene Expression in the Heart of SHRSP.Subrina Jesmin,1Hiroko Togashi,2Ichiro Sakuma,1Ken‐ichi Ueno,2Mitsuhiro Yoshioka,2and Akira Kitabatake.11Department of Cardiovascular Med. and 2Pharmacology, Hokkaido University School of Medicine, Sapporo 060‐8638, Japan. The main purpose of this present study was to investigate in detail the expressions of molecules related to angiogenesis and apoptosis in the heart of stroke prone spontaneously hypertensive rat (SHRSP) by cDNA microarray analysis, Real‐time PCR, immunohistochemistry, Western blotting and in situ hybridization. cDNA microarray technology was used to evaluate the expression of >4000 genes in the heart of SHRSP and that of Wistar‐Kyoto rat (WKY). Changes in gene expression of SHRSP heart were monitored from prehypertensive stage (6 week‐old) to malignant hypertensive stage (40 week‐old), and compared with those of age‐matched WKY heart. Profoundly altered (>2 fold or more) genes in 20 week‐old SHRSP were p53, p63α, GAD45, NF1, p35, p21, cDC46, WT1, Egr‐1, STAT5, E2F3, E2F5, MAD3, c‐myc, Smad3, Smad5, estrogen receptor‐α and ‐β, FAK, Btk, TGF‐β, ‐β2, and ‐β3, VEGF, P1GF, PDGF‐β, IGF‐BP3, TNF‐α, GM‐CSF, IFN‐γ and ‐β, F1t‐1, FGFR‐3, TGFβR‐1, IGF‐1R, DDR‐1, IL‐3Rα and ‐11Rα, SH‐PTP1, p120, MDR1, TIMP‐2, MMP‐10, 14, 16, and 17, H‐ras, M‐ras, Ran BP‐1, Rab 4, 7, and 10, SID 99, CD8α, E‐ and P‐cadherin, CD51, E‐selectin, laminin‐α1 and ‐γ1, Fbn‐2, thrombospondin 1 and 4, Smad 7, PAI‐1, HIC1, GSTT1, phospholipase A2, endonuclease G, and luciferase. When these rats developed malignant hypertension (40 week‐old), around 38 genes showed 2 fold or more alterations with some new genes that were not profoundly changed in hypertensive stage (20 week‐old). VEGF, the potent angiogenic growth factor was highly upregulated in 20 week‐old SHRSP heart at both protein and mRNA levels, whereas its angiogenic receptor, KDR was downregulated or unchanged in mRNA expression although its protein level was increased. Both VEGF and KDR were significantly downregulated in 40 week‐old SHRSP heart at both protein and mRNA levels. bFGF, another potent angiogenic growth factor and its receptor subtype‐3 was highly upregulated in 20 week‐old SHRSP heart, however, both of these expression were decreased when SHRSP became 40 weeks old. eNOS runs in parallel with VEGF in an age‐related manner. Caspase family and its activated nuclease were upregulated in both 20 and 40 week‐old SHRSP heart, but the degree of upregulation was more obvious at 20 weeks compared to 40 weeks. VEGF, KDR, bFGF, eNOS, Caspase‐3, Bax, and Bcl‐2 were unchanged in 6 week‐old SHRSP heart compared to WKY heart. Stroke prone spontaneously hypertensive rat showed a remarkable hypertrophy of cardiomyocytes and a low capillary density in the left ventricular wall. Altered expression of the genes identified in this study may contribute to the development of hypertensive complications and/or represent compensatory mechanisms to sustain cardiac function against malignant hypertension. The novel finding of this study is the upregulation of VEGF and the downregulation of its angiogenic receptor, KDR at mRNA level although its protein expression is increased at established hypertensive stage of SHRSP (20 week‐old). The precise mechanism underlying this result remains for the future study, however, it seems probable that VEGF and KDR are regulated through independent molecular mechanism. The observed changes in expressions of molecules related to angiogenesis and apoptosis may reflect the compensatory cardiac remodeling in SHRSP. It would be most challenging and promising to explore why both VEGF and KDR were downregulated when SHRSP developed malignant hypertension. Effect of Locus Coeruleus on Fractal Structure in Circadian Variation of Heart Rate in Spontaneously Hypertensive Rats.Hiroshi Kawamura and Hiromi Mitsubayashi. Department of Medicine, The Nippon Dental University School of Dentistry, Tokyo 102‐8158, Japan. Noradrenergic nucleus locus coeruleus (LC) is an important sensory and integrating nucleus in the pons that responds to various internal and external environmental stimuli. The external stimuli may act as one of the synchronizers in circadian variation of the heart rate (HR). We previously demonstrated that HR in spontaneously hypertensive rats/Izm (SHR) had circadian variation. Heart rate in SHR may have a fractal structure in time‐series analysis. Therefore, the purpose of this study was to test the hypothesis that HR in SHR has a fractal structure in time‐series analysis and that LC may be involved in the circadian variation of HR. Ten mature male SHR (23 ± 3 week‐old, body weight: 278 ± 8 g) and ten age‐sex matched Wistar‐Kyoto rats/Izm (WKY) (25 ± 3 week‐old, body weight: 285 ± 9 g as controls) were used. They were kept in a thermally controlled (23°C), 12‐hour light‐cycled (07:00 on 19:00 off) environment and fed lab chow ad libitum. After the abdominal aortic catheter was implanted and the rat had recovered completely, 24‐hour monitoring of HR was performed. Heart rate was telemetrically measured. Chemical LC lesions were also made by intra‐peritoneal injection of DSP‐4 (5 mg/100 g). The variation of HR was analyzed using maximum entropy method (MEM) before and after chemical LC lesion (DSP‐4). Heart rate clearly showed the circadian variations in both WKY and SHR. The highest HR was during the dark period, and the lowest was during the light period. The peak period (1/f) of power density was 24.55 ± 1.88 hr for WKY and 23.84 ± 1.51 hr for SHR. The circadian period (1/f) in HR did not differ between WKY and SHR. After DSP‐4, the power spectral densities in HR were also analyzed in both WKY and SHR. DSP‐4 did not affect HR periods (1/f) in both WKY and SHR (WKY, 24.07 ± 0.81 hr vs. SHR, 24.80 ± 2.06 hr, n.s.). However an inverse relationship in both SHR and WKY existed between an observed period (1/f) in HR and a log–log representation of power spectral density. The slope of 1/f in both WKY and SHR still did not differ (−1.35 ± 0.61 vs. −1.42 ± 0.43, n.s.). The slope of 1/f in HR still did not change before and after DSP‐4 and between WKY and SHR (−1.44 ± 0.83 vs. −1.60 ± 0.45 n.s.). The amplitude of HR variation tended to be greater in SHR than in WKY (35.09 ± 8.11 vs. 26.72 ± 6.53 mmHg, n.s.). However the amplitude of SHR HR tended to be smaller after DSP‐4 (35.09 ± 8.11–22.42 ± 8.88 mmHg, n.s.). The important findings of this studies were: although 1/f characteristics in HR exist between the frequency and the power of spectral densities in both SHR and WKY, the 1/f characteristics of HR did not alter after DSP‐4. The power spectra of HR still keeps the 1/f characteristic. Therefore, LC may not be involved in the circadian variation of HR in both SHR and WKY. Hence, it is clear that DSP‐4 has almost negligible effects on 1/f noise. The circadian variation of HR is similar in the two strains (SHR and WKY). Glomerulosclerosis and Glomerular Cell Apoptosis After Prolonged Nitric Oxide Synthase Blockade in Spontaneously Hypertensive Rats.Yukana Chihara,1Hidehiko Ono,1Yuko Ono,2Shigeko Mori,1Megumi Teranishi,1Atsuko Ogata,1Mayumi Maeda,1Hideki Inada,1Toshihiko Ishimitsu,1and Hiroaki Matsuoka.11Department of Hypertension and Cardiorenal Disease and 2Department of Pathology, Dokkyo University School of Medicine, 880 Kitakobayashi Mibu, Shimotsuga, Tochigi, Japan. This study was designed to investigate the relationship between glomerular cell (endothelial, epithelial, and mesangial cells) apoptosis and glomerular injuries in spontaneously hypertensive rats (SHR) treated with NG‐nitro‐L‐arginine methyl ester (L‐NAME). Development of glomerular cell apoptosis was evaluated by assessment of renal hemodynamics, glomerular morphometric changes, and participation of the renin–angiotensin system. 20‐week‐old SHR were invited three groups; control SHR and two similar groups given two doses of L‐NAME (50 or 80 mg L−1) for 3 weeks. Mean arterial pressure (Con, L‐NAME (50 mg), L‐NAME (80 mg); 185 ± 4, 230 ± 7, 221 ± 11 mmHg), renal vascular resistance (32 ± 2, 113 ± 35, 226 ± 53 mmHg mL−1 min−1), and stop flow pressure by micropuncture technique increased, significantly, whereas effective renal plasma flow (3.09 ± 0.03, 1.77 ± 0.3, 0.82 ± 0.2 mL min−1 g−1) and glomerular filtration rate (0.90 ± 0.1, 0.48 ± 0.1, 0.55 ± 0.1 mL min−1 g−1) were diminished by L‐NAME. Glomerular (10.2 ± 0.3, 10.2 ± 0.4, 9.5 ± 0.3 × 103_m2) and glomerular tuft area (115 ± 4, 82 ± 2, 88 ± 3_m2) were diminished as intraglomerular cell number (35 ± 1, 31.7 ± 0.8, 29.7 ± 0.8 number/1Glo) as endothelial, epithelial, and mesangial cells was diminished, significantly. Apoptosis (TUNEL index; 0.99 ± 0.12, 3.81 ± 0.32, 3.2 ± 0.5%/1Glo) and proliferative index (PCNA index; 5.5 ± 1.3, 27.7 ± 2.2, 9.3 ± 1.1%/1Glo) of nonsclerotic glomeruli were induced by low dose of L‐NAME, and cortical tissue level of angiotensin II (Con, L‐NAME(80 mg); 228 ± 24, 755 ± 105 pg mg−1 wt, p < 0.01), caspase‐3 (apoptosis inducer) and transforming growth factor (TGF)‐β1 mRNA were increased in L‐NAME, significantly. These results indicate that intrarenal angiotension II activation and glomerular hypertension were related to glomerular cell deletion and apoptosis together with the pathophysiological changes of severe nephrosclerosis and impaired glomerular dynamics. In addition, glomerular endothelial cell loss was also related to glomerular capillary thrombosis and mesangliolysis associated with altered renal and intraglomerular hemodynamics and, structurally, by diminished glomerular capillary lumina. Regulation of 11 β‐Hydroxysteroid Dehydrogenase by Renin–Angiotension–Aldosterone System in SHRSP.Lila Otani, Norifumi Shirasaka, Hajime Yoshisumi, and Tetsuo Murakami. Department of Food and Nutrition, Faculty of Agriculture, Kinki University Nara, Japan. 11 β‐HSD2 is the enzyme which converts the glucocorticoid (GC) into its inactive 11 ketosteroid, and protects the mineralcorticoid receptor (MR) from high GC concentraton. It was reported that aldostrone plays a major role in the development of vascular injury in salt loading SHRSP. The author showed that the abnormality of the renal 11 β‐HSD2 regulation was recognized in a low protein exposed rat during salt loading. Moreover, the 11 β‐HSD2 activity in the kidneys of a WKY on high salt drinking was significantly increased compared with water drinking groups, but the SHRSP on high salt drinking was not increased and did not differ from the water drinking groups. It has been reported that salt‐loading decreased plasma renin activity in WKY, but increased it in SHRSP. This study examined the involvement of the renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system (RAAS) for the 11 β‐HSD2 regulation in order to clarify the abnormality of low protein exposure SHRSP. The animals used were 15 week‐old male SHRSP. The drinking water was tap water or 1% saline. The each drinking groups were divided into three groups: control group, captopril (40 mg kg−1 day−1) of angiotensin I converting enzyme inhibitor group, and hydralazine chloride (15 mg kg−1 day−1) of vasodilator group. The diet was a commercial diet (Funahasi SP), and the drugs were mixed with a Funahasi SP diet. After 2 weeks of treatment, the blood was sampled under ether anesthesia from the abdomen aorta. After perfusion by physiological saline, the kidneys were removed. The serum and tissue ACE activity was determined using Hip‐His‐Leu as the substrate, and measured produced hipuric acid in the HPLC. In the microsomal fraction of the kidney, the 11 β‐HSD2 activity was determined by measuring the conversion of corticosterone to 11 dehydrocorticosterone in the presence of NAD. The samples were frozen and stored at −80°C. In a tap of water intake experiment, the captopril and hydralazine group had significantly reduced systolic blood pressure compared to the control group. The serum ACE activity of the captopril group was markedly higher than the control group. The ACE activity in renal tissue was significantly decreased in the captopril group compared with the control group. There was no difference between the hydralazine and control group in the serum and renal ACE activity. The captopril group significantly increased 11 β‐HSD2 activity compared with the control group. No effects were seen in the hydralazine group on the 11 β‐HSD2 activity. In a 1% saline intake experiment, however, the blood pressure of the hydralazine group was lower than the control, but the captopril group were similar to the control. The serum ACE activity of the captopril group combined with 1% saline was higher than the control group, so the result was similar to the single captopril group. However, the renal ACE activity of the salt loaded groups were not different from the captopril group to the control. There was no serum and renal ACE activity changed in the hydralazine group with 1% saline. There was no difference in 11 β‐HSD2 among these three groups. We have demonstrated that tissue RAAS contributes to the regulation of renal 11 β‐HSD2, but not circulation RAAS. It is proposed that renal 11 β‐HSD2 may play a role in accelerating blood vessel injury in salt‐loaded SHRSP. Further studies are needed to confirm the abnormality of the renal 11 β‐HSD2 regulation for the shortening in the life of low protein exposed SHRSP in the salt loading. Soy Isoflavones Inhibit NADPH Oxidase Expression and Superoxide Generation in Cultured Aortic Endothelial Cells from SHRSP.Jin‐Wen Xu,1Katsumi Ikeda,2Takanori Teramoto,3and Yukio Yamori.11WHO Collaborating Center for Research on Primary Prevention of Cardiovascular Diseases, Kyoto, 606‐8413, Japan. 2The School of Human Environmental Science, MUKOGAWA Women's University, Nishinomiya, 606‐0805, Japan. 3Fujicco Co., Ltd., Kobe, 650‐8558, Japan. Angiotensin II‐induced reactive oxygen species through NADPH oxidase in cardiovascular cells are one major cause of hypertension and atheroclerosis. Increased expression of NADPH oxidase is involved in the spontaneously hypertensive rats (SHR). On the other hand, experimental studies have generally shown that isoflavones play an important role in prevention of cardiovascular disease. However, their mechanisms are not completely understood. Here, we provide evidence that soy isoflavones inhibit NADPH oxidase expression and superoxide anion generation in vascular endothelial cells from the stroke prone spontaneously hypertensive rat (SHRSP). Vascular endothelial cells were isolated from rat aorta from the SHRSP of male and 16 weeks of age. Endothelial cells were subculture for 5 to 7 passages and characterized with Dil‐Ac‐LDL fluorescence. Western blotting for NADPH oxidase subunits gp91‐phox, p22‐phox was carried out using antibodies against gp91‐phox and p22‐phox. Nitroblue tetrazolium (NBT) reduction was quantified by measuring the absorbance at 510 nm (extinction coefficient of 11,000 M−1 cm−1). Nitrotyrosin was measured using ELISA as the protocol of manufacture. Genistein and daidzein at 50 µmol L−1 decreased in expression of the NADPH oxidase subunit gp91‐phox protein level (70% of control, n = 3, each) after 24 h exposure, and inhibited p22‐phox expression (60% of control, n = 3, each). Moreover, genistein and daidzein inhibited angiotensin II‐induced superoxide anion generation (NBT reduction, p < 0.05, n = 3, each) similar to effect of diphenylene iodonium (DPI, 10 µmol L−1), an inhibitor of NADPH oxidase. Pretreatment with isoflavones for 24 hours or with antioxidants (DPI or vitamin E) for 1 hour decreased N‐nitrotyrosin formation in angiotensin II‐stimulated SHRSP endothelial cells (n = 6, p < 0.05, each). In addition, isoflavones (genistein and daidzein) also reduced endothelin‐1 release in cultured aortic endothelial cells from SHRSP (p < 0.05 or p < 0.01, n = 3 each). Our studies provide the evidence that isoflavones inhibit NADPH oxidase expression in cultured aortic endothelial cells. Thus isoflavones can act as antioxidants at gene transcription level, which are highly suggestive of beneficial effect on primary prevention of cardiovascular diseases. Alteration of Oxidant Stress and Nitric Oxide Synthase Expression with Aging and Hypertension in Rats: Prevention by Angiotensin II Antagonism.Atsuko Niwa,1Hideaki Higashino,1Kana Maeda‐Ooshima,1Masaki Tabuchi,1Saori Suzuki,2and Kaoru Nishiyama.21Department of Pharmacology, Kinki University School of Medicine, Osaka‐Sayama 589‐8511, Japan. 2Faculty of Health Science, School of Medicine, Kobe University, Kobe 654‐0142, Japan. It has been postulated that a decreased synthesis or an increased degradation of nitric oxide (NO) is the mechanism responsible for cardiovascular dysfunction in hypertension or atherosclerosis. Recent large clinical trials suggest that renin–angiotensin system especially in vessel wall is associated with vascular oxidative stress, smooth muscle cell proliferation and leukocyte infiltration. The present study has investigated whether an anti‐hypertensive drug, angiotensin‐converting enzyme inhibitor captopril, can alter the NO/O2‐balance with hypertension and aging, thereby improving vascular function. Captopril or a vasodilator hydralazine was received in the diet for 8 weeks (12–20 weeks of age) in stroke‐prone spontaneously hypertensive rat (SHRSP) and normotensive Wistar–Kyoto (WKY) strain. Then both captopril and hydralazine decreased blood pressure to the same level (250 mmHg before/180 mmHg after) in SHRSP. NO2−/NO3− (NOx) concentration of serum, determined by the method of ozone chemiluminescence, increased with age in untreated SHRSP, but not in WKY. NOx concentration showed 2.5‐fold higher value in SHRSP at 16 weeks old when compared with that in WKY. Extensive immunostaining intensities of inducible NO synthase (iNOS) and neuronal NOS (nNOS) were observed in the endothelial and vascular smooth muscle cells of aortas in untreated aged SHRSP. An indicator of tissue damage, peroxynitrite, was also detected in the endothelial and vascular smooth muscle cells of SHRSP. On the other hand, endothelial NOS (eNOS) protein content and eNOS mRNA expression were quantitatively unchanged among age groups and between SHRSP and WKY. Chronic captopril administration normalized the over‐expression of iNOS and nNOS, but not hydralazine. Further captopril suppressed the increased NOx production and the vascular injury in SHRSP. Basal O2− level, measured with lucigenin‐enhanced chemiluminescence, showed 6‐fold intensity in aortas from untreated aged SHRSP when compared with age‐matched WKY. The number of circulating activated monocytes increased in SHRSP with age, but not neutrophils. An increased degradation of NO by O2− may be responsible for a defective NO availability in the vessels of SHRSP. These alterations were also significantly reduced after the administration of captopril and hydralazine in SHRSP. The present findings suggest that the over‐expression of iNOS and nNOS, and the excess production of O2− are involved in vascular dysfunction and the development of hypertension. These mechanisms are partly mediated by pressure‐independent angiotensin II pathways. Our previous study has clarified the increase of angiotensin II receptors in the resistance vessels of SHRSP. The pathogenesis of hypertension or atherosclerosis may be altered for the better by local angiotensin II antagonism. Changes of Blood Nitric Oxide Metabolite Concentration Induced by Endothelial Stimulation in Stroke‐Prone Spontaneously Hypotensive Rats.Masaki Tabuchi, Kata Ohshima, Atsuko Niwa, and Hideaki Higashino. Department of Pharmacology, Kinki University School of Medicine, Osaka‐Sayama 589‐8511, Japan. In previous studies using the extracted vessels of stroke‐prone spontaneously hypotensive rats (SHRSP), endothelium‐dependent relaxation (EDR) is reported to be attenuated due to continuous hypertension. In our previous report, it had been shown that the concentration of plasma nitric oxide metabolites (NOx), metabolic products of nitric oxide (NO) that was a body of EDR, increased as blood pressures rose. In the present study, blood pressure, heart rate, and NOx in plasma and red blood cell (RBC) after intravenous injection of acetylcholine were measured in order to investigate the difference of vessel relaxing response, and of NO production by endothelial NO synthase (eNOS), between Wistar‐Kyoto rats (WKY) and SHRSP. WKY and SHRSP at age of 16–20 weeks were used. Under anesthesia with pentobarbital, a cannula connecting to a pressure transducer was inserted into the carotid artery, and changes of blood pressure were recorded after 10−7–10−5 g kg−1 of acetycholine was injected to the femoral vein. Blood samples were collected from the carotid artery before, and 30 sec, 2 min and 4 min after injection of acetylcholine. After centrifuging the collected blood sample, deproteinization of plasma was performed using an ultrafilter, and plasma NOx concentration was measured using the ozone chemiluminesence method. For the measurement of amount of NOx in RBC, the fractions of RBC were laked in ice‐cold tris buffer. The amount of NOx in the laked blood was measured using the above method, and the amount of NOx per gram hemoglobin was obtained. There observed no difference in the decrease rate of blood pressure or heart rate after 10−7–10−5 g kg−1 of acetylcholine injection between WKY and SHRSP. A significant change of plasma NOx concentration was not observed in either WKY or SHRSP after 10−7–10−5 g kg−1 of acetylcholine was injected. The amount of NOx in RBC tended to rise in SHRSP compared to that in WKY after 10−5 g kg−1 acetylcholine was injected. There observed no difference of vessel relaxing response or of plasma NOx concentration changes induced by acetylcholine stimulation in vivo between WKY and SHRSP. Therefore, there might be no difference of NO production by eNOS in the vascular endothelial cell between these rats. However, there was a possibility that the ability of RBC to capture NO in SHRSP differed from that in WKY. The further studies should be performed to investigate the relation between the amount of NOx in RBC and hypertensive diseases. Effects of Pitavastatin on Deposition of Lipids in Aorta of Spontaneously Hypertensive Hyperlipidemic Rats (SHHR).Toshio Kumai,1Sigeko Oonuma,2Mamoru Tadokoro,2and Shinichi Kobayashi.11Department of Pharmacology and 2Department of Pathology, St. Marianna University School of Medicine, 2‐16‐2 Sugao, Miyamae‐ku Kawasaki 216‐8511, Japan. In humans, hypertension and hyperlipidemia are frequent complications of cardiovascular disease. For an animal model of hypertension, spontaneously hypertensive rats (SHR) are widely used. For an animal model of hyperlipidemia, Watanabe heritable hyperlipidemic (WHHL) rabbits are widely used. We previously reported that spontaneously hyperlipidemic rats (HLR), which originated from Sprague‐Dawley rats (SD), show a higher plasma cholesterol level. To examine the role of hypertension and hyperlipidemia in cardiovascular disease, we crossbred SHR and HLR, spontaneously hypertensive hyperlipidemic rats (SHHR). In the present study, we evaluate the effect of new statin, pitavastatin (NK‐104, Kowa Kagaku Co., Japan) on cardiovascular system of SHHR. Male SHHR and SD rats (4 months old) were used. N‐G‐nitro‐L‐arginine methyl ester (L‐NAME, 100 mg L−1, in drinking water) was administered for 1 month from 4 months of age. After administered of L‐NAME, high fat diet (2% cholesterol, 1% cholic acid, 5% coconut oil, CLEA, Tokyo) was administered for 2 months. Plasma cholesterol level was determined using cholesterol oxidase method. Histological studies of aorta were analyzed using hematoxylin and eosin (HE) stain and sudan III stain. Systolic blood pressure of control SHHR group was significantly higher than that of control SD. Plasma cholesterol level of control SHHR was significantly higher than that of control SD. Plasma cholesterol level of L‐NAME + high fat diet‐treated SHHR group was significantly higher than that of control SHHR. Plasma cholesterol level of L‐NAME + high fat diet + pitavastatin‐treated SHHR group was not significantly different from that of control SHHR. The low density lipoprotein of L‐NAME + high fat diet‐treated SHHR group was significantly increased than that of control SHHR. The low density lipoprotein of L‐NAME + high fat diet + pitavastatin‐treated SHHR group was significantly lower than that of L‐NAME + high fat diet‐treated SHHR group. The lipid deposition under endothelial cells and endothelium injury were shown in the aorta of L‐NAME + high fat diet‐treated SHHR group. The lipid deposition under endothelial cells and endothelium injury were not shown in the aorta of L‐NAME + high fat diet + pitavastatin‐treated SHHR group. These results suggested that pitavastatin might suppress the lipid deposition under endothelial cells and endothelium injury in aorta of SHHR by L‐NAME and high fat diet treatment without changes in plasma cholesterol level. Amino Acid Substitutions in Phospholipase C‐δ1 of Spontaneously Hypertensive Rats: Implication in Cell Signaling.Hitoshi Yagisawa,1Masashi Okada,1and Hiroshi Nojima.21Department of Life Science, Graduate School of Science, Himeji Institute of Technology, Harima Science Garden City, Hyogo, 678‐1297, Japan. 2Department of Molecular Genetics Research Institute for Microbial Diseases, Osaka University, 3‐1 Yamadaoka, Suita, Osaka, 565‐0871, Japan. We have previously reported base replacements in phospholipase C (PLD)‐δ1 from spontaneously hypertensive rats (SHR), a model animal for human essential hypertension, resulting in amino acid substitutions: I412 by M and T423 by S in the C‐terminal end of the X domain that composes the catalytic domain of the enzyme (Yagisawa H., et al. J Hypertens 1991; 9:997–1004). Although many reports have demonstrated accelerated proliferation and increased [Ca2+]i levels in SHR‐derived cells (Marche P., et al. Clin Exp Pharmacol Physiol 1995; 22:S114–S116; Marche P., et al. Int J Cardiol 1997; 62:S17–S22), the biochemical properties of recombinant enzymes derived from SHR and from the normotensive counterpart, Wistar‐Kyoto (WKY) rats are essentially the same (Yagisawa H., et al. unpublished work). Although PLC‐δ1 is normally localized at the plasma membrane and in the cytoplasm, it shuttles between the nucleus and the cytoplasm and contains a functional nuclear export signal (NES) (Yamaga M., et al. J Biol Chem 1999; 274:28537–28541). Since nucleocytoplasmic shuttling of a molecule is generally regulated by a balance between its NES and nuclear localization signal (NLS), we examined whether PLC‐δ1 contains an NLS sequence. A region corresponding to the C terminus of the X domain and following the XY‐linker, which contains clusters of basic amino acid residues, was essential for the nuclear import of the molecule in Madin‐Darby canine kidney (MDCK) cells. A series of point mutations on lysine residues in this region revealed that K432 and K434 in combination were important for the nuclear import (Okada M., et al. Genes to Cells 2002; 7:985–996). Interestingly, from a 3D‐structural point of view, K432 and K434 are distant from the catalytic pocket but very close to I412 and T423. In the presence of leptomycin B, an inhibitor of the nuclear export, MDCK cells expressing green fluorescent protein (GFP)‐fused SHR‐type PLC‐δ1 show faster nuclear accumulation of the GFP fluorescence than cells expressing GFP‐WKY‐type PLC‐δ1. It seems likely that interaction with the nuclear import machinery is different between the SHR‐type PLC‐δ1 and the WKY‐type PLC‐δ1. The function of PLC‐δ1 in the nucleus has not been clarified yet. It could produce second messengers such as inositol (1,4,5)‐trisphosphate and diacylglycerol in the nucleus as it does at the plasma membrane. Many new roles in the nucleus for phosphoinositides, inositol phosphates and enzymes that regulate their levels have recently been postulated (Irvin R. Science's stke 2002; 150/re13:1–16). Alterations in the nucleocytoplasmic shuttling of the SHR‐type enzyme resulting from the conformational changes due to the amino acid substitutions, therefore, may cause changes in cell growth and other physiological properties that are characteristic of SHR. Supported by Mochida Foundation, Japan Heart Foundation, Ono Medical Research Foundation, Takeda Science Foundation, Inamori Foundation, Novartis Foundation (Japan) and Grant‐in‐Aids from the Ministry of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology of Japan. Effects of DNA‐RNA Chimeric Ribozyme Expression of TGF‐β in Gingiva from Spontaneously Hypertensive Rats.Soh Sato,1Junko Yusa,1Noboru Fukuda,3Hiromi Mitsubayashi,2Hiroshi Kawamura,2and Kyuichi Kamoi.11Department of Periodontology, School of Dentistry, The Nippon Dental University. 2Department of Internal Medicine and 3Department of Internal Medicine II, Nihon University School of Medicine. A number of investigators have reported increasing expression of TGF‐β1 in various tissues of spontaneously hypertensive rats (SHRs). We previously reported that expression of TGF‐β protein is increased in gingiva from SHRs, and additionally demonstrated that administration of nifedipine (a calcium antagonist) stimulated gingival growth with increases in TGF‐β. These previous findings suggest involvement of TGF‐β1 in the drug‐induced gingival hyperplasia in which, the mechanisms have been unclear. Ribozyme is expected to be utilized as a new nucleic‐acid‐based drug that as a gene therapy. Ribozyme can enzymatically cleave target mRNA to suppress the gene expression. The present study was undertaken to explore the possibility of suppress on of gingival growth by ribozyme targeting TGF‐β1. In addition, SHRs were transfected ribozyme and the expression of TGF‐β1 in the gingival tissue of the rats was examined in vivo. Synthesis of DNA‐RNA chimeric ribozyme targeting TGF‐β1. Hammerhead ribozyme and mismatch ribozyme, complementary sequence to the loop structure containing the GUC sequence of rat TGF‐β1 mRNA, were designed. DNA‐RNA chimeric hammerhead ribozyme, whose active site was RNA, while other sites were DNA, was used for the experiments. Effects of ribozyme to TGF‐β1 on human gingival fibroblast growth. Human gingival fibroblasts in culture were stimulated with angiotensin II (Ang II) and PMA phorbol 12‐myristat 13‐acetate (PMA). FITC‐labeled ribozyme was transfected by HVJ‐envelope in fibroblast. Effects of ribozyme on expression or TGF‐β1 1mRNA and protein were evaluated by RT‐PCR, and Western blot analysis, respectively. Distribution of ribozyme to TGF‐β1 in gingival in rats in vivo. FITC‐labeled ribozyme was transfected with HVJ‐envelope into gingival crevice in 8‐week‐old Wistar‐Kyoto Rats at a dose level of 10 µg. The gingival tissue was removed at 0, 1, 3, 6, 12, and 24 hours after the injection, and distribution was evaluated histopathologically. Effects of ribozyme to TGF‐β1 on expression of TGF‐β1 protein in SHR/Izm gingival in vivo. Eight‐week‐old SHR/Izm were divided into three groups (control, ribozyme and mismatch ribozyme groups). Ribozyme or mismatch ribozyme (10 µg) with HVJ‐envelope was injected into the gingival crevice. Twenty‐four hours later, animals were sacrificed, and their maxillary gingival was sampled to evaluate effects of ribozyme to TGF‐β1 on expression of TGF‐β1 protein evaluated by western blotting. Ribozyme to TGF‐β1 significantly inhibited expressions of TGF‐β1 mRNA and protein in human gingival fibroblasts stimulated with Ang II or PMA. Mismatch ribozyme did not affect expression of TGF‐β1 mRNA and protein. Ribozyme was distributed in gingiya 0–6 hours after the injection. Six to twelve hours after the injection, the drug was found to be wide distributing in the gingival connective tissue. Twenty‐four hours after the injection, ribozyme disappeared in the gingival connective tissue. Ribozyme to TGF‐β1 significantly inhibited expression of rat TGF‐β1 protein in SHR. Mismatch ribozyme did not affect expression of TGF‐β1 protein in gingival in vivo. The DNA‐RNA chimeric ribozyme targeting TGF‐β1 specifically inhibit increased in TGF‐β1 expression in cultured gingival fibroblasts in vitro and gingival from in vivo. These findings suggest that the ribozyme to TGF‐β1 will be possible gene therapy agent for the SHR. Studies for Osteonecrosis of Femoral Head in Stroke‐Prone Spontaneously Hypertensive Rats with Hyperlipemia.Kenji Kumagai, Masahiko Suzuki, Masahiro Wada, Masakazu Murata, Masami Niwa, and Hiroyuki Shindo. Departments of Orthopedic Surgery and Pharmacology, Nagasaki University School of Medicine, 1‐7‐1 Sakamoto, Nagasaki 852‐8501, Japan. Stroke‐prone spontaneously hypertensive rats (SHRSP) are thought to be an animal model of osteonecrosis of the femoral head (ONFH). Studies for human ONFH and animal models have indicated that steroid hormone‐induced hyperlipemia causing swollen adipocytes and endothelium dysfunctions is related to the etiology of this necrosis. Human ONFH, complicated by a high dose of steroid hormone‐therapy, is a major side effect of organ transplantation. Our previous studies revealed that ONFH was observed in SHRSP at 15–20 weeks of age, and chronic ingestion of an NOS inhibitor inhibited the onset of the disease. To clarify the participation of hyperlipemia as an etiological factor in the disease, we examined the onset of ONFH in a new animal model of SHRSP fed a high fat and high cholesterol diet (HFC) containing N‐G‐nitro‐L‐arginine (L‐NNA), as an NOS inhibitor. Stroke‐prone spontaneously hypertensive rats with an administration of steroid hormone was used for the purpose of comparison. Male SHRSP/Nagasaki, maintained and bred at the Laboratory Animal Center for Biomedical Research, Nagasaki University School of Medicine were used. At 15 weeks of age, rats were put at random into groups of 30, 25, and 24. The first group of 30 rats were fed the HFC diet containing L‐NNA in a dose of 0.006% (HFC‐NNA group). The second group of 25 was fed the SP diet (Funabashi Farm Co., Chiba, Japan), and at 17 weeks of age, 5 or 10 mg of methylpredonisolone was subcutaneously injected into the second group of rats (Steroid group). The third group of 24 rats, given the SP diet with no drug, served as controls. The rats were decapitated at 19 weeks of age. The total level of cholesterol of the HFC‐NNA group was four‐times higher than those of the control group. Group (nos.) ONFH Body weight TP TC TG HFC‐NNA (30) 41.7% 234 4.9 211 61 Steroid group (25) 50.0% 228 5.2 84 94 Control (24) 38.5% 282 5.5 53 72 Note: TP, serum total protein (g dL−1); TC, total cholesterol (mg dL−1); TG, triglyceride (mg dL−1). The incidence of ONFH in the Steroid group was 50%, but it was lower in the HFC‐NNA group and the control SHRSP. Numerous adipocytes in the metaphysical bone marrow of the HFC‐NNA group and the Steroid group was observed microscopically. In the Steroid group, we noted the epiphysial bone marrow was filled with massive swollen adipocytes. In addition, there was a migration of swollen fatty cells into the necrotic area in the epiphysial bone marrow of the Steroid group. Ninety‐four percent of this group also demonstrated osteonecrosis and/or swollen adipocytes. Epidemiological investigations for human ONFH has suggested that steroid hormone and alcohol intake are etiological factors related to the avascular necrosis of femoral head. We observed here the highest incidence of ONFH in the Steroid group. A hyperlipemia‐induced increase in the number and invasion to necrotic areas of adipocytes seems to be an etiological factor, however, a lower incidence was observed in the HFC‐NNA group with hypercholesterolemia. We admit that other mechanisms may be operative in the experimental model of femoral head avascular necrosis. Pathogenesis of Cataract in SHR.Tohru Katsube,1Ryoichi Horie,2Akihiro Ohira,1and Takayuki Harada.21Department of Ophthalmology and 2Pathology, Shimane Medical University, Izumo, Japan. Senile cataract affinity rats (SCAR) have been established by sib mating of A3 107 line in which hereditary cataract develops in 100% of rats without other malformation after birth (Horie, 2000). We previously reported that ligation of the bilateral common carotid arteries at a 1‐week interval caused cataract on the side opposite to the side ligated first in SHR. In this study, to elucidate the pathogenesis of cataract in SCAR, we performed the bilateral common carotid artery ligation experiment (at a 1‐week‐interval) described above and investigated lens opacity and funduscopic findings in SHR and its substrain based on the diameter of the retinal arterioles, the only vascular system in the body that can be observed non‐invasively. One animal each of 4‐month‐old WKY/Izm and SHR/Izm maintained at the Pathology Laboratory of Shimane Medical University were anesthetized by intraperitoneal injection of 30 mg kg−1 pentobarbital. First, the left common carotid artery was double‐ligated at a site 1 cm proximal from the branching point with N0.3 silk thread. After 1 week, the right common carotid artery was ligated by the same procedure. Blood pressure (BP) and body weight (BW) were measured before and after surgery and fundus photographs were taken. Thirty‐five millimeter slides of the fundus photographs were prepared and input in a computer using a film scanner, and magnified to 1220 × 840 dots on a CRT screen. The diameters of 4–6 arterioles located at two‐fold of the optic disc diameter from the center of the optic disc were measured. After ligation of the left common carotid artery, the arteriolar diameter increased in the bilateral retinal arterioles in WKY. In contrast in SHR, the right retinal arterioles were slightly constricted while the left retinal arterioles were also slightly dilated. After ligation of the right common carotid artery, the right retinal arterioles were gradually constricted and the left retinal arterioles were significantly dilated in WKY (P < 0.05). In contrast, in SHR, the retinal arterioles were significantly dilated on right side (P < 0.05), while those on the left side did not alter as compared to those before the experiment. After ligation of the bilateral common carotid arteries, BP slightly increased in WKY, but rapidly and markedly increased in SHR. Many reports have found that when both common carotid arteries were simultaneously ligated, all WKY survived, but both SHR and stroke‐prone SHR (SHRSP) died within 2– 48 hours in all cases. However, the reason has not been completely clarified. When the left and right common carotid arteries were ligated at a 1‐week interval, SHR survived inspite of the development of hypertension as severe as that in SHRSP (Horie, 1986). The different changes in the arteriolar diameters in WKY and SHR after ligation of the bilateral common carotid arteries at a 1‐week interval observed in this study are considered as follows. Ligation of the bilateral common carotid arteries increased blood flow from the vertebrobasilar arterial system and others into the brain. After ligation of the right common carotid artery performed one week later, cerebral blood flow was markedly decreased (Kagawa et al., 1991). The ophthalmic artery is the first branch of the internal carotid artery after it penetrates the duramater of the brain. Blood flow in the ophthalmic artery may be markedly decreased as in the cerebral arterioles in severe hypertension, and the retinal arterioles and ciliary body arterioles, which are two branches of the ophthalmic artery, may be in an ischemic state. Effects of Blood Pressure on Green and Black Tea Polyphenols in Stroke‐Prone Spontaneously Hypertensive Rats (SHRSP).Hiroko Negishi,1Katsumi Ikeda,2Marina Njelekela,3and Yukio Yamori.11WHO Collaborating Center for Research on Primary Prevention of Cardiovascular Diseases, Kyoto 606‐8413, Japan. 2School of Human Environmental Sciences, Mukogawa Women's University, Nishinomiya 663‐8179, Japan. 3Graduate School of Human and Environmental Studies, Kyoto University, Kyoto 606‐8501, Japan. Oxidative stress has been reported to be involved in not only cardiovascular diseases, but also hypertension, the most prevalent risk of cardiovascular diseases. It is suggested that tea drinking, five or more cups of tea/day, reduced blood pressure (BP) slightly in epidemiological studies. Some recent studies showed that a cup of tea had a greater antioxidant activity than one serving various fruit and vegetables in vitro. To determine whether black and green tea can lower BP in SHRSP. Male SHRSP were allowed to recover for 2 weeks after a transmitter for measuring BP was implanted in the peritoneal cavity of each rat. Fifteen SHRSP were divided into three groups: Control group given tap water, Black tea polyphenol group (BTP) given drinking water (containing 70% thearubigins, 12% theaflavins, 10% flavonols and 8% catechins), and Green tea polyphenol group (TGP) (70% catechins, 10% flavonols and 20% polymetric flavonoids). The telemetry system (Data Science International) was used to measure BP and heart rate, which were recorded continuously every 5 min for 24 hours. Urinary total protein, albumin, creatinine, nitrate/nitrite, and plasma nitrate/nitrite were measured. Plasma polyphenols were analyzed by a HPLC method. Enzyme protein expressions of catalase, myosin light chain phosphorylation (MLC‐p) and NADPH‐oxidase subunits p22phox, p67phox were measured with Western blotting. Both systolic and diastolic BP, especially during daytime, were significantly lower in BTP and GTP groups than in the control. Black tea polyphenol group and GTP significantly increased the catalase expression, and decreased the MLC‐p expression in aorta. Both black and green tea polyphenols attenuated BP increase through its antioxidant properties in SHRSP. Since the amount of polyphenols used in this experiment corresponds to those in five cups of tea, the regular consumption of black and green tea may have some protection against hypertension in humans. Regulation of NADPH Oxidase Subunits p22phox, p67phox, and Catalase by Tea Polyphenols in Cultured Bovine Endothelial Cells.Chen‐Jiang Ying,1,2Jin‐Wen Xu,3Kazumi Ikeda,1,3and Yukio Yamori.1,31The School of Human Environmental Science, Mukogawa Women's University, Nishinomiya, Japan. 2Tongji Medical College of HuaZhong University of Science and Technology, Wuhan, China. 3WHO Collaborating Center for Research on Primary Prevention of Cardiovascular Diseases, Kyoto, Japan. Enhanced reactive oxygen species (ROS) is one of key events in the pathogenesis of endothelial dysfunction in hypertension and other cardiovascular diseases. Tea polyphenols has been proved to prevent stroke in stroke‐prone spontaneously hypertensive rats. However, the underlying mechanisms of the preventive effect have not been well understood. The purpose of this work is to observe the tea polyphenol effects on ROS formation, degradation and cellular functions in vascular endothelial cells. Bovine carotid artery endothelial cells (BCAECs) were maintained at 37°C in 5% CO2 in DMEM containing 10% FBS. Enzyme protein expressions of catalase, Cu–Zn SOD, and NADPH oxidase subunits p22phox, p67phox were measured with western blotting in green or black tea polyphenol (TGP or BTP) treated cells. In serum free DMEM, the angiotensin II (Ang II) induced cell permeability was measured by transwell filters assay with 40 kDa FITC‐dextran, and Ang II induced superoxide anion was determined by nitroblue tetrazolium (NBT) assay after GTP or BTP pretreated for 24 h. Also in serum free DMEM, cell growth curves were drawn according to cell numbers in day 2, 4, and 6 that were counted by hemacytometer, and cell migration was determined by "scratch" wound assay in Ang II, angiotensin II plus green tea extract (Ang II+GTP), and angiotensin II plus black tea extract (Ang II+BTP) treated groups. Treatment with GTP or BTP significantly up‐regulated catalase expression, down‐regulated p22phox expression, and down‐regulated p67phox expression with dose‐dependent tendencies in BCAECs, whereas no obvious effect on Cu–Zn SOD expression was observed. In Ang II challenged BCAECs, the superoxide anion level was significantly elevated (P < 0.05), but no significant difference was observed in either GTP or BTP pretreated cells compared with control (P < 0.05). Pretreated with GTP or BTP also lowered the Ang II‐induced permeable fluorescence intensities (18928.3 ± 438.7, 15918.3 ± 820.7, 10599.3 ± 1039.6 FU, respectively in Ang II, Ang II+GTP, and Ang II+BTP pretreated cells vs. 14577.3 ± 1272.8 FU in the control) (P < 0.05). Both green and black tea polyphenols significantly reversed the cell growth stimulated by Ang II (132.4, 73.6, and 67.9% in Ang II, Ang II+GTP, and Ang II+BTP respectively vs. 100% in the control) (P < 0.05), and migration distances stimulated by Ang II for 48 h (672 ± 36, 444 ± 18, 360 ± 19 µm, respectively in AngII, Ang II+GTP, and Ang II+BTP vs. 561 ± 5 µm in the control) (P < 0.05) and 72 h (783 ± 34, 501 ± 19, 467 ± 29 µm, respectively in Ang II, Ang II+GTP, and Ang II+BTP vs. 729 ± 37 µm in the control) (P < 0.05). Our results showed that Green and black tea polyphenols can modulate Ang II‐related endothelial cell functions such as growth, migration and permeability via up‐regulating catalase expression and down‐regulating NADPH oxidase expression, thus, decreasing the ROS levels. It is suggested that tea polyphenols might beneficially improve the endothelial cell dysfunction and the development of cardiovascular diseases including hypertension. Key Words: ROS; Catalase; NADPH oxidase; Endothelial cell; Angiotensin II This study was supported in part by the Japan‐China Sasakawa Medical Fellowship. Effect of Dietary Phytosterol on Systolic Blood Pressure and Life Span of Salt‐Loaded Stroke‐Prone Spontaneously Hypertensive Rats.Hiroshi Ogawa. Department of Hygiene, Kinki University School of Medicine, Osaka‐Sayama, Osaka 589‐8511, Japan. Recently, several reports demonstrated that salt‐loaded stroke‐prone spontaneously hypertensive rats (SHRSP) exhibited different lifespans depending on the type of dietary vegetable oil. Subsequently, Ratnayake et al. (J Nutr 2000; 130:1166) reported that phytosterol content in dietary vegetable oil might be closely related to the survival time. On the other hand, it is generally known that intestinal absorption of phytosterol is very poor and phytosterol lowers serum cholesterol level by inhibiting intestinal cholesterol absorption in humans. Therefore, in the previous paper (Ogawa & Kamisako. Clin Exp Hypertens 2002; 24:451), we examined effect of dietary phytosterol on the survival time, and reported that phytosterol additives to soybean oil accelerated elevation of systolic blood pressure and stroke onset in dose‐dependent manner, which shortened the life span of salt‐loaded SHRSP. In the present study, by choosing perilla rich in n‐3 polyunsaturated fatty acids instead of soybean oil, I investigated effect of phytosterol additives to perilla oil (PO) on systolic blood pressure, stroke onset and lifespan of salt‐loaded SHRSP. Male SHRSP were used at the age of 6 weeks and divided into four groups. Perilla oil group and canola oil (CA) group were given a semi‐purified diet according to the AIN‐93 formula (casein content: 20%) containing PO and CA as an oil source (10%), respectively. The other two groups were given PO diet containing 0.06% phytosterol (PO+0.06P, total phytosterol content is as same as that of CA) or 0.45% phytosterol (PO+0.45P) by weight. To accelerate the development of hypertension, 3% NaCl was added to all diets instead of 1% NaCl solution. Systolic blood pressure was measured by tail‐pulse pickup method (BP‐98A, Softron Inc., Tokyo) after 5‐week feeding. The onset of stroke was judged by neurological symptoms and marked decreases in body weight and food intake. There were no significant differences in the growth rate and food intake among the four groups before stroke onset. Systolic blood pressure of the PO group was significantly lower than that of the CA group. In contrast, those of the PO+0.06P and PO+0.45P groups increased dose‐dependently and were significantly higher than that of the PO group. In addition, that of the PO+0.45P group was significantly higher than that of the CA group. These results suggested that addition of phytosterol to PO elevated systolic blood pressure dose‐dependently, although PO significantly reduced systolic blood pressure compared with CA, in salt‐loaded SHRSP. Stroke onset was significantly shortened in the CA and PO+0.45P groups, and tended to be shortened in the PO+0.06P group, compared with PO group. Survival period after stroke onset was also significantly shortened in the CA and PO+0.45P: groups, and tended to be shortened in the PO+0.06P group, compared with the PO group. Consequently, survival rates of the CA and PO+0.45P groups were significantly lower than that of the PO group. These results suggested that phytosterol additives to PO promoted stroke onset in a dose‐dependent manner, resulting in shortened lifespan of salt‐loaded SHRSP. In conclusion, we have demonstrated that phytosterol additives to PO induce elevation of systolic blood pressure and promote stroke onset in a dose‐dependent manner, which may cause reduced survival time in salt‐loaded SHRSP. However, it remains unclear whether these unfavorable effects of phytosterol are specific for SHRSP strain or applicable to humans. Strain‐Related Differences in Lipid Metabolism Among Stroke‐Prone Spontaneously Hypertensive Rats, Wistar‐Kyoto Rats, and Wistar Rats.Hiroshi Ogawa and Toshinori Kamisako. Department of Hygiene, Kinki University School of Medicine, Osaka‐Sayama, Osaka 589‐8511, Japan. It is well known that stroke‐prone spontaneously hypertensive rats (SHRSP) exhibit significantly low serum cholesterol levels and high serum triglyceride levels compared with normotensive Wistar‐Kyoto rats (WKY) when both are fed a regular rat chow. Moreover, in the previous paper (Ogawa & Kamisako. Clin Exp Hyperens 2002; 24:451), we reported that phytosterol additives to soybean oil shortened the life span of salt‐loaded SHRSP. However, it remains unclear whether the unfavorable effect of phytosterol is specific for WKY strains including SHRSP or common to all other rat strains. These studies indicate that there may be some strain‐related differences in lipid metabolism. It is therefore important to conduct extensive searches of differences in lipid metabolism among various rat strains, at least one more strain in addition to WKY and SHRSP. In the present paper, we examined strain‐related differences in lipid metabolism by utilizing SHRSP, WKY, and Wistar rats. Male SHRSP (SHRSP/Izm), their normotensive WKY (WKY/Izm) and Wistar rats (Crj/Wistar, Charles River Japan) were used at the age of 5 weeks. Rats were fed a semi‐purified diet prepared according to the AIN‐93 formula (Milk casein content: 20%) for 2 weeks. Blood was obtained from the abdominal aorta under pentobarbital anesthesia (40 mg kg−1) without fasting, and liver was immediately removed and frozen by liquid nitrogen. Serum lipoprotein fractions were separated by stepwise density‐gradient ultracentrifugation. Lipid contents were enzymatically determined by commercial kits. Serum concentrations of apolipoproteins were measured by rocket immunoelectrophoresis. Total RNA was isolated from the liver using TRIzol (Life Tech. Inc., Japan) and hepatic expressions of mRNAs were estimated using RT‐PCR and Printgraph (AE‐6915, Atto Corp.). There were no significant differences in growth rate and food intake among the three groups. Serum total cholesterol in SHRSP was significantly lower than that in WKY, which was associated with a significant increase in the VLDL fraction and significant decreases in the LDL and HDL fractions compared with WKY. No significant differences were observed in cholesterol levels between SHRSP and Wistar rats. On the contrary, serum triglyceride in SHRSP was significantly higher than that in WKY, which was due to a significant increase in the VLDL fraction. In contrast, serum triglyceride in SHRSP tended to be lower than that in Wistar rats, which was due to a significant decrease in the LDL fraction. Serum apoA‐I level in SHRSP was significantly lower than that in Wistar rats and tended to be lower than that in WKY. Serum apoE level in SHRSP was significantly lower than that in WKY and significantly higher than that in Wistar rats. Comparative studies on hepatic mRNA expressions showed that scavenger‐receptor class B type 1 (SRB1, a typical HDL receptor) and hepatic triglyceride lipase (h‐TGL) mRNAs were expressed dominantly in SHRSP compared with the other two strains, while no significant differences were observed in mRNA expressions of microsomal triglyceride transfer protein (MTP) and LDL‐receptor (LDLR) among the three groups. Combined the above results all together, it is suggested that promotion of apoA‐I HDL metabolism through SRB1 and h‐TGL in SHRSP may result in significant reductions of both serum apoA‐I and HDL‐cholesterol when compared with WKY, and that promotion of apoA‐I HDL metabolism and higher contents of apoE‐HDL in SHRSP may result in a significant reduction of serum apoA‐I and a similar serum lipid level when compared with Wistar rats. Stroke Prevention of Sesamin and Sesame in Stroke‐Prone Spontaneously Hypertensive Rats.Yuko Onobayashi,1Takanori Noguchi,2Hiroshi Hara,3Hanzo Kakuta,3Mitsuo Namiki,4Kazuo Yamagata,5Yasuo Nara,5Katsumi Ikeda,6and Yukio Yamori.11WHO Collaborating Center Research for Cardiovascular Diseases, Kyoto, Japan. 2Laboratory for Behavioral Genetics, Brain Science Institute, The Institute of Physical and Chemical Research (RIKEN), Saitama, Japan. 3Fujimi‐Youhou‐En, Saitama, Japan. 4Nagoya University, Nagoya, Japan. 5Graduate School of Integrated Science and Art, University of East Asia, Yamaguchi, Japan. 6Department of Food Science and Nutrition, School of Human Environmental Sciences, Mukogawa Women's University, Nishinomiya, Japan. Previously, we carried out to investigate the antihypertensive and anti‐thrombotic effects of sesamin and vitamin E intake in SHRSP rats. Sesamin produced a marked enhancement of the antihypertensive and anti‐thrombotic activities of vitamin E in SHRSP. Those results indicate that chronic ingestion of vitamin E and sesamin reduced the elevation in blood pressure and thrombotic tendency, suggesting that these treatments might be beneficial for the prevention of hypertension and stroke. It is the most popular situation that we are eating sesame with salt in food culture. In this study, our purpose was to determine whether sesamin, a lignan from sesame oil, and sesame powder could prevent hypertension in salt‐loading SHRSP. At 7 weeks of age, the rats were divided into four groups: (i) a control group; (ii) a sesamin group, 1000 mg sesamin kg−1 diet; (iii) a sesame powder group, 50 g sesame powder kg−1 diet; (iv) a soybean powder group, 50 g soybean powder kg−1 diet. Rats could drink ad libitum from 7 weeks of age to their death. Sesamin was obtained by supercritical fluid extraction in this experiment (Fujimi‐Youhou‐en, Japan). Experiment 1; the rats were given 0.5% NaCl as drinking water. Experiment 2; the rats were given 1.0% NaCl as drinking water. Resting blood pressure was measured using the tail cuff method. Systolic blood pressure, body weight, food intake were measured once weekly. We kept all the rats until natural death and performed autopsy. There was no statistically significant differences in systolic blood pressure, body weight, and food intake among four groups. Water intake in soybean group showed a tendency to increase than the other group, but not significant. In Experiment 1, life span of sesamin group was significantly longer than the control group (sesamin group; 78 ± 17, control group; 62 ± 7 days). In Experiment 2, life span of sesamin group showed a tendency to be longer than control group, but not significant (control group; 39 ± 3, sesamin group; 44 ± 4 days). Body weight, the weight of brain, and kidney was not significantly different (P > 0.1). Heart weight of per 100 g body of control group was significantly larger than sesame powder and sesamine groups (0.77 ± 0.10, 0.66 ± 0.05, 0.65 ± 0.05, respectively). In this study, the elevation of systolic blood pressure was not affected by sesame and soybean, because systolic blood pressure was quickly elevated by salt intake. It was found that extension of life span inhibition of hypertrophy of heart was caused by sesamin intake. Further studies are required to clarify life span extension by sesamin and sesame powder in SHRSP. Anti‐Hypertensive Effects of Isoflavone Aglycones in SHRSP Under Different Food Protein Components.Xingan Song,1,2Katsumi Ikeda,3Weijun Pan,2Minoru Takebe,2and Yukio Yamori.11WHO Collaborating Center for Research on Primary Prevention of Cardiovascular Diseases, Kyoto, Japan. 2Biotics Department, Nichimo Co., Ltd., Tokyo. 3School of Human Environmental Science, Mukogawa Women's University, Nishinomiya, Japan. The main purpose of this study was to assess the effects of isoflavone aglycones (IFA) (product name: AglyMax™, a soybean germ extract containing 30% of isoflavone aglycones by Nichimo Co., Ltd., Japan) on the blood pressure, body weight, and lipid metabolism in stroke‐prone spontaneously hyertensive rats (SHRSP). It has been reported that estrogens have regulation effects on hypertension and lipid metabolism. Isoflavones have similar basic structural features to estrogen, so it was considered that isoflavones could also play similar effects in the blood pressure and body weight control in SHRSP. On the other hand, aglycone type of isoflavone, is proved, can be absorbed in the guts more efficiently than that of the glycoside type. So isoflavone aglycones were used in this study. Thirty‐six male SHRSP rats aged 5 weeks were divided into six groups as (a) animal protein in food; (b) animal protein+IFA; (c) botany protein; (d) botany protein+IFA; (e) half‐and‐half of animal and botany protein; (f) half‐and‐half of animal and botany proteins+IFA. Animals were fed for 6 weeks and the systolic blood pressure, body weight, and nitric oxide (NO2/NO3) levels in urine were determined every 2 weeks during experiment period. Cholesterol in liver and serum, bile acid in feces, and isoflavone level in urine were also analyzed in the end of the experiment. After the administration of IFA, significant decreases in SBP were confirmed in those groups (b, d, f) to which IFA was added in the food compared to their control groups (a, c, e) from the fourth week. At the sixth week, the control group (a) to which animal protein was fed in the food showed highest SBP, and at the same time it's paired IFA group (b) showed most significantly lower SBP compared with (a) because of the administration of IFA. Lowest SBP was observed in the (f) to which was fed with half of animal protein and half of botany protein and IFA. For isoflavone levels in urine of the three IFA groups, the (b) was highest followed by the (d), and the (f) showed the lowest. NO2/NO3 levels in urine and serum were also significantly higher in the IFA groups than that in the control groups respectively. For cholesterol levels, IFA groups showed tendencies of comparatively lower TG and HDL than those in the control groups. Isoflavone aglycones (AglyMax) decreased SBP significantly and had beneficial effects in the metabolism of lipid and body weight control. As a selection, AglyMax is expected to be utilized in prevention of hypertension, obesity, and other diseases related to life style like. Pharmacological Studies on Steryl‐β‐D‐glucosides.Tadatoshi Nomura. Japan Institute of Invention and Innovation, Kyoto 611‐0002, Japan. As one screening test of active principle from various plants the protective effect against pulmonary vascular fragility was assessed in mice. It was found that a fraction extracted from various plants showed an extremely potent effect. The active principle was found to be a mixture of steryl‐β‐D‐glucosides (SGS), composed of β‐sitosteryl‐β‐D‐glucoside (I) mainly, campesteryl‐β‐D‐glucoside (II) and stigmasteryl‐β‐D‐glucoside (III) by gas chromatography. The activities of specially synthesized (I), (II) and (III) were in the following order: (I) > (III) > (II). Therefore, the activity of mixture of SGS isolated from plants appeared to be related to the content of (I) in every mixture. A series of experiments were performed in order to characterize the vascular stabilizing activity and other related properties of SGS isolated from Glycine Max Merill (SG). Effect of SG on pulmonary and cutaneous vascular fragility in mice, rats and guinea pigs, bleeding by cutting the tail end in mice, increased capillary permeability by histamine, hyalunoidase and lecithinase A in rats as well as increased cerebral vascular permeability or edema induced by the bilateral carotid occlusion in SHR were much more potent than those of carbazochrom sodium sulfonate (CSS) and conjugated estrogens. Unlike CSS, SGS had no influence on prothrombin time, plasma recalcification time and euglobulin lysis time. Mechanisms for the specific vascular stabilizing action are now being investigated and the results herein indicate that SGS may be an unique agent for clinical application. A Novel Possibility of Measurement of Oxidative Stress in Spontaneously Hypertension Rat (SHR) and Stroke‐Prone SHR (SHRSP) Brain Using In Vivo Electron Spin Resonance (ESR) Imaging.Hirofumi Shoji,1Hiroyuki Miyazaki,1Kazunori Anzai,2Toshihiko Ozawa,2and Masaichi‐Chang‐il Lee.11Department of Pharmacology and ESR Laboratories, Kanagawa Dental College, Yokosuka 238‐8580, Japan. 2National Institute of Radiological Sciences, Anagawa, Chiba 263‐8555, Japan. It has been thought that free radicals play a significant role in certain aspects of various diseases, and particularly in brain ischemia, brain tumor, and neurodegenerative diseases. Therefore, we need to understand the physiological and pathophysiological role of free radical generation in the brain. This study examined two nitroxyl compounds, blood–brain‐barrier (BBB)‐permeable 3‐methoxycarbonyl‐2,2,5,5‐tetramethyl‐pyrrolidine‐1‐yloxy (MC‐PROXYL) and BBB‐impermeable carbamoyl‐PROXYL, as spin probes for in vivo ESR technique. Preliminary comparisons were made by ESR imaging of the heads of live mice and using MC‐PROXYL and the carbamoyl‐PROXYL. The results showed that MC‐PROXYL, but not carbamoyl‐PROXYL, was widely distributed in the head region. These methods were also applied for the imaging of isolated brains from SHR and SHRSP. We could detect the high oxidative stress in the isolated brain from SHR and SHRSP using ESR imaging system. Our laboratories have previously reported that the ESR 2D images of the distribution of MC‐PROXYL in the head region by using in vivo ESR imaging system, however, we could not obtain slice patterns of ESR–CT images or the 3D distribution of MC‐PROXYL in the head region. As a result of further development of the ESR imaging technique, we have now obtained slice patterns of ESR–CT images in the head region of living mice, and have succeeded in reconstructing 3D images of the distribution of carbamoyl‐PROXYL in the head region non‐invasively. Thus, in vivo ESR imaging of spin probes in the head region could be a powerful technique for investigating the free radical‐induced brain diseases. Further advances in the instrumentation of ESR imaging and this technology holds great promise for non‐invasive assessment of free radical induced oxidative stress in the head region, and may be of great advantage in the clinical treatment and diagnosis of brain disease. Pathophysiological Mechanism of Increased Microvascular Permeability in SHRSP Brain.Hiroyuki Ishida, Hiroyuki Ito, Kumiko Takemori, and Tsuneyuki Suzuki. Department of Pathology, Kinki University School of Medicine, Osaka‐Sayama, Osaka, 589‐8511, Japan. It is well known that cerebral edema is an important initial event of various kinds of cerebral injuries such as stroke, hypertensive cerebral injury, and so on. However, the mechanisms behind the increase of vascular permeability in severe hypertension still remain to be clarified. In cerebral microvessels, the blood–brain barrier consists of endothelial cells and glial cells. In the present study, to elucidate the molecular mechanisms of increased microvessel permeability, we investigated the expression of glucose transporter‐1 (GLUT‐1, a marker of the blood–brain barrier) and aquaporin‐4 (AQP‐4, a water channel protein) in cerebral cortex. We further investigated the effects of renin‐angiotensin system blockade on these molecule expression. Male SHRSP and Wistar‐Kyoto rats (WKY), as control, were used at 19 weeks of age, just before the onset of cerebral edema. Angiotensin converting enzyme inhibitor (ACEI, Captopril, 20 mg kg−1 day−1) and Angiotensin II type 1 receptor blocker (ARB, TCV‐116, Takeda Chemical Industries, Ltd., Osaka, Japan, 0.5 mg kg−1 day−1) were orally administered for 4 weeks. The dose of these antihypertensive agents were determined as minimum as in decreasing of blood pressure. Using cerebral cortex, expression of GLUT‐1 and AQP‐4 were investigated by immunohistochemical staining, Western blot analysis, and RT‐PCR examinations. It was clearly demonstrated by immunohistochemical examination that GLUT‐1 was positively stained in endothelial cells and AQP‐4 was positively stained in microglia around microvessels. The expression of GLUT‐1 in the cerebral cortex was downregulated in SHRSP both in protein level and mRNA level as compared to that in WKY. AQP‐4 expression in SHRSP cerebral cortex was higher in protein level, but was lower in mRNA level, as compared to that in WKY. The treatment by ACEI and ARB ameliorates such altered expression of GLUT‐1 and AQP‐4 in SHRSP, being similar to that in WKY. These results indicate that increased microvascular permeability in SHRSP cerebral cortex is caused by altered expression of GLUT‐1 in endothelial cells and AQP‐4 in microglia. Furthermore, the results show that ACEI and ARB have beneficial effects on the prevention of cerebral edema in addition to lowering blood pressure. Thus, Angiotensin II may play an important role as a mediator in hypertensive microvascular injuries. Development of Cataract After Ligation of the Bilateral Carotid Arteries (at a One‐Week Interval) and Changes in Behavioral Movement in SHR.R. Hoire,1T. Kagawa,2T. Katube,3W. Saito,4and T. Harada.11Department of Pathology, 2Neurosurgery, 3Opthalmology and 4Psychiatry, Shimane Medical University, Izumo 693‐8501, Japan. Ligation of the bilateral carotid arteries at a one‐week interval in spontaneously hypertensive rats (SHR; Okamoto, K., Aoki, K., 1963) aggravated hypertension as severe as that in stroke‐prone SHR (SHRSP; Okamoto, K., Yamori, Y., Nagaoka, A., 1974), (>240 mmHg), and cerebral neurologic manifestations similar to those observed in SHRSP developed. In this study, we reconfirmed the development of cataract early after ligation of the bilateral carotid arteries, which was previously recognized by Kagwa, in 1991, and examined changes in the amount of movement in those animals. Experiment I: Among animal models maintained at the animal study facility of Shimane Medical University, 19 male SHR aged 3 months were used. The animals were divided into two groups: eight animals in which the bilateral carotid arteries were ligated at a one‐week interval (Ligated G.) and eight sham‐operated animals (Sham‐Operated G). Changes in blood pressure (BP) and body weight (BW) were measured and the modified open field test based on the Van den Buuse method was performed (the upper half of a round container with a diameter of 62 cm was covered and lit with an incandescent lamp over the container, adjusting the luminous intensity to 300 lux, and the time of staying in the dark region (3 lux) during the 3‐min period was measured three times in each rat, and the mean value was obtained). Experiment II: Male rats aged 13, 15, and 17 months (18, 15, and 8 animals, respectively, divided into the Ligated G. and Sham‐operated G.). These animals underwent the light‐dark‐light (LDL) test reported by Saito, W. (in the open filed test, the amount of movement was measured for 1 min in light, dark, and light, a total of three minutes). Experiment I: Cataract developed in four of nine SHR (44%) in the Ligated G. No cataract developed in the Sham‐operated G. The stay time in the dark region was significantly shorter in the Ligated G. (P < 0.05). In the Ligated G., however, the stay time in the dark region was not significantly shorter in animals without cataract than in animals with cataract. Experiment II: The LDL pattern was the normal SHR pattern (the amount of movement is high during the dark period and low during the light period) in the Sham‐operated G., while the behavioral pattern in the Ligated G. was similar to the pattern of SHRSP (excluding the first light period, the amount of movement gradually decreases during the subsequent dark and light periods without a significant difference between the dark and light periods). We previously reported that ligation of the bilateral carotid arteries at a one‐week interval in SHR decreased cerebral blood flow caused by increased BP, resulting in cerebral neurologic manifestations similar to those in SHRSP. In addition, the amount of movement under light and dark conditions became close to the patten observed in SHRSP, and cataract developed early after ligation. These findings may be probably due to cerebral ischemia caused by decreased cerebral blood flow caused by aggravation of hypertension in SHR. Effects of a Selective α4β2 Nicotinic Acetylcholine Receptor Agonist and Nefirecetam on Short‐Term Memory Deficit in Juvenile Stroke‐Prone Spontaneously Hypertensive Rats, an Animal Model of Attention‐Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder (AD/HD).Ken‐ichi Ueno,1Hiroko Togashi,1Hideya Saito,2Masaru Minami,3and Mitsuhiro Yoshioka.11Department of Pharmacology, Hokkaido University Graduate School of Medicine, Sapporo 060‐8638, Japan. 2Department of Basic Sciences, Japan Red Cross Hokkaido College of Nursing, Kitami 090‐0011, Japan. 3Department of Pharmacology, Faculty of Pharmaceutical Sciences, Health Sciences University of Hokkaido, Ishikari‐Tobetsu 061‐0293, Japan. Attention‐deficit/hyperactivity disorder (AD/HD) is a disorder of developmentally inappropriate degree of inattention and/or hyperactive‐impulsive behavior, not due to pervasive developmental disorder, severe mental retardation and psychosis. Psychostimulants therapy, such as methylphenidate, D‐amphetamine and pemoline, has been the mainstay of the pharmacological treatment of AD/HD whereas they have detrimental adverse effects. The objective of the present study was to develop a new therapeutic strategy based on non‐psychostimulant treatment for ameliorating AD/HD symptoms. Recently, therapeutic vista for AD/HD has been focused on the treatment with nicotine skin patch or nicotine receptor agonist. Thus, the present study was carried out to clarify the role of nicotinic acetylcholine receptor (nAChR) subunits and to examine the effects of pyrrolidone‐type nootropic drug nefiracetam, which modulates cholinergic transmission, on short‐term memory deficit in juvenile stroke‐prone spontaneously hypertensive rats (SHRSP), an animal model of AD/HD. Male SHRSP (6 weeks of age) and age‐ and sex‐matched Wistar‐Kyoto rats (WKY) as genetic control were used. Short‐term memory was evaluated by spontaneous alternation performance using a Y‐maze. Methylphenidate (MPH; 0.01–1 mg kg−1) or (−)‐nicotine (NIC; 0.1–1 mg kg−1) was administred intraperitoneally (i.p.) and subcutaneously (s.c.) 20 min prior to the Y‐maze task, respectively. A centrally acting non‐selective nAChR antagonist mecamylamine (MEC; 1 mg kg−1, i.p.), a peripherally acting nAChR antagonist hexamethonium (HEX; 5 mg kg−1, i.p.), a selective α4β2 nAChR antagonist dihydro‐β‐erythroidine (DHβE; 3–10 mg kg−1, i.p.) or a selective α7 nAChR antagonist methyllycaconitine (MLA; 3–10 mg kg−1, i.p.) was injected 10 min prior to (−)‐NIC administration. A selective α4β2 nAChR agonist RJR‐2403 (1–10 mg kg−1, s.c.) or a pyrrolidone derivative nefiracetam (3–30 mg kg−1, i.p.) was administered 20 min prior to the Y‐maze task. Spontaneous alternation impairment based on attention‐deficit observed in SHRSP was significantly ameliorated by a treatment with MPH or (−)‐NIC. (−)‐NIC (1 mg kg−1, s.c.)‐induced spontaneous alternation improvement was significantly antagonized by a pretreatment with MEC or DHβE in a dose‐dependent manner, but not with HEX or MLA. Furthermore, RJR‐2403 or nefiracetam also dose‐dependently and significantly improved spontaneous alternation deficit in SHRSP. These results reveal that cholinergic intervention improves short‐term memory, pos‐sibly via the activation of central α4β2, but not α7, nAChR in SHRSP, an animal model of AD/HD. Thus, it is suggested that cholinergic mechanism might be responsible for short‐term memory deficit in SHRSP. Alternatively, our evidence possibly indicates that selective α4β2 nAChR agonists and a pyrrolidone derivative nefiracetam may provide a new strategy as a non‐psychostimulant therapy for treating attentional dysfunction in AD/HD. Response to Cold Stress in Reciprocal Congenic Rats for Chromosome‐1 Blood Pressure QTL.T. Nabika,1K. Ikeda,2Z. H. Cui,1Y. Yamori,3and J. Masuda.11Department of Laboratory Medicine, Shimane Medical University. 2School of Human Environmental Sciences, Mukogawa Women's University. 3WHO Collaborating Center. The sympathetic nervous system plays an important role in the pathogenesis of hypertension in SHRSP. In this rat strain, sympathetic responses to various stimuli were exaggerated. In this study, we examined the response to cold stress in reciprocal congenic strains for the chromosome‐1 QTL for blood pressure (BP) to see whether this QTL affected the stress response in the rats. Two reciprocal congenic strains, WKYpch1.0 and SHRSPwch1.0 were constructed by serial backcrossing between WKY/Izm and SHRSP/Izm. The region between D1Wox29 and D1Arb21 was introgressed from SHRSP/Izm to WKY/Izm in WKYpch1.0, and vice versa in SHRSPwch1.0. This congenic fragment covered the 100:1 confidence interval for the chromosome‐1 QTL for BP. Blood pressure was measured by the tail‐cuff method. Rats at 12 weeks old were exposed to cold stress for 6 h at 4°C. The urine was collected to measure catecholamines by HPLC. Blood pressure tended to be higher in WKYpch1.0 (n = 5) than in WKY (n = 7) (146 ± 2.5 vs. 139 ± 8.1 mmHg, p = 0.07), and was significantly lower in SHRSPwch1.0 (n = 5) than in SHRSP (n = 5) (215 ± 13 vs. 243 ± 13 mmHg, p < 0.01). However, BP of SHRSPwch1.0 was still higher than that of WKYpch1.0 (p < 0.001), indicating the contribution of additional genes other than the one on chromosome 1. In contrast, urinary norepinephrine (NE) excretion was higher in SHRSP and WKYpch1.0 than in WKY and SHRSPwch1.0 (5610 ± 1250, 6360 ± 590, 3950 ± 560, and 4200 ± 290 pmol/6 h, respectively, p < 0.0001 by ANOVA). Significant differences were not observed between SHRSP and WKYpch1.0 (p = 0.26 by the t‐test), and between WKY and SHRSPwch1.0 (p = 0.40 by the t‐test), in spite of significant BP differences between them. This observation suggested that urinary NE excretion under the cold stress was controlled by the chromosome‐1 QTL region in a monogenic manner, and that this phenotype may be an intermediate phenotype of the hypertension gene. Evaluation of Physical Activity of Stroke‐Prone SHR (SHRSP).W. Saito,1R. Horie,2J. Horiguchi, and T. Harada. 1Department of Pathology and 2Psychiatry Shimane Medical University, Izumo, 693‐8501, Japan. To evaluate the relationship between hypertension and activity, we improved the method of measurement used to obtain an index of activity, and evaluated the activity of stroke‐prone spontaneously hypertensive rats (SHRSP) before the occurrence of hypertension‐induced organic changes in the brain. Male SHRSP maintained at the Department of Pathology Shimane Medical University were used in this study. Wistar–Kyoto rats (WKY) were used as the controls. These rats were maintained under a 12‐hour light and dark cycle. Using a circular open field of 62 cm in diameter with the base radially divided into 19 segments, the number of segments where rats migrated in light area of 300 lux or in a dark area of 3 lux was respectively measured for 3 min on the day after measuring body weight (BW) and blood pressure (BP). Experiment 1: Twelve‐week‐old rats were divided into the following two groups: a day group consisting of 21 SHRSP and 26 WKY, in which the amount of movement was measured between 9 a.m. and 12 p.m., and a night group consisting of 39 SHRSP and 37 WKY, in which the amount of movement was measured between 9 p.m. and 12 a.m. Experiment 2: The activity level was measured in 6‐week‐old SHRSP (n = 40) and WKY (n = 17), 8‐week‐old SHRSP (n = 40) and WKY (n = 40), 12‐week‐old SHRSP (n = 39) and WKY (n = 37), 20‐week‐old SHRSP (n = 8) and WKY (n = 16), and 24‐week‐old SHRSP (n = 14) and WKY (n = 8). Subsequently, the characteristics of the activity during the night (9 p.m.–12 a.m.) were serially evaluated among different age groups to compare between the two strains. Experiment 1: Although the activity level during the day and night did not significantly differ among WKY, SHRSP in the day group showed a significantly less activity in a dark area than those in the night group (P < 0.05). When activity in a light area was compared to that in a dark area, WKY showed significantly more activity in a dark area than in a light area during both the day and night (P < 0.05). However, SHRSP in the night group showed a significant difference between activity in a light area and that in a dark area (P < 0.05). When activity during the day and night was compared between SHRSP and WKY, SHRSP showed significantly more activity than WKY solely in a light area during both the day and night (P < 0.05), but activity in a dark area did not significantly differ between the two strains. Experiment 2: Among the respective strains, activity did not correlate with BP or BW. Stroke‐prone spontaneously hypertensive rats always showed significantly higher BP than WKY (P < 0.01). During Experiment 2 performed during the night, both strains of rats showed more activity in a dark area than in a light area in all age groups. Although activity in a dark area did not significantly differ between WKY and SHRSP, SHRSP showed significantly more activity in a light area than WKY (P < 0.05). Hendley et al. suggested that a higher activity and susceptibility to hypertension were not due to the same gene or genes mapped on near loci. However, higher BP in SHRSP than in WKY despite almost the same movement level in a dark area may support their speculation. The Contribution of Myosin Light Chain Kinase Cascades to the Increased Epinephrine Release from the Adrenal Gland in Spontaneous Hypertensive Rats.Hisashi Ohmori, Takuzo Hano, Hiroko Nakanishi, and Ichiro Nishio. Department of Cardiovascular Medicine, Wakayama Medical University, Wakayama, 641‐8510, Japan. In spontaneous hypertensive rats (SHR/Izm), an increased catecholamine turnover and synthesis rate and an accelerated epinephrine secretion have been observed in the adrenal medulla. The mechanism of enhanced adrenal function is related to CaM kinase II‐dependent cascades in our previous report (Ohmori et al., Jpn Heart J 1998; 39: 559). Extracellular Ca and calmodulin contributes to the epinephrine release in rat adrenal gland, and there is no difference in this contribution between each rat strains (Ohmori et al., Clin Exp. Hypertension 2002; 24(6): 424). It was reported that Myosin light chain kinase (MLCK) inhibitors inhibited the Ca2+‐induced catecholamine secretion in bovine adrenal chromaffin cells, and that MLCK cascade was associated with the vesicle priming reaction. However, in SHR/Izm, the role of MLCK cascades of the epinephrine release from the adrenal gland is unknown. The present study was designed to clarify the role of MLCK cascades in the release of epinephrine from adrenal gland, and it's contribution to the increased adrenal function in SHR/Izm, using MLCK inhibitor, ML‐7. Male SHR/Izm (8 weeks, n = 5) and Wistar–Kyoto rats (WKY/Izm) (8 weeks, n = 6) were used. The left adrenal gland was cannulated through the adrenal vein. Retrograde perfusion was immediately performed with Krebs–Henseleit solution at room temperature. Epinephrine released by electrical stimulation (5 Hz, 20 V, 30 sec.) was measured. Effects of ML‐7 (0.01 µM, 0.1 µM, 1.0 µM, 10 µM) on epinephrine release under perfusion with Krebs–Henseleit solution were examined. The absolute amount of epinephrine release by electrical stimulation was greater in SHR/Izm (43.0 ± 12.9 ng, mean ± SEM) than that in WKY/Izm (26.2 ± in 5.9 ng). The absolute amount of epinephrine released by electrical stimulation was suppressed in the presence of ML‐7. Percent change of epinephrine release by ML‐7 were not significantly different between SHR/Izm and WKY/Izm. In summary, epinephrine release from adrenal gland was significantly accelerated in SHR/Izm. The inhibitory effect of ML‐7 on epinephrine release was no different between SHR/Izm and WKY/Izm. Otherwise the down stream of CaM kinase II‐dependent cascades might contribute to the increased epinephrine release from adrenal gland in SHR/Izm. The increased adrenal function in SHR/Izm was associated with CaM kinase II Cascade, but not with MLCK cascade. [ABSTRACT FROM AUTHOR] Copyright of Clinical & Experimental Hypertension is the property of Taylor & Francis Ltd and its content may not be copied or emailed to multiple sites or posted to a listserv without the copyright holder's express written permission. However, users may print, download, or email articles for individual use. This abstract may be abridged. No warranty is given about the accuracy of the copy. Users should refer to the original published version of the material for the full abstract. (Copyright applies to all Abstracts.)
