AB  - The rural Latino farmworker population is underserved in health care, including care for chronic diseases. Through a Rural Health Outreach Grant, the small bilingual staff of the Familias Saludables screened 1,300 individuals and enrolled 338 of them to educate those with hypertension, hypercholesterolemia, diabetes, and depression, and manage their conditions through a family intervention model. In these primarily farmworker rural Latino participants, diastolic blood pressure and hemoglobin A1c (HbA1c) were significantly reduced at 6, 12, and 18 months, and cholesterol low-density lipoprotein (LDL) through 12 months. Systolic blood pressure was also reduced, but the deduction was not statistically significant. Most of the participants who screened positive for depression chose not to participate in individual or group interventions and follow-up measurements. Other challenges included bureaucratic delays and migration of participants to Mexico during winter months.
