AB  - This study assessed the personality pathology of 125 chronic low back pain (CLBP) patients in a functional restoration program, using three methods of personality assessment, the SCID II, the SNAP, and the MMPI. Distinct profiles of personality pathology emerged for the CLBP patients, as compared to normals, regardless of method of assessment. Furthermore, distinct subgroups of these patients were identified according to their MMPI and SNAP profiles, suggesting that CLBP patients are a heterogenous group. The derivation of homogeneous subgroups of these patients has important treatment implications, especially if the results of this study are replicated. Although personality pathology was not related to specific long-term treatment outcome, such as return to work status at one-year follow up, it was concluded that the treatment failure group in this study was likely too small to detect effects of personality pathology. This study also determined that the personality pathology of CLBP patients seems to essentially remain stable over time, even after they have been physically rehabilitated and much of the psychological sequelae to their disability has been successfully treated by functional restoration treatment. It was therefore hypothesized that personality pathology likely impairs these patients' ability to cope with the stress of chronic pain and therefore, may play a role in the development of chronicity. It was further emphasized that clinicians should be sensitive to the complex psychological nature of these patients and appreciated that managing co-existing Axis I and Axis II pathology is vital to maximize successful physical rehabilitation outcome. Additionally, this study compared dimensionally based personality disorder assessment with the traditional categorical method. Although researchers often argue for one approach or the other, the present study suggests that, together, they provide important clinical information and empirical clarification of the re (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
