AB  - Background: Alcoholic liver disease (ALD) is the leading cause of cirrhosis in Western countries and the second most common indication for liver transplantation (LT). Few studies have investigated explant histology, addiction treatment and engagement in "self-help" or Alcoholics Anonymous (AA) as predictors of long-term abstinence post-transplant. This study aimed to assess risk factors for post-transplant relapse and survival in alcoholic liver recipients. Method(s): We retrospectively reviewed all LT recipients with ALD from 1/1/2000 to 12/31/2012 at Mayo Clinic Rochester. Demographic and clinical data were abstracted from an electronic medical record. Descriptive statistics are presented as percentages and mean +/- standard deviation. Correlation was made by Pearson coefficient. Categorical data analyzed by chi-square test and the quantitative by student t-test. A p-value of <0.05 was considered significant. Result(s): In total, 236 patients with ALD underwent LT. Mean follow-up was 84.8 +/- 56.4 months. Recipients were 83% males with 5-year survival of 75.8%. Relapse to any alcohol use occurred in 17.4% in the total follow-up period, with 5.9% during the 1st year and <2% after the 4th year. Of these, approximately half (47%) resumed high-dose drinking (defined as >4 standard drinks/day or 14/week for men; and >3/day or >7/ week for woman), while the rest resumed limited or low-dose drinking. No pre-transplant interventions affected outcomes when isolated (relapse, high-dose relapse, or alcohol-related death); however, combinations of interventions improved relapse outcomes (Table 1). Recipients who participated in pre-transplant treatment (PTT) and AA or PTT, AA and AA sponsorship had less high-dose relapse (p=0.04 and p=0.03, respectively). Those who underwent PTT, AA, AA sponsorship, and post-transplant AA had lowest overall (p=0.002) and high-dose relapse rates (p=0.002). Explant steatohepatitis was a strong predictor of relapse (p<0.001), but it was not associated with increased rejection, graft failure or lower survival. Conclusion(s): This study emphasizes the importance of addiction treatment and AA engagement on prevention of alcohol relapse post-transplant. Most notably, we demonstrate the additive benefit of pre-and post-transplant interventions in reducing any and high-dose alcohol relapse after liver transplant. Finally, findings of active steatohepatitis on explant should prompt for a more aggressive addiction treatment plan post-transplant. (Table Presented).
