AB  - Introduction: Inpatient cardiac rehabilitation (ICR) programs provide important servicesto patients, but they are not universally available or utilized. Additionally, little is knownabout which factors are associated with ICR utilization or about the presence of age,gender, or racial disparities. Purpose(s): To determine patient factors associated with the receipt of ICR. Design(s): Retrospective cohort. Method(s): We examined the use of ICR in a geographically and structurally diverse sampleof 223 US hospitals (PREMIER data warehouse, Inc.) that provided ICR services to patientsbetween January 2007 to June 2011. We included patients if they had an ICD-9 principaldiagnosis of myocardial infarction (MI) or heart failure (HF), or a procedure code forcoronary artery bypass surgery, valve surgery, or percutaneous coronary intervention(PCI). We considered any patient with = 1 service code for ICR to have received ICR.Patient-level predictors of the receipt of ICR were then analyzed in a hierarchal multivariate model according to treatment group (surgical procedure, PCI procedure, or medically managed HF/MI.)Results: A total of 689,615 patients were included in the study, of whom 40.4% receivedat least one session of ICR. Patients who underwent surgery or PCI were significantly morelikely to receive ICR than those with medically managed MI or HF (76.3% vs. 49.2% vs24.1% respectively, p < 0.001.) Among surgical patients, older age (OR 0.90 for 80+ vs.55-64 years, p = 0.01), non-white race (OR 0.88, p <0.001), female sex (OR 0.92, p<0.001), Medicare insurance, and higher comorbidity burden were independently associated with lower odds of receiving ICR. Results were similar among patients with PCIprocedures or medically managed MI or HF. Tobacco abuse was the only factor examinedthat was consistently associated with significantly increased ICR utilization (OR range, 1.07to 1.14, p<0.001 for all)Conclusions: Among hospitals that offer ICR, less than half of patients with an eligible diagnosis for outpatient cardiac rehabilitation ever receive ICR services. Receipt of ICR appears tobe biased towards younger, healthier patients who smoke, which may contribute to previously observed outpatient cardiac rehabilitation referral and participation disparities. Additionalresearch is needed to understand and mitigate the factors leading to these disparities.
