AB  - Counselors providing group or individual counseling to public offenders may work in a variety of areas, such as offender rehabilitation,youth service, probation or parole, vocational rehabilitation,and mental health. Many of these counselors have dual responsibilities in upholding certain agency regulations and protecting the interests of society, as well as providing counseling services to their clients. The counselor working with public offenders in agency settings often is confronted with a conflicting set of expectations from agency superiors and frequently has conflicting ideas concerning the role of the counselor. The dilemma of many public offender counselors is whether the job of the counselor is to help the client or to further the interests of the system. Clearly,this has far-reaching ethical implications for persons providing counseling services to public offenders. Many of these counselors have conflicting job roles that make counseling public offenders difficult and cause clients to play various types of authority games with them. It may be revealing to examine the types of counseling services generally offered to public offender sand the degree to which this counseling is consistent with the American Personnel and Guidance Association (APGA) Ethical Standards, Section B: Counseling Relationship. Public offenders are as entitled to receive professional counseling services as any other group of clients. APGA can take a leading role in promoting the delivery of ethical and effective counseling services to public offenders. Despite this risk, it is the ethical responsibility of APGA and its members to offer counseling services that actually help the clients. Public offenders, like other clients, need to receive professional counseling that adheres to sound ethical standard sand that upholds their basic human rights. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
