AB  - Background: Detoxification and abstinence from opioids increase the risk of opioid overdose due to the loss of tolerance. Long-term naltrexone treatment also leads to decrease in tolerance and as a result theoretically might increase the risk of overdose. Additionally, the rate of opioid overdose is even higher among those with HIV. The aim of our study was to compare the rate of fatal overdose (OD) in two cohorts in Russia with HIV and opioid use, one of which received naltrexone treatment for 12 months and the other a non-pharmacological intervention to link addiction and HIV care. Method(s): We examined baseline and 12 month follow-up (FU) data from two randomized controlled trials of HIV+ participants with opioid use disorder (OUD): LINC (Linking Infectious and Narcology Care, n = 349), evaluation of a 6-month case management intervention to increase linkage to HIV care; and HAART (Adherence to HIV Therapy in Heroin Addicts Oral vs. Extended Release Naltrexone, n = 200), treatment for 12 months with naltrexone implant + oral placebo or oral naltrexone + placebo implant in persons starting antiretroviral therapy (ART). All HAART study patients were offered biweekly drug counseling. Both cohorts were completing inpatient detoxification at the time of enrollment with HAART participants having been approved for ART. The primary outcome was the proportion with fatal OD at 12 months post enrollment. Result(s): We did not observe any significant differences between cohorts in demographic or clinical variables except depressive symptoms. LINC participants had depressive symptoms above the positive standard threshold score than HAART participants (LINC [CES-D scale >21] 76.8% vs. HAART [BDI scale >21] 38.3%, p<0.05). At 12-month FU visit 13% of LINC participants were engaged in outpatient addiction care compared to 67.5% in the HAART study (73.3% of these were on naltrexone treatment). A total of 33 (13.5%) deaths have been registered in the LINC project, 18 (7.3%) were due to drug overdose. Among HAART patients, 7 participants (5.2%) had died, and only two of the deaths were related to overdose (1.5%) (LINC 7.3% vs HAART 1.5%, p = 0.008). Conclusion(s): Fatal overdoses among HIV+ persons with opioid use from two Russian cohorts were significantly less in participants in the cohort starting ART and treated with either oral or implantable naltrexone compared to a cohort randomized to a non-pharmacological intervention to help link them to HIV care. Our results raise the possibility that naltrexone treatment might reduce the rate of fatal OD in this population. Moreover our findings highlight the need for overdose prevention interventions for persons with OUD and HIV infection. Given the limitations of this post-hoc secondary analysis comparing data from two very different studies, further examination of the role of naltrexone therapy in the prevention of fatal opioid overdose among persons with HIV+ is needed. The difference in the severity of the underlying disease and the level of depressive symptoms between two cohorts could be a factor affecting intergroup differences.Copyright © 2018
