AB  - Background: Aging populations increase the prevalence of health problems amongst the elderly. Group play may contribute positively to health and development in this vulnerable population. Purpose: Authors designed this study to measure frailty amongst nursing home residents prior to and after receiving a group play intervention. Methods: Sixty residents staying at two nursing homes in Kaohsiung County participated in this quasi-experimental study. Experimental group participants joined 10 play sessions over a five-week period, while the control group did not. Both groups submitted responses to the same questionnaires, distributed prior to and after the intervention. Results: The experimental group showed a significant difference (p < .01) between pre-and post-intervention frailty. The control group showed no significant change in frailty between the two periods. Also, change in experimental group frailty was more significant than in the control group (p < .01). Conclusions: Group play can decrease degree of frailty in nursing home residents. Thus, such may be applied in long-term-care facilities. To enhance the physical and mental health of nursing home residents, the authors suggest including information on group play in nursing continuing education programs that will enhance healthcare staff understanding of group play advantages and methods.
