AB  - Background and Aim: There is a high prevalence of hepatitis C virus (HCV) among prisoners globally. Recent data show that HCV seroprevalence in Australian prisoners exceeds 25%, reflecting the criminalization of injecting drug use (IDU), the primary risk factor for transmission in developed countries. In this context, the Statewide Hepatitis Program was developed to provide HCV treatment to all prisoners in Victoria, Australia. However, a subset of prisoners are released untreated due to inadequate sentence duration. There are no data about the likelihood of linkage to HCV care among this group, who face competing priorities on release relating to accommodation, employment, returning to social networks, and recidivism. People who inject drugs (PWIDs) are less likely to access medical care in the community. We aim to determine the likelihood of commencing HCV therapy among this group. Method(s): Prisoners with active HCV infection, defined as detectable HCV RNA, were eligible for inclusion if released without treatment from a Victorian prison. Before release, prisoners were assessed by a program nurse and underwent blood-based investigations and transient elastrography (FibroScan). Prisoners were referred to a health care service near their intended residence for treatment after release. The primary outcome was commencement of HCV therapy within 6 months of release from prison, confirmed through national medication prescribing records. Result(s): Seventy-five HCV RNA-positive prisoners were consecutively recruited and followed for 6 months after release. The mean age was 40 years, 88% were male, and 15% had cirrhosis. A subset discussed risk behaviors, 78% of whom identified as PWID in the month before incarceration or had injected during their current prison sentence (52/67). Thirtysix percent were prescribed opioid substitution therapy (OST). A psychiatric illness was reported by 75%. Reincarceration rate within 6 months was 21%. Only 25% (19/75) were prescribed HCV therapy within 6 months of release. Of these, 37% (7/19) were prescribed treatment on reincarceration. Treatment commencement was associated with reincarceration (P = 0.011) and IDU before incarceration (P = 0.048) on univariate analysis; however, on multivariate analysis, only reincarceration was significant for commencing HCV treatment within 6 months (P = 0.028.) Treatment commencement was not associated with psychiatric illness, cirrhosis, or OST. Conclusion(s): A minority of prisoners released with HCV were capable of accessing treatment in the community, which was significantly more likely if they were reincarcerated. The development of novel models to engage this vulnerable population are required for treatment scale-up among this priority group to achieve HCV elimination.
