AB  - The Hypnotic Ability Response Profile, or HARP was developed as a hypnotizability assessment that obtains clinical information useful for high, medium and low-levels of hypnotizability. In this study, a replication of the pilot validation study, 24 inpatients from Tinley Park Mental Health Center participated in a counterbalanced experimental design, during which they were administered both the HARP and Stanford Hypnotic Clinical Scale (SHCS). Diagnostic categories included psychotic disorder, bipolar disorder, substance abuse, obsessive-compulsive disorder, and depressive disorder. The HARP consists of eight scales, five of which were rated for internal consistency. The HARP total score demonstrated extremely high internal consistency (r = 0.88, p <01), which was higher than in the pilot study. The HARP correlates highly significantly with the SHCS which is a well-validated and established measure of hypnotic responsiveness. This study contributes to the establishment of the HARP as reliable measure of hypnotic responsiveness. As was expected from the literature, psychotic subjects demonstrated a lower level of hypnotizability than non-psychotic subjects. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
