AB  - Background: Historically, there has been a treatment divide between mood and substance use disorders within mental healthcare (Newman 2008). Having both a substance use disorder and mood disorder can increase the risk of bad outcomes, such as suicide attempts or completions (Grant et al 2004; Newman, 2008). As a result, these patients are in need of improved comprehensive care to address the severity and complexity of their illnesses. While 12 step groups can be effective, Cognitive Behavioral Therapy, CBT, is effective as an evidence-based means to treat both mood disorders and substance use (Newman, 2008; Riper, et al 2013; Sobell and Sobell, 2011). Currently, there is no substance use therapy group at Los Angeles County Hospital, Adult Outpatient Psychiatry Clinic (AOPC), despite a large patient population with substance use and comorbid psychiatric disorders. Given the high demand for limited resources, we aimed to implement a pilot of short term group CBT for all substance use disorders to better serve our population. If successful, it may provide an efficient and effective treatment option for similar populations across the country. Method(s): This pilot study was intended to determine the feasibility of implementing a short term CBT based substance use group within the Los Angeles County+University of Southern California (LAC+USC) Adult Outpatient Psychiatry Clinic. Measures, handouts, and homework were all obtained from: Sobell, LC and Sobell, MB. (2011). Group Therapy for Substance Use Disorders: A Motivational Cognitive-Behavioral Approach. New York, NY: The Guilford Press. Fals-Stewart, W, O'Farrell, TJ, Hooley, JM. Relapse Among Married or Cohabiting Substance-Abusing Patients: The Role of Perceived Cricism. Behavior Therapy, 32, 787-801. 2001 And Background questionnaire courtesy of Dr. Barbara Van Noppen All participants underwent an initial assessment of their background, substance use and current readiness. They then participated in 4 group therapy sessions as outlined in Sobell and Sobell 2011 with 1 additional optional session for psychoeducation with the group substance use counselor. Weekly substance use logs were completed, with periodic situational confidence questionnaires, perceived criticism, homework assignments assisting with option plans during triggering situations, and decisional balance exercises. Subjects: 6 participants were initially recruited and one was excluded for failure to attend sessions. Ages ranged from 28-63 years old, four women and 1 man participated. Substance use disorders included methamphetamines, tobacco, alcohol, and marijuana. There was no control group. Patients were referred by other psychiatry residents in the clinic and were required to be willing to participate in a group, complete homework and be able to utilize CBT based techniques. Analysis: Excel was utilized to examine changes over time, means, and standard deviation. Qualitative measures such as participants' perceived triggers were recorded during groups with the challenges and advantages to their option plans documented. Result(s): Common triggers on the brief situational confidence questionnaire (BSCQ) included pleasant times and Social Pressure, but results varied widely between the participants. These differences were utilized in developing options in their triggers. These triggering situations included "being bored", "going to a party", "having a bad mood", and "when my son drinks" per patient reports. On average, the BSCQ did not change more than 10% between the first and third session. The only areas which showed positive change were Pleasant Times and Social Pressure. Perceived criticism was not related to the amount of change on the BSCQ or amount that use was decreased. Based on weekly logs, participants largely showed a decrease in use, but one alcohol user did have a binge in the middle of treatment, which was utilized in session to assist with future option plans. One participant had consistent difficulty quantifying marijuana use which complicated its interpretation. The end of therapy satisfaction questionnaire showed that participants found the sessions helpful, liked the program length, found homework useful and were satisfied with being in the group. Conclusion(s): Our pilot showed that providing this short term, CBT based group treatment for substance use is feasible within the LAC+USC AOPC clinic. Participants generally were able to decrease their use. They were able to form alternative plans during triggering scenarios and reported utilizing them with success during the group. We hypothesize that while some areas of the brief situational confidence questionnaire showed improvement, participants' overall confidence level may have decreased with increased awareness about their substance use. Finally, patients found the group helpful and were satisfied with the knowledge they gained. Limitations include our small number of participants, lack of control group, and difficulty comparing between substances. Despite the limitations, utilizing a group-based CBT model for patients appears effective in a community clinic that provides treatment to underserved people. We hope to continue and grow the group-based CBT for substance use. This model type may be effective in similar clinics across the country and warrants replication. Summary: We hope to show that group based CBT for substance use disorders is effective for a diverse and underserved patient population. Our pilot allowed for integration of mood and substance use disorder treatment within the same clinic to increase access to care. We hope to increase the utilization of this group within our clinic this upcoming year and assist more of our patients with overcoming their substance use. Hopefully this will also encourage other clinics with underserved populations to offer similar services.
