AB  - Research Objectives: To investigate the feasibility of offering an evidence-based brain injury coping skills (BICS) group via telemedicine. Design(s): Feasibility trial. Setting(s): Post-acute Rehabilitation Hospital. Participant(s): Thirteen participants completed the study. Participants consisted of nine brain injury survivors and four caregivers.Participants consisted of both males (N = 6) and females (N = 7), were mostly Caucasian (N = 11) and African American (N = 2), and were between the ages of 22 and 69 years. Of the brain injury survivors, 4 had a traumatic brain injury (TBI), 3 had a stroke, and 2 had another brain injury diagnosis. All participants were at least 6 months post-injury, ranging from 1 to 15 years post-injury. Of the caregivers, 1 was a parent and 3 were spouses. Intervention(s): All participants were provided IPads preloaded with Zoom for use during the study. The BICS-T intervention lasted 12 weeks consisting of one, two-hour weekly session. The twelve sessions were live/interactive and covered six modules (Introduction to the Brain Injury, Expectations for Recovery, Tips on Managing Challenging Problems, Depression after Brain Injury, Stress Management, and Communicating Effectively with Professionals). Main Outcome Measure(s): Primary outcome measures include the Brain Injury Coping Skills Questionnaire (BICSQ) for assessing self-efficacy, Group Climate Questionnaire (GCQ), and custom satisfaction ratings unique to the intervention. Result(s): Similar to the results from the publication on in-person BICS, group participants showed significant improvements in self-efficacy (p=.014) from baseline to end of treatment, as well as high satisfaction and group cohesion post treatment. Conclusion(s): It is feasible to provide BICS via telemedicine to patients/caregivers with brain injury. Next steps include an RCT comparing BICS-T to in-person BICS. If successful, BICST could be offered to homebound/rural patients. Author(s) Disclosures: N/A. Keywords: Brain Injuries, Traumatic, Feasibility Studies, TelemedicineCopyright © 2020
