AB  - The article provides information on the journal "Family Relations." For 2002, the planned special collection titled "Family Resilience" for the April issue is well under way. This project represents the culmination of first joint effort with "Journal of Marriage and Family," in collaboration with the Center for Mental Health Services, to highlight a topic of particular interest to researchers and practitioners. Two additional special issues are being planned for 2003 and 2004. One is tentatively titled "The Legacy of Divorce: Controversies, Clarifications, and Consequences," with Drs. Sanford Braver and Jeffrey Cookston as special editors. The papers in this issue will explore viewpoints and data sets relevant to evaluation of the long-term effects of divorce and their implications for practice, program, and/or policy. The second special issue will focus on alcoholism and families, with Dr. W. Glenn Clingempeel serving as special editor. The editor assures the readers that without the expertise of dedicated professionals, "Family Relations," would be much less than it is.
