AB  - The Sobriety Management Series was developed as a specific treatment protocol to assist clients in the transition from hospitalization for severe chemical dependency to sober living in the community. Forty-five adult male poly-substance dependent clients participated in the study. The investigation took place from July 21, 2000 through November 15, 2000 at Connecticut Valley Hospital-Addiction Services Division, Middletown, Connecticut. The study compared three treatment modalities to discover effectiveness in retention in treatment and outcome. The three treatment groups were: #1-Relapse Prevention Training (RPT), #2-Relapse Prevention, Motivational Enhancement Therapy-Sobriety Management (RPT/MET/SMS) and #3-Standard Rehabilitation Treatment (SR). The Addiction Severity Index (ASI) was administered at two points: admission to treatment and 30 days post-discharge. The Sobriety Management Checklist and the SOCRATES (Stages Of Change Readiness And Treatment Eagerness Scale) were also used in this study. The results of the study showed that there was a significant decrease in drug use in treatment groups #1 and #2 at 30 days after discharge. The only life dimension showed significant change was the drug use scale. A larger sample may have demonstrated a more significant difference on family conflict and psychological scales. The interviews revealed that participants who returned to their usual residences experienced an increase in anxious and depressive symptoms. Those who reported more satisfaction post-discharge were predominantly participants who took residence in 1/2 way or 3/4 way houses. Further study in early recovery is necessary to address family conflict, depression and anxiety that may occur following an in-patient treatment experience. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
