AB  - Introduction Intrathecal morphine (ITM) provides analgesia following total joint arthroplasty (TJA) . However, ITM is associated with increased post-operative nausea and vomiting (PONV), pruritus and respiratory depression . A multimodal analgesic approach for TJA significantly reduces postoperative pain compared to a mostly opioid based approach . In patients undergoing TJA, the relative risks and benefits of adding ITM to multimodal analgesia has not been studied. Materials and methods (NA for case report) We retrospectively analyzed primary TJA patients at a single institution, who received Bupivacaine spinal anesthesia alone (control) or with ITM. We stratified ITM administration as low (100 micrograms or less) or high (greater than 100 micrograms) dose. Multimodal analgesia consisted of an adductor canal perineural catheter, pre- and postoperative acetaminophen, Neurontin and NSAID medications. PONV was identified by Ondansetron use, secondary anti-emetic use, and emesis. Multivariate regression predicting PONV was performed to control for confounders. This study was approved by the UCSF institutional review board. Results/Case report 849 patients were identified (140 control, 68 low dose ITM, 641 high dose ITM) with similar baseline characteristics and intraoperative administration of PONV prophylaxis (Table 1). A significantly greater percentage of patients who received high dose ITM were taking opioids preoperatively compared to either low dose ITM or control groups (Table 1). On postoperative day 0 (POD0), mean VAS score was significantly lower in both low (1.6+/-1.7) and high (1.4+/-1.6) dose ITM compared to control (2.4+/-1.9) groups (Table 2). Patients receiving ITM were able to ambulate significantly farther and in a dose-dependent manner (control: 97.4+/-138.9m, low dose ITM: 128+/-159.1m, high dose ITM: 147.7+/-163.1m) (Table 2). Patients receiving ITM were less likely to develop PONV during their hospital stay (control: 50.0%, Low ITM dose: 42.6%, high ITM dose: 38.2%) (Table 2). Throughout their hospital stay patients receiving a low ITM dose consumed significantly fewer morphine equivalents (50.9+/-68.4) than control (81+/-163.6) or high ITM dose (76.4+/-156.1) (Table 2). No significant differences were seen over the entire hospital stay for use of diphenhydramine/naloxone for pruritus, rates of discharge to care facility, lengths of stay, and 90-day readmission rates (Table 2). Discussion Patients receiving ITM reported lower average VAS pain scores on POD0 which likely facilitated the increase in mobilization observed on POD 1. Interestingly, patients receiving low dose ITM consumed significantly fewer morphine equivalents than those receiving high dose ITM. We anticipated that increasing the dose of ITM would decrease morphine equivalent consumption postoperatively, however, this was not observed. This is likely due to more patients in the high dose ITM group taking opioids prior to surgery and continuation of their baseline opioids in the perioperative period. The incidence of PONV decreased with ITM. Although ITM administration itself is associated with development of PONV, we hypothesize that the rate of PONV was lower in patients receiving ITM because of decreased opioid consumption and subsequent opioid induced PONV. While multimodal analgesia and regional anesthesia have improved post-operative pain control, the addition of ITM improved POD0 analgesia, decreased post-operative opioid consumption, and decreased PONV.
