PY  - 1994
DA  - 1994///
Y1  - 1994///
AB  - The high rate of benzodiazepines (BZD) consumption has been repeatedly confirmed by epidemiological surveys in most major western world countries. In a recent french survey 7 % of chronic users of BZD (use in 517 days for the last 12 months) were found the general population (17 % in the population aged above 65). It has been suggested that the high BZD consumption rate could be related to dependence. The existence of BZD dependence was described in the early sixties with very high dose of chlordiazepoxide but it has become a real concern for the medical community since the late seventies with increasing number of reports of withdrawal symptoms. The extend of the actual rate of withdrawal symptoms at BZD tapering is still very controversial and according to the different studies it varies from 39 to 90 %. The between studies difference in parameters such as: the patient populations (psychopathology, treatment duration), the type of tapering employed (duration, nature of the medical and psychological support) and the used operational criteria for withdrawal definition most likely explain this wide variation in the rate of occurence of withdrawal manifestations. According to the American Psychiatric Association Task Force on Benzodiazepine Dependence, Toxicity and Abuse three type of pathological events can happen after treatment discontinuation: rebound, withdrawal syndrome and recurence. The rebound consists in the early and transitory reapperance of the anxiety symptoms pre-existing to the treatment but in an exacerbated form; the withdrawal syndrome associates the resurgence of the pre-existing anxiety symptoms and new symptoms as sensory disturbances (metallic taste, hyperosmia, cutaneous exacerbated sensitivity, photophobia..) nausea, headache, motor disturbance in some rare cases depersonalization, paranoid reaction, confusion, convulsion. Rebound or withdrawal syndrome appearance delay varies from hours to few days according mostly to compounds elimination half-life. The relapse develops later with a progressive reapparence of pre-treatment symptoms. In practice recurence and rebound are often difficult to isolate: recurence can follow rebound. Different operational criteria of definition for this different entities have been proposed but there is a need for a consensual position. The treatment lenght, a high daily dose, an alcohol abuse history, a dependant personality and the severity of the psychopathology of the patients have been found to be predictive for the occurence of withdrawal symptoms. Behavioural therapies (individual or in group) have been proposed with some success for the treatment of benzodiazepine dependence; drug treatment with carbamazepine or imipramine have demonstrated some efficacy. Other drug as buspirone clonidine having anxiolytic properties have not demonstrated efficacy. Biological basis of dependence have been recently explored and it has been suggested that specific functional change in BZD receptor could support the dependence phenomena. Since the early discovery of BZD receptor in 1977 a large body of knowledge has accumulated on the nature of this receptor and the mechanism of action of BZD. The BZD receptor knowledge to be a part of a macromolecular complex including a GABA receptor coupled to a chloride ionophore, exert a modulatory action on chloride conductance via its effect on GABA function. BZD receptor occupations can impact GABA function in three ways: a potentiation as exerted by classical anxiolytic BZD called agonist, a neutral effect with antagonist as flumazenil or an inhibition with inverse agonist ligand being anxiogenic and proconvulsant. This modulatory action at BZD receptor is now believed be on a continuum from a full agonist action to full inverse agonist with intermediate position called partial agonist or inverse agonist with a neutral point. Recent data from animal and human works suggested that BZD dependence could result from sensitivity change in BZD-GABA receptor complex. It has been shown that pretreatment with BZD could alter the intrinsic activity of the BZD-GABA receptor in the direction of an increase response to inverse agonist BZD receptor ligand. Authors as David Nutt and co-workers have suggested that this effect could result from a shift of this receptor set-up point towards the inverse agonist pole. This effect can be reverted in animals by the administration of a pure antagonist as flumazenil which is supposed to restore initial receptor sensitivity. This treatment scheme has been recently proposed to prevent withdrawal symptoms in BZD chronic users. It has also recently been shown that in animals CCK antagonist, compound having anxiolytic activity, could block BZD withdrawal. Taken together this data open new directions for the understanding and the treatment of benzodiazepine dependence as well for the treatment of anxiety disorders.
