AB  - Introduction: Older adults are unlikely to receive mental health services, due to a number of barriers including cost, transportation, and stigma, as well as a lack of qualified providers to serve this population. Community-based outreach services provide a potential opportunity to address these barriers. Referral Education Assistance & Prevention (REAP) is a statewide program in New Hampshire (NH), designed in 1993, to provide outreach preventive services to older adults who are at risk for mental health concerns. REAP was designed to accept referrals from a variety of sources and has been widely promoted throughout the state via word of mouth, outreach/community education, and NH community mental health centers. The program has braided funding provided by 4 sources, which enables the program to provide brief prevention services at no cost to participants. A program director, a program coordinator, and a multi-stakeholder workgroup oversee the program. Currently, there are 40 REAP counselors across the state and 12 supervisors. All counselors are subcontracted through NH community mental health centers and hold either bachelors or masters degrees. The objective was to describe services provided by REAP counselors and participant characteristics. Method(s): Through our ongoing partnership with the REAP program, we employed methods consistent with communitypartnered research to observe programmatic guidelines and counselor practice. We conducted univariate analyses with participant data collected from 2010 to 2012. Result(s): REAP policies indicated that counselors screen all participants for depression using the Geriatric Depression Scale (Yesavage et al., 1983), and alcohol abuse using the Michigan Alcoholism Screening Test - Geriatric Version (The Regents of the University of Michigan, 1991). REAP counselors were expected to generate recommendations for referral to other services as needed, but were not authorized to provide direct referrals to providers. Counselors were expected to provide brief preventive services (up to 5 sessions) employing psychoeducation, supportive counseling, and Motivational Interviewing. REAP provided counseling services to N=1,179 individuals from 2010 to 2012. Participants were referred to REAP by: the Bureau of Elderly and Adult Services (12%), self-referral (11%), family members (10%), home health workers (10%), and other sources (57%). Regarding session utilization, 32% of participants used 1 session, 25% used 2 sessions, and the remaining 43% used 3 or more sessions. The majority of participants were over 65 years old (81%), female (63%), and white (97%). Despite the expectation that all participants would be screened, only 61% were screened for depression, and 64% were screened for alcohol abuse. Of those screened, 39% screened positive for depressive symptoms, 8% screened positive for alcohol abuse, and 5% screened positive for both. These results have prompted program modifications including improved screening procedures and the addition of brief depression and at-risk drinking interventions. Conclusion(s): In response to modest adherence to screening expectations which yielded relatively high rates of depressive symptoms and alcohol abuse, the REAP program has implemented programmatic changes. Community-based outreach programs, such as REAP, serve as potential disseminable and sustainable avenues for screening and delivering preventive services in the home and other community settings. Through provision of REAP outreach, counselors with various educational backgrounds have begun to address barriers to mental health services, thus promoting health and wellbeing among older adults.
