AB  - Introduction: Computer-based treatment could address several barriers to mental health services for older adults by providing intervention through a self-guided, computer-based program that can be completed in the comfort of one's own home or at a community site. Computer-based treatments for are effective in the general adult population but these treatments have been infrequently studied with older adult participants. Electronic Problem Solving Treatment (ePST) was designed to deliver in flight treatment for astronauts and has also been used to provide treatment for a general adult population. Problem Solving Therapy is particularly well suited for computer-delivery because it is highly structured and client driven. ePST simulates a session with a therapist who provides guidance, support, and feedback via numerous video and audio clips to engage and activate the client, teach core PST skills, and develop a therapist alliance. This program has the potential to be highly acceptable to older adults due to its interactive format and emphasis on creating the sense of a therapeutic relationship between the user and ePST. Objective(s): To report the results of usability testing of ePST with older adults to determine what modifications are needed to optimize acceptability and usability for older adults. Method(s): We employed a mixed-methods design with data gathered through usability testing, specifically collecting data as participants complete the first session of ePST and also collecting data on their impressions of the program after the session. Seven participants, aged 65 or older, who had at least some experience using computers, participated individually. We collected both observed and self-reported usability data. As participants used the program, they were audio recorded and observed by a research assistant. Additionally, the computer screen was video recorded. Participants completed questions presented in both Likert scale and open-ended question format before, during, and after using the program. De-identified data were analyzed for usability, satisfaction, acceptability, and creditability. Result(s): All participants navigated through the program without difficulty and completed the session. Overall, participants found the program highly satisfactory, acceptable, and credible. Participants reported that while engaging with the program it felt as if the ePST therapist was speaking directly to the user, which they indicated was surprising but pleasing. Additionally, participants reported that the structure of session was logical and well paced. However, there were also critiques of the program. The most consistent criticism of the program was that references to space were undesirable. Participants suggested these be removed and replaced with examples and references that were more relevant to older adults such as medical problems or isolation. Participants also noted that the program font was too small and in some cases was too crowded. Conclusion(s): Participants found this computer-based program usable and acceptable, however, areas for improvement were identified. Because the program was designed for astronauts, it targets a population of individuals who are actively working and are in good health. Older adults may have frailty, disability, chronic pain, sensory loss, and transportation concerns. We intend to adapt the original version for use with older adults to optimize usability.
