AB  - The article presents a study on the perceived effectiveness of fear appeals in AIDS education posters in 261 community residents, college students, and intravenous drug users in the U.S. The definitive framework for describing and predicting the effects of fear-inducing communications upon the adoption of preventive health behaviors is provided by protection-motivation theory. Posters portraying severe consequences of AIDS were highly-feared by the respondents. On the other hand, respondents prefer neutral posters regarding disease severity. Results revealed that high severity/fear posters were rated more effective compared to the other type of posters.
