AB  - Background: Genetic variation contributes to interindividual variability in psychotropic drug response, which may result in long trials, delayed efficacy, and poor tolerability. Pharmacogenetic testing may facilitate treatment selection by identifying individuals affected by genetic polymorphisms altering drug bioavailability, metabolism or effectiveness. However, the clinical utility of pharmacogenetic tools in psychiatry has not been evaluated through randomised clinical trials (RCTs) in populations of adequate size. Aim: We conducted an RCT (EudraCT:2013‐002228‐18) to evaluate the utility of pharmacogenetic information obtained with a pharmacogenetic platform (Neuropharmagen®) in patients with major depressive disorder (MDD). Methods: A 3‐month prospective, naturalistic, double‐blind randomized clinical trial in 316 adult patients with MDD was conducted. All patients were genotyped with the Neuropharmagen® test, randomized either to the study group (pharmacogeneticguided treatment) or the control group (treatment as usual), and evaluated at baseline, 6 and 12 weeks, and through telephone interviews at 4, 8 and 12 weeks (double‐blind evaluation of the primary variable). The primary efficacy variable was the proportion of patients self‐reporting “Much”/“Very much” improvement according to the Patient Global Impression of Improvement scale (i.e. PGI‐I ‐2), as collected by a blind observer. Additional assessments included the 17‐item Hamilton Depression Rating Scale (HDRS), Frequency, Intensity, and Burden of Side Effects Ratings (FIBSER), Sheehan Disability Inventory (SDI) and Treatment Satisfaction with Medicines Questionnaire (SATMED‐Q). Differences among groups were assessed using Chi‐squared or T‐Test, according to data type. Results: The study cohort displayed a large diversity in terms of duration of disease, number of previous treatments, depression severity and psychiatric comorbidities. In the study group, a significant difference in the rate of sustained response (starting at weeks 4 or 8) could not be observed. However, a higher proportion of patients with positive response to treatment (PGI‐I ‐2) was observed at study endpoint, i.e. 12 weeks (47.8% vs 36.1%, p = 0.0476, OR= 1.62 [95% CI 1.00‐2.61]). Patients in the study group showed a higher reduction in HDRS at 6 weeks (p = 0.0364), and a close‐to‐significant reduction at 12 weeks (p = 0.0771). Differences in PGI‐I at 12 weeks and HDRS at 6 and 12 weeks were significant and more pronounced in the subgroups of patients with baseline HDRS ≥14 (n = 254), and in those who had received 1 to 3 previous treatments (n = 190). Significant differences were detected in all additional questionnaires, except SDI. Among subjects reporting some burden of side effects at baseline (FIBSER “burden” score), the likelihood of reaching a score <3 (i.e. no general need to address side effects) was higher in study group subjects at 6 weeks (66.7% vs 50.0%, p = 0.0294, OR 2.00 [95% CI: 1.07‐3.75]). This effect was also maintained at 12 weeks (68.5% vs. 51.4%, p = 0.0260, 2.06 [95% CI: 1.09 ‐ 3.89]). Conclusions: Pharmacogenetic‐guided treatment resulted in a significant improvement in subjects of the study group and in medication tolerability in subjects with minimal to disabling side effects. The improvement was more pronounced in patients with baseline HDRS ≥14 and specially those who had received 1 to 3 previous treatments.
