AB  - Objective. Violence in a relationship has devastating effects on women's mental health. Typically it leads to elevated rates of depressive and anxiety disorders. Immigrant Latina women exposed to Intimate Partner Violence (IPV) need mental health services that are adequate to their emotional/coping needs. To date there is a scarcity of research on their perspectives and expectations of mental health service. The present qualitative cultural study examined how 32 immigrant Latina women who had been exposed to IPV perceived mental health and what they expected from mental health services. Researchers and mental health providers must develop culturally sensitive mental health services to optimally serve this growing population. Method. A qualitative analysis was done with three focus groups with 32 immigrant Latina women. Their ages ranged from 19 to 40 years, with a mean age of 30.9 years; 43.8% completed high school, and 53.1% were married. Prompted by open-ended questions, the focus groups' discussions were audio taped, and the tapes were transcribed verbatim and coded independently by four coders. Results. Women cited a range of emotional disorders caused by their IPV, and their need for culturally sensitive treatments. The study participants' most-reported complaints were trauma, depression, and stress, as their emotional problems. In particular, the women reported their need for sensitive mental health services that take into account their difficulties with differences of culture and language, financial difficulties, fear of immigration authorities if they have undocumented immigration status, fear of discrimination, and stigmatization. Conclusions. Results suggest that immigrant Latina who have experienced IPV would benefit from mental health services that are culturally sensitive, family oriented, bilingual and bicultural, free of charge, weekly therapy sessions, with providers who have some understanding of the Latino culture, and from wider availability of information. Further research. Further research is needed which assesses and compares the desired outcomes for cultural sensitivity according to both providers and immigrant Latina women, to assist mental health providers to resolve possible discrepancies. Cultural, geographic, and socioeconomic characteristics may also play a significant role in preferences for intervention outcomes. Further research will elucidate points of improved practice and effective interventions. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
