AB  - Compared the efficacy and tolerability of 2 serotonin selective reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs), citalopram (CIT) and paroxetine (PAR), for treating panic disorder (PD). 58 outpatients with PD were randomly assigned to either the PAR or CIT treatment group in a single-blind, randomized design. Each patient was assessed at days 0, 7, and 60 by the Panic Associated Symptoms Scale (PASS), the Sheehan Disability Scale (SDS) and the Fear Questionnaire (FQ). Primary outcome measures were the percentage of patients free of panic attacks, anticipatory anxiety and phobic avoidance in the last week of the trail and the percentage of good responders, as defined by a reduction of at least 50% from baseline of both PASS and SDS global scores at day 60. At day 60, 86% of patients receiving CIT and 84% of those receiving PAR responded well to treatment. No significant differences between the 2 drugs were found. Both were well tolerated, although sexual side effects and weight gain were frequent. Anticipatory anxiety decreased significantly after the first week of treatment, and no initial worsening in the panic attacks was observed. These results support evidence that the serotonergic system plays a significant role in the anti-panic properties of these 2 SSRIs. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
