AB  - In this article, the authors report on a qualitative study, which investigated, through the use of ethnographic interviews, the transmission dynamics underpinning HIV infection within a university student population in South Africa. Data collection was by means of focus group & individual interviews. Drawing on a systems perspective, the interpretation of emergent themes was informed by a social constructivist understanding of gender relations & located within Green & Kreuter's (1999) PRECEDE-PROCEED health promotion planning framework, as well as the Tavistock Clinic model of organizational change. The latter introduces psychoanalytic principles into the systems approach to community/organizational interventions. The study reveals how the dynamics underpinning HIV transmission among students at the U of Durban-Westville are a product of a number of interrelated issues, which operate at different levels within the system. For example, issues operating at the level of the individual in terms of the sociocultural construction of masculinity & femininity & the need for status are reinforced by factors operating at the social level through mechanisms of peer pressure & student culture. Furthermore, many first year students experience anxieties related to adjustment to university life that renders them vulnerable to HIV infection in the absence of a containing & supportive environment at a structural level. The implications of these findings for a comprehensive systemic university-wide intervention are discussed. 33 References. Adapted from the source document.
