AB  - ADHD is a disorder that has been found to affect approximately 2-10% of the adult population in the United States. For these individuals, ADHD impacts many areas of functioning, including, employment, academic functioning, relationships, family, personal impairments, societal impairments, driving, and comorbidity with other mental health disorders. Following a psychological evaluation for ADHD, individuals are provided with treatment recommendations aimed to address their specific symptoms and impairments. The most common treatment recommendations are medications, psychological counseling, and behavioral interventions, specifically ADHD medications, individual/family therapy, academic accommodations, tutoring, organization strategies, and support groups. Non-adherence to treatment recommendations has been found to be a significant reason for treatment failure. The present study examined adults' level of adherence to ADHD treatment recommendations in the areas of medication, psychological and behavioral interventions. This study also examined the role of demographic variables on treatment adherence. Although the sample size for this study was small, results found that more participants adhered to medication recommendations than psychological or behavioral recommendations. Results also suggested that participants experienced greater improvement with psychological recommendations compared to medication or behavioral recommendations. Regarding demographics, there was some indication that these variables have a role in adherence; however, due to the small sample size definitive conclusions could not be made. Implications of these results for understanding adult adherence to ADHD treatment recommendations are discussed. It was suggested that it would be beneficial for future research to expand on the present study by increasing the sample size, selecting participants from additional psychology clinics, examining in greater detail the reasons for participants' adherence, and gathering additional information in the questionnaire. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
