AB  - This article presents guidelines for what data should be contained in intake summaries, along with assessments, treatment plans, case notes, and other supplemental materials. Keeping adequate client records is necessary both for the client's welfare and for the protection of the counselor. The purpose of this article is to present procedures that can be used to develop a comprehensive client record. These procedures originally were developed by the senior author and have been implemented over the past 10 years in various mental health and substance abuse clinical settings, including the clinical training center operated by the Department of Counselor and Human Services Education, The University of Toledo. The guidelines consistently meet numerous state and federal requirements for record keeping, as well as standards established by the Joint Commission on Accreditation of Hospitals. The reader should note that this article is not intended to be an exhaustive consideration of the data available for inclusion in a client's record. The recommendations contained in this article reflect the authors' biases, and different agencies or practitioners may wish to include data other than that which is presented.
