AB  - Rural communities have greater mental health needs but fewer mental health resources than urban communities. As a result, rural residents are more likely to receive mental health treatment later in the course of an illness and are more likely to utilize emergency services and become hospitalized for mental health problems. This study conducted focus groups with 16 residents of northern New York to identify and understand the processes that contribute to mental health risk or well-being in rural communities. Data were analyzed using inductive thematic analysis and were organized into five main themes. Barriers to accessing support included a general lack of providers, complexities navigating the mental health system, and mental health-related stigma. Lack of effective support involved problematic responses from mental health clinicians and other staff and a lack of training or expertise among some providers. Successful support was found in informal, formal, and logistical support systems. Participants identified numerous sources of stress, particularly a lack of positive social outlets, inaccessibility of resources, and stigma and prejudice. Their responses to stress included a number of adaptive coping mechanisms, while emergency services were accessed when these efforts were inadequate. Based on this analysis, several interventions are recommended, focusing on the availability of formal and informal supports, ease of navigating mental health resources, community awareness and education about mental health, support for marginalized groups, and support for current mental health providers. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved) In a series of focus groups in northern New York, rural community members described failures to get timely and effective support, leading them to seek emergency mental health care to prevent negative mental health outcomes. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
