AB  - This study evaluated three archived data sets from both the United States and Great Britain and examined the development of posttraumatic stress disorder (PTSD) in females stalked by intimates who are defined as being married, divorced, separated, cohabitated, engaged, or serious dating. The goal of this study was to provide a clearer picture of the connection between Intimate Partner Stalking (IPS), Intimate Partner Violence (IPV), and the development of Posttraumatic Stress Disorder (PTSD). Seven multilayered hypotheses were developed to test these relationships. Statistical techniques used were (a) simple frequencies to allow for demographic comparisons, (b) the Explore function in SPSS 11.5 to examine the distributions and normality of the independent variables and the dependent variables, (c) cross-tabulations to generate the characteristics of treatment groups, and (d) multiple regression analyses, t-tests, correlational analyses, and logistic regression analyses to test the hypotheses. The samples included 187 women from Brewster's research conducted in Southeastern Pennsylvania (1991-1995), 967 women from Tjaden and Thoennes (1994-1996) National Violence Against Women Survey (NVAWS), and 903 women from the British Crime Survey (1998). Results suggested a strong link and predictive relationship between stalking, levels of intimacy, IPV, and the development of PTSD. Using the PTSD Diagnostic Symptom Scale (PDS), the Brewster archived data were recast since the data set contained variables that directly correlated to the PDS and the DSM-IV-TR diagnosis of PTSD. Results indicated the longer one was stalked, one's ethnicity, and being childless were predictive of the rates of PTSD experiences. Women's age, prior IPV experiences, low income status (SES), having sought assistance from the criminal justice system or sought counseling and/or other social services directly impacted reported numbers of PTSD symptoms. Recommendations for further research and intervention to lessen the impact of intimate stalking on the victim and the subsequent development of PTSD were offered. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
