AB  - Objective: Menopause significantly affects quality of life in women experiencing severe symptoms while transitioning. Population studies show increased absence from work among ageing employees and higher rates for women compared to men. With increasing life expectancy, age of retirement is gradually moving up to 70 years, but practicability for the female workforce raises concerns. Mild menopause complaints have been associated with a 33.8% risk of absence from work among 208 Dutch women aged 40 to 60 m a study by Geukes (2011). Awareness of the potential setback of menopause on workability is growing but little is known about specific needs of women concerned and effective policies within organizations to maintain their presence in the workplace. This study aimed to assess menopause symptoms and associations with workability of healthcare workers aged 45-60 and to explore conditions promoting their participation in the workforce Design: Between April 2018-March 2019 a total of 590 female employees from healthcare facilities for mentally disabled participated. Frequency and intensity of menopause symptoms were assessed using the Greene Climacteric Scale (GCS) a well known self report questionnaire. The Work Ability Index (WAI) served to estimate whether an employee was able to work at present and during the next 2 years, taking into account influencing factors e.g. individual health, absence during the past year and specific job demands. A third questionnaire assessed medical information, body mass index (BMI), lifestyle habits and educational background. Pearson's correlation analysis was used to detect associations between variables and the WAI. Two open questions were added as follows: 1. What should your employer do to improve your workability? 2. What can you do to improve your workability? Results: Average age was 52.8 (SD 4.49), BMI 26.0 kg/m2 (SD 7.06); 29% of participants had higher education, 15% were current smokers, 60.5% exercised more than twice a week. Average total GCS score was 10.9 (SD 6.57), with highest average scores of 5.64 (SD 3.97) for psychological subscales. Difficulty sleeping, muscle and joint pains and feeling tired or lacking energy were most frequently reported bothersome symptoms. WAI score was 39 (SD 5.24); 27% had a poor WAI score<37 and risked being absent within the next two years. Total GCS and subscale scores were significantly negatively associated with WAI scores: Pearson r-0.568 (p< 001) for WAI/total GSC. Depressive symptoms were most predictive for risk of absence: Pearson r-0.554 (p< 001). Open questions: Participants needed employers to reduce job demands, improve work environment, flexibility of schedules and quality of communication and leadership. Individual goals were optimizing lifestyle, discussing boundaries, more assertiveness and help seeking behaviour when needed Conclusion(s): This study confirmed the negative association between bothersome menopause symptoms and risk of absence from work as shown by others. A 27% risk of menopause associated absence among healthcare workers aged 45-60 was found. Suggestions to improve workability at organizational level relate to work load and environment, communication and autonomy. Specific needs symptomatic women bring to the attention of employers are more awareness of their complaints, positive support and menopause-specific education about coping at the workplace. Benefits of responsible health behaviour e.g. exercise, smoking cessation, and information about effective treatments of menopause complaints also deserve more emphasis. Whether targeted interventions based upon our findings will result m improving workability, less risk of absence and continued participation of menopausal women m the workforce needs further study.
