AB  - A symposium providing a current summary of the status of res in alcoholism from the perspectives of psychiatry, psychol, sociol & physiology, in the specific context of the Community Mental Health Res Training Program of the Soc Sci Instit of Washington U, with a Foreword by John C.Glidewell- in II Sections & 10 Chpts. I - RESEARCH IN THE ETIOLOGY OF ALCOHOLISM - presents: (_) Jackson Smith on 'Psychiatry Research in the Etiology of Alcoholism' - which deals with the problem of diagnosis; theories on etiology; the etiological signif of the early signs of alsoholism: the course of the illness: & its treatment (metabolism, physical changes, etiology of delerium tremens & treatment by interference with the metabolism of alcohol during the patient's non-drinking period). It is concluded that 'psychiatric res in the etiology of alcoholism should provide more satisfactory means for the early detection of the potential alcoholic.' (2) John W. Chotlos & John B. Deiter discuss -Psychological Considerations in the Etiology of Alcoholism' - a review of the literature for psychol'al studies relevant to the etiology of alcoholism. Studies fall into 3 categories: (a) exp's demonstrating the effects of alcohol on efficiency of psychol'al functioning, (b) exp's demonstrating the conditions under which alcohol has reinforcement qualities leading to habit formation, & (c) those attempting to isolate a typical personality pattern which predisposes the individual to alcoholic addiction. Findings from studies of alcoholism, both clinical & exp'al, surveys & logical expositions are reviewed. Exp's viewed from the standpoint of a subjective conception of alcoholism - how alcohol affects the experience of man & his total life course - are assessed & the gross S-cul milieu in which alcoholism is embedded is suggested. It is felt that a psychol of the etiology of alcoholism needs to be directed toward a study of the alcoholic himself as he lives in life & in particular needs study from the standpoint of the way in which it alters man's relationships to the world around him. Further res in level of arousal theory studies of the time structure of alcoholics is recommended. (3) Charles R. Snyder on 'A Sociological View of the Etiology of Alcoholism' - discusses the problems posed by group cliff's in alcoholism not only between the sexes but from 1 soc milieu to another. A scheme for the analysis of these cliff's is proposed, advancing that group rates of alcoholism may be thought of as resultants of the interaction of 3 sets of variables: dynamic factors (level of acute psychic tensions in the group), normative factors (norms, ideas & sentiments centering on the act of drinking embedded in the cultural traditions of diff groups), & alternative factors (culturally patterned behaviors, normal or pathological, other than excessive drinking which may serve as functional equivalents to drinking as modes of adjustment to acute psychic distress). An analysis of group alcoholism rates of the Jews & the Irish serves as an illustration. (4) 'An Overview of Psysiological Research in Alcoholism.' by Ebbe Curtis Hoff - considers possible lines of future study of alcoholism: a deeper knowledge of alcohol metabolism, the biological effects of alcohol & the fact that the action of alcohol in inhibiting the growth of certain microorganisms can be counteracted by glutamine. A brief summary of the effects of alcoholism upon performance is presented. (5) David J. Pittman on 'Interdisciplinary Considerations in Alcoholism Research' - outlines some of the problems involved in interdisciplinary studies of alcoholism: each discipline's frame of reference for viewing a res problem , terminological barriers & barriers which grow out of the perceptual observations of the alcoholic. The area of inquiry must be delimited & a logical definition of the phenomenon under investigation constructed. Crucial problems confronting alcoholism researcher are: lack of continuity in the field in terms of personnel involved & construction of new hyp's based on previous findings; the low status alcoholism res holds; & the lack of building on previous res studies & of adequate controls. II - NEW PERSPECTIVES IN ALCOHOLISM RESEARCH - reports on conference discussion groups held at the first Annual Conference on Community Mental Health at Washington U: (6) Raymond G. Hunt & George Winokur on 'Barriers to Interdisciplinary Communication' - reports that collaborative efforts by members of diff specialties growing out of interest in special problems should be pursued & places emphasis on the need for independent contribution by independent disciplines left to pursue problems of their own choosing by asking questions relevant to their own more general preoccupations & using their own historically derived methods. (7) George A. Ulett & James Vanderplas on 'Research Procedure in the Investigation of the Etiology of Alcoholism' - offers suggestions for the avoidance Df interdisciplinary res problems & presents recommendations for further study to arrive at a definition of the alcoholic: working out hyp's by exploring cliff's between alcoholic & non-alcoholic groups; longitudinal investigations of large random samples; employing an epidemiological approach & detailed experimentation on lower animals. (8) Walter L. Wilkins & Albert F. Wessen on `Studying the Social Epidemiology of Alcoholism' - proposes methods for the study of soc epidemiology & types of epidemiological studies which merit consideration: ethnic differentials in incidence, suspected high yield groups in the pop & occup'al factors relating to alcoholism. (9) 'Medico- Social Perspectives for Research in Alcoholism,' by Edwin F. Gildea & Mildred F. Kantor - recommends a more vigorous pursuit of physiological & biochemical investigations with increased attention to controls; in the psychol'al area more res with animals & studies in perception; & in the S-cul area studies of the prevalence of alcoholism in various pop's, & studies of selected pop groups & att'inal studies. (10) 'Social and Psychiatric Perspectives for Research in Alcoholism,' by Eli Robiims & Richard de Charms - discusses the usefulness of models, the exp'al production of alcoholism in animals & the need for clinical studies which attempt to separate the symptoms & consequences of drinking from the symptoms of a possible psychiatric illness associated with the excessive drinking. References to each Chpt & sketches of contributors. No index. E. Weiman.
