AB  - Background: Measuring objective resource-use quantities is important for generating valid cost estimates in economic evaluations. In the absence of acknowledged guidelines, measurement methods are often chosen based on practicality rather than methodological evidence. Furthermore, few resource-use measurement (RUM) instruments focus on the measurement of broader resource use, which is particularly relevant in the context of mental health interventions. Aims of the Study: To bridge these research gaps, ProgrammE in Costing, resource use measurement and outcome valuation for Use in multi-sectoral National and International health economic evaluAtions (PECUNIA) project set out to develop a multi-sectoral multi-national RUM instrument, the PECUNIA RUM, for the measurement of broader resource use in all relevant sectors for costing from a societal perspective in the adult population: health and social care, education, (criminal) justice, productivity losses, and informal care. In this presentation, we discuss the development and pilot testing of the PECUNIA RUM. Method(s): For the development of the PECUNIA RUM, the methodological approach was based on best practice guidelines. The development process included six steps: definition of the instrument attributes, identification of cost driving elements in each sector, review of methodological literature and development of a harmonized cross-sectorial approach, development of questionnaire modules and their subsequent harmonization. The PECUNIA RUM was reviewed in a focus group meeting with health economics experts and pilot-tested by conducting individual think-aloud interviews with former mental health service users and caregivers of mental health service users in Austria, Germany, the Netherlands and the United Kingdom. The instrument also underwent professional English editing, wording review, translatability assessment and concept elaboration to prepare the instrument for formal translation. Result(s): The PECUNIA RUM is a pen-and-paper questionnaire in the English language. It contains nine sections on resource use: place of living and overnight stays, non-residential health and social care, medication, unpaid help (informal care), education, employment and productivity, safety and justice system, out-of-pocket and other expenses, and final remarks. Its modular structure allows for adapting the instrument to a specific setting. Sixteen health economists participated in the focus group to review the draft version of the instrument. The PECUNIA RUM was subsequently pilot tested with twenty potential users. Discussion and Limitations: The PECUNIA RUM aims to standardize the collection of resource use data for multi-sectoral multinational economic evaluations across Europe. It is available free of charge for non-commercial purposes. The PECUNIA RUM can be used in conjunction with the PECUNIA Reference Unit Costs to produce comparable cost data and improve the quality and feasibility of future economic evaluations and their transferability across countries. Implications for Health Care Provision and Use: The application of the PECUNIA RUM can help gain insight into the health care provision and contribute to optimizing health care delivery in Europe. Implications for Health Policies: The use of the PECUNIA RUM can contribute to the development of cross-sectoral policies in relation to health. Implications for Further Research: The PECUNIA RUM will require further formal validity and reliability testing in different settings and population groups.
