AB  - Background: Opioid misuse is a significant public health concern, and is now the leading cause of accidental death. Among pregnant women, the rate of opioid use has been estimated at up to 21%. Maternal opioid misuse is associated with a six-fold increase in obstetric complications, such as low birth weight, toxemia, puerperal morbidity, fetal distress, and meconium aspiration. Additionally, neonatal complications are common and can include Neonatal Abstinence Syndrome, and increased risk of neonatal mortality and sudden infant death syndrome. Objective(s): To report on development and implementation of a comprehensive care program at VCU Health for pregnant and post-partum women with Opioid Use Disorders. Method(s): Planning for the clinic began in spring 2016, specialized services (e.g., group therapy) started in summer 2016, and the Starting Anew Clinic was fully implemented in summer 2017. Result(s): Development. An interdisciplinary team reviewed literature and models to date for care of the target population. Based on components of Centering Pregnancy (Rising, 1998) and an on-site support group (e.g., Svikis et al., 1998), the team collaboratively created a program that integrated substance abuse treatment and case management with ongoing prenatal care. Starting Anew provides: medication assisted treatment, health education, psychological assessment, psychological treatment, social work interventions, and care coordination. Implementation. This poster presents on the N= 65 pregnant women who have received Starting Anew services. Pregnant women were seen at the clinic for an average of 5.8 medical visits. The mean age was 28.8 (range: 18 to 39) years old. Almost one-third (30.3%) of patients were incarcerated. Conclusion(s): The Starting Anew Clinic was developed based on evidence-based practices and literature on care of pregnant women with Substance Use Disorders. Findings to-date confirm that Starting Anew services are being utilized by the target population. Future clinic objectives include implementation of a newly-funded smoking cessation intervention and expanded psychological/psychosocial assessments.
