AB  - This paper describes the results of a study exploring the attitudes of 208 women attending maternity clinics to voluntary counseling & testing during pregnancy in rural areas in southwestern Uganda. It was a qualitative study using 24 focus group discussions (FGDs), which were recorded, transcribed, & analyzed using standard computer-based qualitative techniques. Almost all women were willing in principle to take an HIV test in the event of pregnancy, & to reveal their HIV status to maternity staff. They were anxious, however, about confidentiality, & there was a widespread fear that the maternity staff might refuse to assist them when the time came to deliver if their status were known. This applied more to traditional birth attendants than to biomedical health staff. There were also rumors about medical staff intentionally killing HIV-positive patients to stem the spread of the epidemic. Women were concerned that if their husbands found out they were HIV-positive they would be blamed & separation or domestic violence might result. Although VCT during pregnancy is acceptable in principle, much will need to be done to ensure confidentiality & allay women's fears of stigmatization & discrimination during delivery. Community sensitization will be necessary & male partners will have to be involved if interventions are to be acceptable. 24 References. Adapted from the source document.
