AB  - An evaluation of an established UK program of Early Intervention in Psychosis included clarification of the program components & the underlying rationale of intervention. Investigating presumed linkages between program activities & expected outcomes allows the 'logic' or 'theory' of a program to be illuminated. This presentation will draw on the findings of a program logic exercise that was conducted in 2006 involving discussions with Early Intervention Service management groups in four different project sites. Not surprisingly, given the UK Department of Health policy implementation guidance & a demonstrated degree of consensus over essential elements of early intervention teams, this investigation found that there was essential agreement between the services about what they were aiming for overall, & how to go about it. However, responses to a question asking what indicated 'recovery' from psychosis, produced varying & contradictory responses. Different underlying program models appeared to surface & intertwine at this point, & it was evident that there was no absolutely necessary or sufficient indicator of recovery. This raised questions about the underlying rationale of the program & the meaning & implications of the term recovery in psychosis. The supportive interventions described, fit well with a model of individual empowerment & self-management of an ongoing condition; and one acknowledged indicator of recovery is awareness of the vulnerability to recurrent symptoms & how to manage them. In keeping with this, the use of the term recovery, has in practice, a relative & subjectively defined/negotiated meaning.
