AB  - Schizophrenia is a debilitating illness that affects an individual's neuropsychological and psychosocial functioning. This paper reviewed the literature demonstrating that these impairments lead to greater rates of homelessness, suicide, unemployment, and relapse. However, enhanced social support is associated with lower rates of relapse and greater quality of life. The purpose of this project was to design a psychoeducational support group for primary caregivers of individuals diagnosed with schizophrenia within the last year. An evaluation of the research provided evidence of the effectiveness of emotion-focused therapy, group therapy, support groups for caregivers of mental illness, and multi-faceted early-intervention programs for schizophrenia. Therefore, a 16-week psychoeducational emotion-based support group for primary support persons of schizophrenia was proposed and described. It aims to help people diagnosed with schizophrenia increase their level of social support necessary to create opportunities for meaningful and productive lives. The limitations and considerations of this group are considered. Copies of dissertations may be obtained by addressing your request to ProQuest, 789 E. Eisenhower Parkway, P.O. Box 1346, Ann Arbor, MI 48106-1346. Telephone 1-800-521-3042; e-mail: disspub@umi.com
