AB  - The aim of this study was to assess the similarities & differences of patients with co-existing psychiatric & substance use disorders attending treatment in either a mental health setting or a substance abuse treatment setting. A total of 129 patients were assessed, including 65 individuals from the substance abuse treatment center & 64 individuals form the mental health program. Treatment records were reviewed for diagnoses & sociodemographic data. While the two groups were highly similar with regard to age & ethnicity, there were significant differences in psychiatric profile, with the substance abuse treatment group having less severe diagnoses & no patients with schizophrenia, while the mental health treatment group had a majority of patents with schizophrenia. Other differences in the two groups, such as marital & parental status, disability status, & medical problems appeared to be directly linked with the aforementioned diagnostic profile. These data suggest important differences in characteristics of patients with comorbid disorders that appear to be dependent on the type of treatment program they attend. For the most effective management, integrated treatment programs should be aware of these differences & tailor service provision accordingly. 2 Tables, 29 References. Adapted from the source document.
