AB  - [...]quality of care is among the items it tracks in its California Health Care Almanac series, and it continues to manage the website CalQualityCare.org, which contains information on hospitals, long-term care facilities, and medical groups. In 2014 CHCF, in partnership with the Center for Care Innovations, launched the Safety Net Analytics Program (SNAP) to teach leaders at twenty community health centers and clinics to mine data in electronic health records to improve care for their patients and to better manage their organizations. In "Primary Care Providers' Views of Recent Trends in Health Care Delivery and Payment," an August 2015 issue brief reporting findings from a 2015 Commonwealth Fund/KFF survey, 50 percent of physicians and 38 percent of nurse practitioners and physician assistants "feel that the increased use of quality metrics to assess provider performance is having a negative effect on quality of care." Partnering with the Greater New York Hospital Association on many projects, the United Hospital Fund's Quality Improvement initiative aims to make New York City's health care system safer and more effective.
