AB  - Social policies for the mentally ill in GB continue to be influenced by social attitudes & Victorian hospital architecture. Policy must consider needs & resources, & deficits in the latter are compounded by an increase in psychiatric need associated with the ramifications of unemployment. Community care is an interdisciplinary service but there are hidden costs to caregivers & evidence of interaction of family response & admission to a hospital. The effectiveness of mental health programs is considered, & wide-ranging types of approaches are found to offer measurable improvements over nonintervention. Separation of hospital & community care services can lead to hidden competition & disincentives because of varied funding. Government is urged to develop further joint funding as an incentive for social services departments to support people in the community. A progressive & feasible strategy for mental health policy is outlined: (1) the commitment of government to determine medium- & long-term targets; (2) a health education program for public attitudes; (3) the improvement of interprofessional collaboration; (4) the betterment of information on types of effective intervention; (5) efforts to integrate formal & informal systems of care; & (6) the encouragement of specialization & an eclectic team approach. AA.
