AB  - Studied the efficacy and tolerability of cognitive psychotherapy in 84 male and female adults (aged 18–60 yrs) (individual treatment) and 14 male and female adults (aged 18–60 yrs) (group therapy) undergoing outpatient treatment for panic disorder with or without agoraphobia. Ss were evaluated before and after participation in individual acute cognitive psychotherapy sessions for 17 wks or group sessions for 3–10 wks. Ss also participated in consolidation sessions for 2 mo. The Disability Scale (D. V. Sheehan, 1986), the Hamilton Rating scale for Anxiety, and the Phobia Scale (I. M. Marks and A. M. Matthews, 1979) were used. The results show 60 percent of patients are in remission by the end of acute treatment phase and 91 percent after consolidation phase; that only 2 percent of patients offer true resistance to treatment; and that 5 percent dropout prior to treatment completion. Cognitive therapy is considered effective for the treatment of panic disorder with low incidence of side effects and high treatment compliance. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2017 APA, all rights reserved)
