AB  - Described a therapeutic community (TC) for psychopathic and nonpsychopathic mentally disordered male offenders at the Oak Ridge Division of the Mental Health Centre in Ontario, Canada, and evaluated the TC's effect on recidivism. The program employed radical therapeutic techniques and was humanistic, heavily peer operated, run by a charismatic leader, and somewhat coercive. The program was evaluated by comparing the postrelease criminal and violent behavior of men who participated in the program with the outcome of matched control Ss who did not participate from 1968 to 1978. Overall, the TC program had a marginally positive effect on general recidivism (59%) and no effect on violent recidivism (40%). However, whereas treated nonpsychopaths had lower recidivism rates than did their untreated counterparts, the opposite was true for psychopaths. (Dutch abstract) (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
