AB  - In a randomized clinical trial, two psychosocial interventions were evaluated for efficacy in reducing emotional distress of family caregivers for persons with dementia. One intervention was designed to build caregiver skills (CSB) to manage behavioral symptoms of dementia, which contribute to caregiver distress. An information-and-support oriented (ISO) group served as the comparison condition. The treatment protocol for each intervention included five group sessions, seven individual telephone contacts, two group booster sessions, and as-needed telephone contacts. The primary outcome measure was the Center for Epidemiological Studies Depression (CES-D) scale, collected at 0, 3, 6, 9, 12, and 18 months. Of 295 participants randomized into treatment (ISO = 141, CSB = 154), CES-D scores significantly improved (p<.05) in both intervention conditions, but there was no difference in efficacy between interventions. The findings support the efficacy of psychosocial caregiver interventions, with CSB and ISO comparable in reducing depressive symptoms. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
