AB  - The dissertation focuses on the provision of key public health services to those living with mental illness. The metabolic disease study evaluates a federally-funded integrated healthcare program aimed at reducing the weight of adults living with serious mental illness. Weight was collected at baseline and six-month follow-up from a sample of 145 adults served by a federally-funded integrated healthcare project and from a sample of 94 adults in a comparison group. At 6 month follow-up, 62.8% of intervention group participants lost weight. Participants in the intervention group lost an average of 4.25 lbs at follow-up while the comparison group did not exhibit significant weight loss. Results indicate that provision of collaborative care integrated health services was associated with weight reduction six-month post intervention. Future research should consider the specific mechanisms of collaborative care that are associated with positive health outcomes. The Hepatitis C (HCV) study aimed to investigate the feasibility of implementing a HCV testing and prevention program at an outpatient community mental health center (CMHC). We utilized psychometrically sound assessment tools and the best available blood-based assays to determine the prevalence of HCV and associated risk-taking behaviors in this population. Participants were randomized into an intervention group (which received HCV prevention messaging prior to a HCV-related knowledge test) or a control group (which received a dental health message prior to a HCV-related knowledge test). We found that 82% of patients in a representative sample of CMHC patients reported histories of medical risk for HCV. We also found that 75% of those living with mental health and addiction disorders met criteria for identification as 'high risk' for HCV infection based on prior work. Finally, we found the use of a United States Department of Health and Human Services prevention and education toolkit for HCV to be effective at increasing HCV-related knowledge in those living with mental health and addiction disorders. These findings suggest conducting public health surveillance, prevention, and testing in an outpatient CMHC can make a significant impact in reducing health disparities faced by those living with mental illness and addiction. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
