AB  - BACKGROUND Issues surrounding gender identity and preferences do not spare older adults, and will only become more prevalent in years to come. This case describes issues with family perception of a patient's transvestism, commonly referred to as crossdressing (to dress in the clothing of opposite sex). METHODS / CASE DESCRIPTION The daughter of an 83 year old man called her father's geriatrics clinic with concerns for altered behavior. She found a woman's bra in his drawer, and she has observed him dressing in women's clothing recently. She is concerned about his cognition, and a workup was requested. Upon further investigation and review, the patient has privately been a heterosexual cross dresser for many years and does not want his family to know about it. His crossdressing never involves sexual arousal (transvestic fetishism) and has never been harmful in any way. Cognitive and psychiatric screening diagnostics and an MRI showed normal results without any signs of cognitive impairment or psychiatric disturbance. His comorbidities and medications are noncontributory. No other changes in his behavior, mood, cognition, or physical symptoms have been noted. RESULTS / OUTCOMES The daughter of the patient does not feel that the behavior is harmful to anyone, and she has started counseling for herself to help her cope with her father's transvestism. The patient still wishes for his cross dressing to remain a private matter. He remains cognitively and psychiatrically intact. CONCLUSIONS / DISCUSSION Crossdressing only requires intervention if it causes distress, interferes with functioning, or involves dangerous or harmful behavior. Family perception of crossdressing can often be the only pathologic consequence. Psychotherapy and support groups, as well as patient/ family education, can be effective in helping with coping strategies.
