AB  - Purpose: Primary care providers (PCPs) are ideally positioned to improve the health of lesbian, gay, bisexual, and transgender (LGBT) adolescents (LGBTA), who experience mental health, sexual health, and substance abuse disparities. Prior evidence indicates that time, discomfort, and other barriers can prevent PCPs from learning when adolescents identify as LGBT or engage in same-sex behavior, and that many PCPs lack training on caring for LGBTA. Without adequate training, PCPs may fail to provide biomedical preventive measures such as pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) to LGBTA. In this study, we conducted interviews with PCPs and with LGBTA to gather information on the quality of care for LGBTA and on potential interventions to improve primary care for LGBTA. Method(s): The setting for the study was two Maryland primary care clinics, one that specialized in adolescent medicine and one that pro-vided general primary care in locations throughout the state. We recruited 14 LGBTA and 12 PCPs through provider referral and flyers posted in the clinics and in LGBT community centers. We conducted semi-structured interviews lasting an average of 40 minutes and had interviews transcribed verbatim. We used a phenomeno-logical framework to guide and analyze the interviews. Result(s): LGBTA participants had a mean age of 21 years (SD: 2.4), while PCPs had a mean age of 41 (SD: 3.1). Three themes emerged: Low levels of sex education among LGBTA, including on PrEP; Low levels of provider training on caring for and providing sexual risk counseling or PrEP to LGBTA; and (3) LGBTA sensitivity to PCP and clinical space about sexual orientation and gender identity. LGBTA participants specifically indicated that visual indicators of support for LGBT populations, such as rainbow flag stickers on badges or safe spaces stickers in clinic rooms, would improve their comfort discussing their sexual behavior with PCPs. One adolescent shared, "I was apprehensive to tell doctors because I don't know how they could react. Unless it's clear, like they have a rainbow flag in their office or something like that." Providers were also supportive of this idea. Both providers and patients also supported the concept of a pre-visit screening on sexual orientation, gender identity, and sexual behavior. One provider shared, "[A screening form] would definitely make it much more fruitful. I could definitely target my questions better and I could discuss prevention." Conclusion(s): There is an urgent need to improve primary care and sexual health care for LGBTA. Potential interventions to improve LGBTA primary care include visual indicators of LGBT support (e.g. safe spaces stickers), increased education on LGBT health and PrEP in medical school and through CME, and pre-visit patient screenings to assess sexual behavior and gender identity. Sources of Support: NIAID T32AI102623 and NIMH R21MH113431.
