AB  - Objectives: The objectives of this presentation are: 1) to develop a new portable CBT-based skills group for depressed LGBTQ youth in community settings; and 2) to examine the intervention's impact on depression, anxiety, and trauma symptoms. Method(s): Investigators adapted an existing CBT-based resilience curriculum customized for low-income ethnic minority adults for depressed LGBTQ adolescents, ages 12-17 years. Initial test groups provided qualitative feedback about content and format, leading investigators to produce the 8-week skills-based curriculum. Investigators then completed a larger trial with waitlisted control subjects to examine the impact on depression (Patient Health Questionnaire-9, primary outcome); anxiety (Generalized Anxiety Disorder 7-item); trauma (PTSD CheckList-Civilian Version); and substance use (CRAFFT [Car, Relax, Alone, Forget, Friends, Trouble] Screening Test). They also collected data on demographics, use of community health and LGBTQ services, attitudes about depression and homophobia, and family functioning (FAD) from the teenage participant and primary caregiver. Result(s): Initial data show a significant difference in PHQ9 depression scores from preintervention to immediate postintervention. There was also a significant difference in PHQ9 depression scores from preintervention to 6 months after the intervention. Reductions in anxiety symptoms also were observed. The curriculum was designed to address common challenges facing LGBTQ youth and to be easily delivered by nonlicensed facilitators at various community sites. Conclusion(s): A CBT-based resilience group designed specifically for sexual and gender minority youth offers a deliverable, effective tool for reaching higher-risk and underserved youth. DDD, CBT, LGBTCopyright © 2019
