AB  - Few before [Moses Mendelssohn] had articulated so precisely so many observations on stuttering. It is of course his own experience that he is describing: frequent fatigue, speaking in nonnative languages (including German), and delicate social situations arising out of his attempt to participate simultaneously in what were still in many ways two distinct Jewish and German worlds are all well-attested facts of his life. That he claims to have had the "beste Gelegenheit" to make these observations suggests that the other issues he discusses here were also part of his experience, too, but he is at pains to discuss stuttering in general rather than autobiographical terms. His observations have largely been upheld by later speech pathologists, and his explanations also anticipate many current ideas. Historian Benson Bobrick singles out Moses Mendelssohn and Erasmus Darwin for "psychologiz[ing] the problem in new ways."17 Speech pathologist Henry Freund asserts that "it was Moses Mendelssohn . . . who . . . first seems to have conceived of stuttering as being due to a psychological conflict between opposing forces" and that his views are "far advanced for his time and anticipate modern analytic and learning theories."18 More recently, speech pathologist Oliver Bloodstein has written that "virtually all theories of stuttering can be reduced to three basic ideas," and credits Mendelssohn with helping inaugurate two of them: until the nineteenth century, stuttering was primarily considered "an organic disorder consisting of the recurrent breakdown of a weak, vulnerable speech apparatus," but David Hartley, Erasmus Darwin, and Mendelssohn initiated a new approach to stuttering as "a disorder in which learning, emotions, and mental processes played the principal part."19 This led to the other two major ideas about stuttering: "Here, in embryo, was a conception of stuttering that was ultimately to lead to both the anticipatory struggle and the unconscious need hypotheses."20 Mendelssohn's theory, however, is far more elaborate than those of Hartley and Darwin, with whom Bloodstein associates him, and his psychology is based on different principles: we have already seen that he insists on a "geistige" origin for thought and action, which always remain "Wirkung[en] der Seele" rather than "blos mechanische Wirkung[en] des Körpers."21 As part of this, Mendelssohn provides a careful account of the unconscious;22 [Alexander Altmann] writes that "Mendelssohn's essay . . . introduces, with remarkable emphasis, the concept of the 'unconscious' in two senses of the term: (1) as the zero of awareness in purposive actions that, as a result of expertise, proceed almost mechanically; (2) as repressed ('dislodged') ideas that, being as yet 'unwelcome,' have been consigned to oblivion but become effective in dreams."23 (One must add that it is not just in dreams that they can become effective, but also in the collisions that give rise to stuttering.) Altmann's second sense may sound somewhat Freudian, and with good reason.24 Yet Mendelssohn does not claim that every collision of two trains of thought involves the unconscious, and even when it does, as in [Joseph Joachim Spalding]'s case, Mendelssohn interprets the results ("funfzig [Thaler] zur Heiligung des Bra-") differently than Freud presumably would have. 21. Mendelssohn, Jubiläumsausgabe 6.1:167. This corresponds to other efforts elsewhere to dissociate himself from the empiricism and associationism of British thinkers even while adapting some of their insights: Mendelssohn finds them rich in noteworthy observations but without a coherent conceptual account of them. Mendelssohn also includes a physiological dimension Hartley and Darwin do not, focusing on function localization in the brain: "allgemeine, abstrakte Begriffe," he claims, engage "blo&szlig; einen gewissen Bezirk [in dem Gehirn], eine ihnen zukommende Region, und lassen das übrige System ohne alle Thätigkeit" (Jubiläumsausgabe 6.1:178). [Joachim Gessinger] declares in Auge & Ohr that in Mendelssohn's essay "deutete sich der Versuch einer Verortung verschiedener Gehirnfunktionen in einem Gesamtsystem mentaler Verrichtungen an, ein Thema, das später von Franz Joseph Gall zu einer anatomisch-funktionalen Lokalisierungstheorie ausgebaut wurde" ( 358). See also [Michael Hagner], Homo cerebralis, 15-19. Erasmus Darwin discusses stammering in Zoonomia, vol. 1, sec. 17, ("The Catenation of Animal Motions"), sec. 1.10, and 2.10; and in Zoonomia vol. 2, Class 4 ("Diseases of Association"), Ordo 2, Genus 3, Species 1 ("Titubatio linguae") (London: J. Johnson, 1794-1796). David Hartley mentions stuttering in Observations on Man, vo1. 1, ch. 2, sec. 7, "Of Other Motions, Automatic and Voluntary" (London: S. Richardson, 1749). 39. In a footnote Mendelssohn speaks of what he explicitly calls an exception to the law of noncontradiction: he expresses the hope that "alle Völker" will one day admit "diese Ausnahme von dem allgemeinen Satze des Widerspruchs: der Fasttag des Vierten und der Fasttag des Zehnten Monats mag in Wonne und Freudentag verwandelt werden, nur liebet Wahrheit und Frieden (Zachar. 8:19)" (Jubiläumsausgabe 8:168). The biblical passage need not be understood as involving any contradiction, and Mendelssohn does not elaborate his meaning. But Mendelssohn's tone does not seem ironic to me here, and he returns to the passage in the final lines of Jerusalem: "Liebet die Wahrheit! Liebet den Frieden!" Clearly Mendelssohn finds this love important, and its effects valid even though contradictory in some way. I have no space to discuss Mendelssohn's cryptic claims in the detail they require, but possibly such love unites "alle Völker" in a "living script" that can combine in lived experience what may seem to be contradictions in thought (joy and mourning, maybe, or "alle Völker" with their historical antagonisms): the necessary basis for a pluralistic society, perhaps, with various local commitments that may make universal claims and that sometimes compete, but that are bound together by the common love of peace and truth.
