AB  - Evaluated the impact of a multi-component manualized psychosocial protocol (MMPP) treatment designed to enhance the clinical value of naltrexone (NX) for opioid dependence (OPD). Ss were 81 detoxified outpatients meeting Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders-IV (DSM-IV) criteria for OPD. Ss were inducted onto NX and assigned to a standard group (SG; monthly medical monitoring visits) or an enhanced group (EG; counseling and educational interventions 3 times per week). Measures included the Addiction Severity Index, Profile of Mood States, Symptom Check List—90-Revised, and an admission questionnaire. Ss completed pre-treatment, 6-, and 12-mo assessments in addition to urine samples (analyzed for opiod and cocaine metabolite), collected weekly during the 1st 6-mo, and a pill count conducted weekly. The results indicate the efficacy of NX as a treatment for OPD can be significantly enhanced during treatment through the use of a MMPP approach. Further, time in treatment, amount of NX taken and psychosocial services received, opioid use as measured by urinalysis, measures of affective state, and many follow-up measures were all significantly superior in the EG. However, differences achieved during treatment did not translate into superior outcomes at 6- and 12-mo follow-up with regard to opioid use. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
