AB  - Purpose: Individuals with acute substance use concerns experience difficulties with cognitive functioning, whichmay impede their ability to benefit from substance use treatment. The following study was designed to identify patterns of cognitive ability in an intensive outpatient (IOP) setting in order to inform substance use treatment delivery and improve outcomes. Method(s): A sample of 36 Veterans (86% male; Mage = 51) with alcohol and/or substance use disorders at the San Francisco Veterans Affairs Health Care System was administered the Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA) and Brief Addiction Monitor, Revised (BAM-R) at IOP enrollment. Veterans were categorized into two groups based on MoCA total score: i) 26 or more ("Normal"; n = 17), and ii) 25 or less ("Below Cutoff"; n = 19). Independent-samples t-tests were conducted to compare MoCA subscale performance between Normal and Below Cutoff groups. Bivariate correlations were run betweenMoCA cognitive scales and BAMsubscales. Resuts: The Normal group performed significantly better than the Below Cutoff group on MoCA subscales of executive functioning, attention, language, and delayed recall (all p < 0.03). There was a significant negative correlation between MoCA attention and BAM Use subscale scores (r=+/-0.34, p = 0.04), and a significant positive correlation between MoCA total score and BAMRisk Factor subscale scores (r = 0.38, p = 0.02). Conclusion(s): A large proportion of Veterans (53%) entering IOP scored below the MoCA cutoff. These low scores were driven by poor performance in domains of executive functioning, attention, language, and memory. As expected, Veterans who reported greater amounts of past 30-day substance use demonstrated poorer performance on attentional tasks. Veterans who reported greater amounts of substance use risk factors on the BAM-R displayed better cognitive functioning. Providers in intensive substance use treatment settings may want to consider bolstering programmatic efforts and materials to enhance cognition, especially in the areas found at detriment in this study. The finding that increased cognitive function was associated with reporting of more substance use risk factors warrants further exploration. One possible explanation for this relationship is that intact cognitive functionmay be associated with greater awareness of substance use risks. Overall, remediating cognition during IOPmay enhance outcomes related to cognitive functioning and result in improved treatment engagement.
