AB  - This dissertation investigated the effects of ROM (range of motion) Dance, an adaptation of Tai Chi, on symptoms of depression and anxiety and on work productivity. ROM Dance consisted of 29 slow, flowing movements performed in a group setting accompanied by soft music and the recitation of a calming poem. The study was conducted at Lakeside Center, a sheltered workshop in north-suburban Chicago, Illinois. The 23 participants had diagnoses of either mild, moderate, severe, or profound mental retardation (MR). A 16-week time-series design consisted of four weeks of baseline, four weeks of ROM Dance treatment, four weeks of control with no treatment, and concluded with four weeks of ROM Dance treatment. ROM Dance treatment consisted of group practice of the ROM Dance for 30-minute sessions five times a week. Symptoms of depression and anxiety were informant-rated on the depression and anxiety scales of the Behavior Rating Scale (BRS), a component of the Emotional Problem Scales (EPS). Work productivity was measured by comparing items produced by workers to an established workshop standard. Assessments were administered every two weeks throughout the study, at the midway point and at the end of each of the four-week treatment and control periods. A MANOVA indicated significant differences in all three dependent variables as an effect of ROM Dance. Symptoms of depression and anxiety decreased and work productivity increased at the midpoint of the first treatment period and continued throughout the duration of the study. There was no reversal of these trends during the second control period, when ROM Dance was withdrawn. This study discusses implications of the importance of ROM Dance, a movement-meditation program, and recommendations for incorporating ROM Dance into programs for adults with MR. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
