AB  - Individuals with developmental disabilities, borderline intellectual functioning, or illiteracy and a substance abuse diagnosis have been underserved in regard to standardized substance abuse treatment and psychotherapy in general. The incorporation of proven cognitive teaching strategies for presentation of information to this population is critical, because of the distinct needs of this population in terms of their ability to comprehend and internalize didactic material. With the understanding that these individuals benefit from therapy that utilizes modified individual and group psychotherapeutic techniques, the development of a relapse prevention program is indicated. It is recommended that such a program be developed that is mindful of those techniques and strategies, as well as specific relapse-prevention techniques that would maximize the benefit for this type of intervention for this population. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
