AB  - Introduction and Objectives: Paraphilic disorders are recurrent, persistent and intense sexual interests in objects or atypical activities. They occur for at least six months, with clinically significant suffering and harm in several personal areas. Frequently, paraphilias are in comorbidity with others sexual disorders, humor or personality. This subject discussion focuses on the possibility of redirect the sexual preference of an individual. We intend to review the effectiveness of group psychotherapy associated with pharmacological treatment, based on the experience of group psychotherapy for patients with paraphilic disorders in a sexual outpatient facility, and with an interdisciplinary team composed of psychiatrist and psychologist. Material(s) and Method(s): Weekly psychotherapeutic group treatment. Medication. Search on Pubmed using keywords: "Paraphilic Disorder"; "Sexual Group Therapy" and "Multidisciplinar Approach". Result(s): Most existing publications describe cases of hipersexuality and not paraphilic disorder. These disorders treatment is associated to address related issues such as adequate intimacy, self-control skills and to redirect sexual preference. The psychotherapeutic treatment in group has the differential to allow the patients to train interpersonal skills in therapeutic context and to perform a vicarious learning. Therapies in group have the advantage of helping patients to understand that their problem is not exclusivity of them. Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) reduced the paraphilic impulse, meanwhile the therapy was conducted. Conclusion(s): Paraphilic disorders are a heterogeneous group of disorders and a therapeutic challenge due to their diversity, gravity and patients' level of motivation to treatment. Combined approaches have demonstrated benefits for treatment and prevention of recurrences. Financing: NoCopyright © 2019
