KW  - Defendants
KW  - Program Development
PY  - 2005
DA  - 2005///
Y1  - 2005///
AB  - A competency to stand trial evaluations are one of, if not, the most frequently performed forensic evaluation. In 2000, Bonnie & Grisso estimated that there are 60,000 evaluations performed annually. After such an evaluation, if a defendant is adjudicated Incompetent to Proceed, they are either committed to the public mental health system or found not to meet commitment criteria. In the State of Florida, for the latter, the defendant can be placed on a conditional release program for a period not to exceed 1 year. A court order can be issued for the defendant to receive outpatient treatment designed to restore competency, and order the defendant to be available for extended evaluation (Petrila and Otto, 1996). While the court can order the defendant to treatment designed to restore competency, this rarely happens, as few if any, such programs exist in the state of Florida, or in the nation for that matter. Typically, defendants are placed on conditional release and live in the community. While they are typically court ordered for involuntary outpatient mental health treatment and receive case management, they do not receive specific treatment aimed at restoration of competency. Many defendants receive no treatment at all, despite statutory requirements. Although Florida Rules of Criminal Procedures limits the terms of conditional release to two years for misdemeanor defendants and five years for felony defendants, typically those found Incompetent to Proceed remain on conditional release for the full statutory limit, if not longer. The background of this program includes a review of the literature that summarizes the historical conceptualizations of adjudicative competence leading up to its current perspective, including relevant court rulings; as well as forensic assessment measures for competence. Legal, ethical, and cultural issues are explored related to treatment, or lack thereof, for those found Incompetent to Proceed, particularly those placed on conditional release. The development of this program follows Bonnie's theoretical model of adjudicative competence to treatment, utilizing a didactive psycho-educational approach with a combination of group and individual therapies. It is an adaptation on inpatient treatment methods as well as outpatient treatment methods for those placed on conditional release due to being found Not Guilty by Reason of Insanity, as well as this writer's professional knowledge and experience treating those 916 defendants (incompetent to proceed) committed to one of Florida's three forensic state hospitals and also those defendants who were eventually downgraded to treatment at Florida's only privately run state civil hospital. This writer believes the program will serve an important currently unfulfilled need in forensic psychology services in the State of Florida, and across the nation. The program is designed to provide treatment aimed at restoration of competency for those defendants found incompetent to proceed, but who do not meet the criteria for involuntary commitment. The primary goal of this program is to provide a competency restoration program for those defendants placed on conditional release, as a diversion measure in place of inpatient commitment, in accordance with their due process and sixth amendment constitutional rights. Program effectiveness is measured through the comparison of measures of competence abilities at the onset of program participation and referral back to the court of jurisdiction for a formal evaluation of competency, (and termination from program participation). Program development, design, and a description of services detailing all group sessions are provided. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
