Y1  - 2005///
AB  - This qualitative concept generating study of hope and mental health recovery employs a social constructionist theoretical framework. The research examines hope and recovery from the perspective of people with psychiatric disabilities, in order to contribute crucial knowledge to the emerging mental health recovery paradigm. The research analyzes data gathered from mental health service recipients in 10 focus groups conducted in 9 states. Grounded theory and constant comparative data analytic methods were used to build a set of relevant and useful concepts. Initial ideas were taken back to the field, and elaborated and revised in 11 workshops that actively involved people in recovery in knowledge-building. An 8-person Review Panel made up of consumer recovery researchers/educators also contributed to the evolution of core concepts. The lived experience of mental health consumers was contrasted with concepts drawn from the theoretical and research literature on hope. Study findings explore and map the geography of hope, define important temporal dimensions, examine existential hope versus tangible hopes, look at the interface of hope and several major passages on the journey of recovery, and describe how hope and recovery take form in the inner world, in the outer world, and in the spiritual domain. The dissertation provides a holistic model of hope, examines the disjuncture between processes of hope and recovery and the current structures and functions of formal helping systems, and calls for the development of hope-instilling social work practice. An addendum describes the stance and subjective experience of the researcher.
