AB  - Substance abuse and dependence is a problem that continues to affect numerous adolescents. This study identified characteristics of chemically dependent adolescents (as compared to non-chemically dependent adolescents) to gain a clearer understanding in the identification of this population. Fifty-two adolescents (ages 12-18) participated in this study at an outpatient community mental health center in Louisville, Kentucky. Chemically dependent adolescents (N = 21) were compared to non-chemically dependent adolescents (N = 31) on the following variables: Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders - Fourth Edition (1994) five axis diagnoses; demographic characteristics of gender, age, race, family psychiatric and substance abuse history; treatment recommendations; and behavioral, social, emotional characteristics. Chemical dependency was defined through obtaining a rating of chemically dependent versus non-chemically dependent on the Substance Abuse Subtle Screening Inventory - Adolescent form (SASSI-A). Clinicians were trained on providing appropriate Substance Abuse Subtle Screening Inventory (Adult version) and SASSI-A administration, scoring, interpretation and treatment recommendations. Behavioral, social and emotional characteristics were evaluated with the Achenbach Youth Self-Report Form. Results indicated the chemically dependent group and non-chemically dependent group did not differ in respect to age, race, gender, prevalence of family psychiatric history or recommendations for individual, family or group treatment. The chemically dependent group comprised significantly higher prevalence of diagnoses of substance-related disorders, family substance abuse history and recommendations for inpatient and substance abuse treatment. Most striking, the chemically dependent group indicated a significantly higher number of behavioral, emotional and social problems. The training procedures providing appropriate Substance Abuse Subtle Screening Inventory (Adult version) and SASSI-A administration, scoring, interpretation and treatment recommendations were also evaluated. Implications of the results, limitations of this study, and future directions are discussed. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
