AB  - Presents a new paradigm for understanding addiction to drugs, alcohol, smoking, & food. It is argued that two distinct types of addiction exist: simple, in which the individual craves the substance & experiences withdrawal when it is denied; & complex, in which the individual also relies on the substance to regulate mood & has an emotional dependence on it. It is posited that simple self-help techniques, eg, willpower & hypnosis, are effective for simple, but not complex, addiction, while intensive professional treatment programs & 12-step groups are effective for complex, but not simple, addiction. It is concluded that, by recognizing two distinct types of addiction, more effective addiction treatment strategies can be developed. 1 Photograph, 8 References. M. Nichols-Wagner
