AB  - The present research examined the association of attachment-related discourse to reported history of therapy, individual and family mental health in a large middle-class sample of expectant parents (N = 238), who volunteered to participate in a two-year longitudinal study investigating family relationships and the transition to parenthood. During the third trimester of pregnancy, first-time mothers and fathers were administered the Adult Attachment Interview (George, Kaplan, & Main, 1984), the Center for Epidemiological Studies Depression Scale (Radloff, 1977), and the Mental Health Survey. Chi-square analyses and ANOVAs were conducted to assess the relationship of AAI attachment classification to therapy and mental health variables. As expected, secure adults, though unlikely to report individual or family mental health problems, nevertheless demonstrated a willingness to seek therapeutic assistance and were generally satisfied with the treatment they received. In contrast, Dismissing adults were less likely to report a history of therapy and were less satisfied with their experience when they did seek treatment. Providing encouraging support for the efficacy of psychotherapy in the process of maintaining or achieving a secure internal working model of attachment, history of therapy was significantly related to greater coherence of discourse and to 'earned secure' attachment in adults who experienced difficult childhoods or past traumas. Insecurity of attachment was linked to a number of mental health factors. In particular, Unresolved status was significantly associated with suicidal ideation, emotional distress, substance abuse, elevated depression levels, and parental separation/divorce. Unresolved adults also frequently reported a history of individual and group therapy. Overall, the study supports previous research regarding the links between adult attachment and mental health and contributes new empirical evidence validating theoretical conceptualizations of attachment and psychotherapy in our current cultural and historical context. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
