AB  - Follow-up of people with cerebral palsy (CP) at adult age is limited because of discontinuity between paediatric and adult care. In order to investigate participation and health issues of (young) adults with CP, and to innovate transition and lifespan care, we conducted cohort studies in samples of (young) adults with CP aged 16 to 24 years (n=103), 25 to 35 years (n=54) and 25 to 45 years (n=56) in 10 rehabilitation centers and two consecutive samples of 14 young adults with a disability participated in feasibility studies of transition care. Using tests and semi-structured interviews we assessed physical impairments, peak oxygen uptake and questionnaires on transition (Rotterdam Transition Profile), and experienced problems (Canadian Occupational Performance Measure). With a questionnaire rehabilitation professionals judged innovation and feasibility of transition care. Young people with CP without severe learning disabilities showed a delayed transition into adulthood in housing, employment and sexual experience. They experienced problems in self-care (59%), productivity (52%) and leisure activities (37%). Only 40% visited a rehabilitation physician recently. After childhood utilization of rehabilitation, care decreased. Adults with CP reported impairments such as pain (59%), joint deformities (59%), and vision, speech or swallowing problems (20%). Aerobic fitness of adults with bilateral CP was 23%decreased as compared with reference values (p<0.01). Since 2 years rehabilitation centres were innovating transition and lifespan care and exchanging expertise. They developed a series of seven interventions and related e-information to improve participation. For interventions on work and lifestyle the first participants seemed to increase on their readiness for work and level of physical activity. These results demonstrate the need of lifespan care for people with CP. The generic approach to develop this care seems adaptable to other countries. Randomised trials are needed to assess the cost-effectiveness of transition care.
