AB  - There are many challenges associated with the treatment of the dually diagnosed, those that suffer from comorbid substance abuse disorders and mental illness. For example, merely defining this population can be an area of debate depending on whether those working with this population come from the mental health system or the drug and alcohol system. Chemical dependency counselors may underestimate the effects of a comorbid mental illness, while mental health practitioners may underestimate the effects or existence of a substance abuse disorder. Another challenge that is often encountered in the treatment of the dually diagnosed is the question: 'Which came first, the chicken or the egg?'. For example, is a dually diagnosed patient clinically depressed, and therefore, uses substances in an attempt to self-medicate, or are the psychiatric complaints of 'feeling depressed' a direct result of the effects of a substance abuse disorder, or even the intoxication or withdrawal of a depressant substance? It is not uncommon to find service delivery systems debating this question for quite sometime before deciding on a treatment plan, which usually involves referring the patient to the 'other' system of care, whether it is the mental health system or the drug and alcohol system. The result is usually one where dually diagnosed patients 'fall between the cracks' of these two systems of care. To help address these issues, an Integrated Approach is currently recommended. This approach views both the substance abuse disorder and the mental illness as both being equally primary disorders, each requiring treatment. This dissertation focuses on an attempted dual diagnosis group intervention utilizing an Integrated Approach at the outpatient level of care. The dual diagnosis group did not sustain after being formed, and as a result, an individual case study of a dually diagnosed Mexican male is utilized as the case presentation. Results and discussions of both the attempted group intervention and the individual case study are discussed in this clinical dissertation. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
