AB  - Mothers of children with special health care needs have more stress and mental and physical health issues than mothers of typically developing children. Maternal outcomes are known to impact children and family outcomes. Without previous work on self-care use in this population, this mixed methods participatory action research study investigated barriers to self-care as a method of coping using a survey, the Coping Health Inventory for Parents (CHIP), and interviews of mothers of disabled children as well as a focus group of social workers who work with families of children who have disabilities. Findings indicate that self-care engagement is not uniform amongst this population. Barriers to self-care were found to include a lack of family, social and community support, financial resources, the severity of the condition of the child and the mother's health. Engagement can benefit from protective factors including social supports and financial resources. Results also indicate that a positive outlook on the child's condition and the situation may serve as a beneficial method of coping. Supporting mothers of children with special needs can benefit the entire family's outcomes. Implications for social work practice include a need for awareness of self-care, coping, and known adverse effects of special needs parenting for social workers who serve children and their families, the ability of social workers to provide referrals to community resources, state and county programs to provide respite or other resources, and the ability to provide guidance on coping and self-care. The action portion of the research includes work with stakeholders to craft guides for both mothers of children with disabilities and the social workers who serve them in order to best meet their needs for self-care. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
