AB  - Studied the outcome of a 24-mo combined treatment of opiate addicts with the opiate antagonist naltrexone and a psychosocial intervention program. The intervention group consisted of 24 opiate addicts (mean age 30.5 yrs) in the Netherlands who participated in a study with a pretest–posttest experimental design involving the combined use of naltrexone and the community reinforcement approach (CRA). The reference group consisted of a methadone population of 20 Ss (mean age 29.9 yrs). Ss were administered the SCL-90, the Personality Trait Questionnaire, the Amsterdam Biographical Questionnaire, and the short form of the Groningen Intelligence Test. Among the intervention group, 58% of Ss had not relapsed at the 6-mo follow-up, and at least 55% of Ss still met the initial goal of abstinence at the 1-yr follow-up. Abstinent Ss differed significantly from relapsing Ss in personality disorders and in drug-use patterns. However, the treatment results of both subgroups were unexpectedly good. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
