AB  - A 10-point program for reducing the mental hospital pop is proposed. (1) Regulation of influx: The decision to admit an individual to a mental hospital ought not to be taken lightly. The important question to ask is whether hosp'ization is necessary or good for him. (2) Intensified drug trial with chronic in-patients: It is advisable that mental hosp's, with the exception of special res units & U dept's, should not experiment with recent admissions. On the other hand, intensive drug trials with chronic patients may eventually lead to the discharge of some of them. (3) Chronic patients in the soc workers focus: With the introduction of the Mental Health Act 1959 in the UK, the activities & responsibilities of local health authorities have greatly increased. A great deal of work formerly executed by psychiatric soc workers is now performed by mental welfare officers. Psychiatric soc workers can now afford to shift their attention from the acute admission wards on to the chronic patients. (4) Employment of patients in mental hosp's: A large N of chronic mental patients could be effectively employed by their own hosp's. This applies either to long stay patients who are well but have nowhere to go, or to those who still require some measure of shelter within the therapeutic community of a mental hosp. (5) Criteria for discharge: The following considerations matter: (a) is the patient likely to improve any further through continued stay in a mental hosp?, (b) is he likely to be a danger to himself or to others?, (c) is he soc'ly acceptable?, (d) is it possible to arrange adequate supervision & aftercare?. (6) Increase of out-patient activities: The modern trend, whereby the working time in hospital is reduced at the expense of more intensive extra-mural activities, pays dividends. Where possible the entire psychiatric team should attend the out-patient clinic simultaneously for the purpose of reducing waiting lists. (7) Expansion of domiciliary visits: The fact that a patient cannot always be seen immediately at the out-patient clinic is not a sufficient reason for his admission to a mental hosp. Domiciliary visits should aim at decreasing, not increasing the admission rate. (8) Extra-mural soc clubs: Because of their low cost & multiplicity of purpose, therapeutic soc clubs, apart from re-soc'ization, re-educ, & group therapy, lend themselves as excellent means for aftercare. (9) Provision of hostels: Although the new Act makes the provision of hostels part of the local health authority's duty, it is nevertheless up to the psychiatrists to advise & to urge their speedy availability. (10) Expansion of intra-& extramural work therapy: The adoption of work therapy on a nat'l scale would have a deep effect on rehabilitation & hence on the rate of discharge. S. Schwartz.
