AB  - [...]Grob has consistently-and for many years unfashionably-focused on the dilemma of the gravely ill, those who cannot manage in society. Growth of the federal bureaucracy with its potential to act as a shadow government, abetted by the power resident in the legislative committee system, allowed a comparatively small group of highly motivated individuals to effectively advocate an expanded federal role, first through NIMH and the Veterans' Administration (VA) hospital system, then in some ways even more expansively in the Community Mental Health Act. [...]is the irony centering on the social effectiveness of particular historical actors: in this book, for example, Felix, Lasker, and the Menningers; in Grab's studies of the nineteenth century, the parallel figures of Samuel B. Woodward, Dorothea Dix, and Thomas B. Kirkbride. [...]readers unfamiliar with the secondary literature might have benefited from a discussion of the ways in which general medicine's problems in dealing with chronic illness parallel the post-World War II history of psychiatry, in its turning away from the gravely incapacitating conditions that are the author's organizing and moral focus.
