AB  - This study assessed how to change criminal thinking and reduce criminal risk for female offenders. On national average, women represent the fastest growing demographic in the increase of crime (www.ojp.usdoj.gov). Many researchers attribute this trend to women experiencing 'thinking errors' that lead to poor judgment and anti-social behaviors, thus resulting in incarceration (Broidy, 2001; Benda, 2005; Williams, 2005). Forensic researcher Christopher Williams (2005) suggests that offenders, who are incarcerated and released without proper care and treatment, are likely to endorse the same thinking and behavioral patterns that prompted their incarceration. Yet, research has not explained how to more effectively address and treat the issue of recidivism. Choice Theory, as outlined by Dr. William Glasser (1998), is an approach to behavioral treatment which supports the view that any change in thinking patterns can create changes in behavior. This study hypothesized that Choice Theory would be ideal for modifying criminal thinking errors in female offenders, reduce their criminal behaviors, and address the internal struggles that mark their criminal choices. To test this hypothesis, participants were given the Criminal Sentiments Scale (CSS) and Level of Service Inventory - Revised (LSI-R) assessments to measure their criminal thinking patterns and level of criminal risk pre- and post- treatment. A treatment group took part in a six month Choice Theory program. Their assessment results were compared to a control group which did not receive any formal treatment. Outcomes demonstrated that Choice Theory had a positive and significant impact on reducing criminal proclivities. This research was able to illustrate how Choice Theory played an integral role in helping female offenders make better choices and live pro-socially. Copies of dissertations may be obtained by addressing your request to ProQuest, 789 E. Eisenhower Parkway, P.O. Box 1346, Ann Arbor, MI 48106-1346. Telephone 1-800-521-3042; e-mail: disspub@umi.com
