AB  - This study evaluated the effectiveness of a ten-week, pet-facilitated, supportive-expressive group therapy (PFT-SET) for individuals with Chronic Depressive Disorders (CDD) in an adult community-based outpatient mental health clinic. Sixty participants were contacted after a referral by physician, nurse, therapist, or self-response to a poster that offered a Coping with Depression Group. Fifty women and 10 men, age 18 to 70 ( M = 41.78) were assigned to either PFT-SET or supportive-expressive therapy (SET). Twenty-five female and five male were assigned to PFT-SET, while 25 female and five male were assigned to SET. All participants had a DSM-IV diagnosis of Chronic Depressive Disorder (CDD) as evidenced by a Major Depression or Dysthymic disorder present for two or more years. After initial no-shows and dropouts, 27 of the original 60 participants fully completed the study. Fifteen (all female) participated in the PFT-SET, and 12 (four male and eight female) participated in the SET. Ages ranged from 18 to 70 (M = 42.77). Each participant was exposed to ten weekly, one-hour group therapy sessions, either PFT-SET or SET. Depression, anxiety, and self-esteem were measured before and after the 10 sessions. Verbalizations and attendance were counted and measured each session. Results reflected no differences between PFT-SET and SET on depression, anxiety, or self-esteem. Although more participants initially attended PFT-SET, SET retained more participants. Participation, as noted by number of verbalizations, was significantly higher in the SET group, possible reflecting that this chronic population was more comfortable with traditional talk therapy. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
