AB  - Studied predictors of treatment response over 1 yr in a comorbidly ill group of primary care patients with panic disorder participating in a randomized effectiveness study. Patients with panic disorder were recruited from 3 primary care clinics and randomly assigned to an on-site collaborative care intervention (57 Ss), in which psychiatrists provided education, 2 visits, follow-up phone calls, and paroxetine, or to usual care by their primary care physician (58 Ss). Predictors of response at 3-mo intervals over 1 yr were determined using logistic regression analysis. Patients with consistent response over the year were significantly more likely to be White, employed, in higher income strata, and in the intervention group and had less medical comorbidity and phobia severity, fewer recent hospitalizations and emergency room visits, and higher reported physical and role functioning. The final regression model indicated that responders were more likely to be in the intervention group, be employed, and lack a recent emergency room visit. Univariate findings linking medical comorbidity and low SES with poor response, as well as multivariate findings that unemployment and recent emergency room use are the most potent predictors of poor response, have not been previously reported. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
