AB  - One out of six individuals will experience depression during their lifetimes, yet the majority of these individuals will not seek treatment, & this problem is even larger for African Americans, for whom multiple barriers to mental health treatment seeking exist. One widely recognized factor that negatively impacts treatment seeking is stigma. Efforts to develop & disseminate depression anti-stigma programs have been dominated by a biomedical model that presents depression as a medical disease. Alternately, a contextual model emphasizes the role of environmental influences. This study compared a biomedical model & a contextual model within depression anti-stigma programs with 115 community members (mostly African American) with elevated depressive symptoms. The main hypothesis was that the contextual model would be more effective at decreasing stigma. Results indicated that the biomedical model had negative effects immediately following the programs, but that neither depression anti-stigma program had a long-term impact on stigmatizing attitudes. Limitations are highlighted & future research directions based on Community-Based Participatory Research are discussed. Adapted from the source document. Reprinted by permission of Guilford Press
