AB  - The Court Clinics Program of the US Dept of Mental Health, established in 1956, is described. By 1959 there were 9 court clinics; today there are 20. They provide diagnosis & treatment in close working relationship with the court personnel. Each clinic is dependent upon its judge, who determines the number & kinds of cases that his clinic should receive. A survey was made among judges & probation officers who had clinics available to them (N =not given). All the judges in this program in interviews expressed a positive interest in their clinic, but in the clinic's opinion only 50% of the courts made full use of them. About 25% of the judges had a close working relationship with the directors & participated occasionally in the probationclinical conferences. Clinics treated about twice as many juveniles as adults & were on the average more pre-dispositional than post-dispositional. Most probation officers interviewed regarded the presence of a clinic service within the court setting as a definite asset. Most courts consult with their clinics before committing patients for observation to mental hosp's. The role of the court clinical soc worker has undergone an interesting development during the past 5 yrs. Originally it was limited to case work with parents & spouses; since then it has included soc diagnostic studies, case work treatment of se' . lected offenders, group treatment, fam therapy, intra-court liaison & liaison with community agencies. The court clinic must harmonize with the court structure. Psychiatrists have reasonably good working relationships with the judges. Clinics vary from employing only 1 psychiatrist to a number of psychiatrists & soc workers. They have developed not only effective techniques of the evaluation & treatment of offenders, but techniques for working with the faro's of juvenile & adult offenders. Their next aim is to develop closer cooperation with other community agencies & to become better known by the community at large. M. Maxfield.
