AB  - The goal of this study was to explore the emergency room (ER) experiences of individuals diagnosed with a mental illness seeking care for a non-acute medical problem. Semi-structured interviews were conducted with 10 participants diagnosed with bipolar disorder and ranging in age from 40 to 60 years old. The conditions that brought participants to the ER were in general described as non-acute and included a head wound, gastro illness, orthopedic injury, and a kidney-related condition. Grounded theory was used as the method of data collection and analysis. Several themes emerged from the data, including discrimination/stigmatization, communication with staff, impact of experiences in the ER on follow-up care, and suggestions for ER visits. Five participants reported the care they received was poor, with one participant describing a life-threatening outcome as a result of the care received. Two participants reported mixed results in the way in which they perceived the care they received in the ER setting. These participants believed their mental health diagnosis, along with their history of substance abuse, resulted in stigmatizing attitudes during the visit. The final three participants described positive experiences. These patients described a smooth process, adding that the medical treatment was helpful and they received answers to questions regarding the medical visit and follow-up treatment. The results can be used to inform future research, clinical practice, and supportive interventions for a group that has traditionally been neglected in the research. More psychiatric training for ER physicians, with a focus on stigma reduction, may be a useful intervention. Another specific suggestion for practice is having an ER advocate who can assist mental health patients in navigating the discharge process. More integrated care may also enhance the treatment of individuals diagnosed with mental illness in the ER. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2017 APA, all rights reserved)
