AB  - Background/Objectives: Deinstitutionalisation of mental health services has led to increased focus on compulsory treatment outside of hospitals. Despite a lack of evidence for their efficacy and a long standing, heated debate about surrounding ethical and clinical issues, Community Treatment Orders (CTOs), which allow for psychiatric patients to be treated under compulsion in the community, were introduced by the 2007 amendments to the Mental Health Act in England and Wales. The OCTET RCT was designed to test the new provision against existing options for supervising patients in the community. In this paper we report on the study design, baseline data and on some of the practical issues surrounding conducting such a trial. Methods: OCTET is designed as a single‐outcome randomised trial to test the hypothesis that CTOs reduce readmissions to hospital in a 12‐month follow‐up in patients subjected to CTOs compared with those receiving other treatment forms. Secondary outcomes include: number of days in psychiatric hospital, time to readmission, engagement with clinical services and loss to care, adherence to prescribed medication, satisfaction with services, and clinical and social outcomes. Patients are recruited from NHS Mental Health Trusts across England, each of which needs to give permission for the trial to recruit locally. To be eligible for a CTO, patients need to be detained under section 3 of the Mental Health Act, which means they are in hospital or on leave from hospital at the time of recruitment. For a study such as this to be lawful and feasible, the collaboration of the patient's psychiatrist and the team who will look after him or her in the community is required. Results: Just over 300 patients were recruited between November 2008 and December 2010. Study participants came from 35 different Trusts and a breakdown of their characteristics shows that they are representative for the people currently on CTOs. The full data set will be completed in December 2011 and the subsequent analysis should provide for a rigorous analysis of the efficacy of CTOs, as discussed in this paper. At this stage in the process, a number of issues have emerged as relevant to how the CTO process is unfolding and on recruitment and data collection in the RCT. These include: recent changes to NHS mental health service configuration; local interpretation of the amendments to the Mental Health Act; and the governance structures of NHS research. Discussion/Conclusions: The OCTET trial provides a unique opportunity to test the efficacy of CTOs against other available forms of community supervision in the UK, and the findings will have international application. Learning from the process of the OCTET trial, we have observed that local interpretations of the law, of the evidence based and of national guidelines impact on how a new legal regime unfolds. For example, the differing governance structures of NHS research constitute a disincentive to the conduct of large scale, national studies which are needed for generalisable findings. This works against patients' best interest and against the ability for the NHS to be leading the way internationally. Conducting an RCT in the field of Social Psychiatry is consequently exceedingly demanding, and a range of unpredictable issues can impact on such trials, which necessitates a flexible research strategy.
