AB  - This study evaluated the effects of two different six-week forgiveness group therapy programs for college women who have been wronged in a romantic relationship. Participants (N = 58) recruited from psychology classes were randomly assigned to one of the following conditions: secular, religiously-integrated, or no-treatment comparison. Both of the interventions (i.e., secular, religiously-integrated) consisted of a variety of activities, discussion topics, and homework assignments which were designed to facilitate forgiveness. Participants randomly assigned to the comparison condition received no special intervention. Participants completed a survey measuring a variety of mental health constructs on three occasions (i.e., one-week pretest, one-week posttest, six-week followup). Process data were also collected regarding participants' reactions to the program and regarding the forgiveness strategies they used. Consistent with hypotheses, participants in both treatment conditions improved significantly more than participants in the comparison condition on measures of hope, religious and existential well-being, and forgiveness (i.e., forgiveness affect, forgiveness appraisal, and forgiveness concept). Participants in the secular condition also showed significant treatment effects on measures of anger and hurt, depression, and avoidance of hope, whereas participants in the religiously-integrated condition showed treatment trends on these measures. Treatment gains were maintained at six-week follow-up. There were no treatment effects with respect to anxiety, hostility, loneliness, fear of intimacy, or forgiveness likelihood. Contrary to hypotheses, there were no differential treatment effects when directly comparing participants in the secular and the religiously-integrated conditions. Results of the process evaluation indicated that participants generally rated the programs favorably and that religiously-based forgiveness strategies were commonly used across conditions. Possible explanations for the findings and study limitations are explored. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
