AB  - Background: Depression has a high prevalence and high social costs. However, only about one‐fifth of people who experienced depressive symptoms visited the medical facility, and the patients had a feeling of high burden of psychiatric visits. Also, patients receiving treatment show an average remission rate of about one third, which means that current interventions have a low treatment efficiency. There is currently no diagnostic service and treatment that can integrate psychological, biological, and endocrinological evaluation. We want to integrate data, interpret them, and develop a biomarker for diagnosing depressive disorder. In addition, we want to create a virtual reality‐based treatment program that patients can use easily, to reduce burden on psychiatric treatment and increase treatment effect. Methods: The psychological assessment for screening was conducted on online and 24 subjects who exceeded the cut‐off score and 20 healthy controls were recruited. Diagnostic kits containing a smartphone and a headset for virtual reality based psychological evaluation, a smart band for biosignal collection, and a saliva collection tube are delivered to the subjects' homes and data were collected. 24 subjects were randomly assigned to the clinical trial group and clinical control group. The clinical trial group received virtual reality based mental health training program developed by the researchers for 4 weeks, and was assessed immediately after the end of the program. The control group was treated with medication for 4 weeks and assessed. For statistical comparison, outcome variables included depression, suicidal tendency, borderline personality trait, and degree of resilience. Results: 14 subjects in the clinical trial group completed the mental health training program and the final assessment. There were statistically significant differences in depression, borderline personality trait, and resilience score between the clinical trial group and the clinical control group, indicating that both treatment methods were effective. However, the clinical control group showed no significant decrease in the risk of suicide(p = 0.833), while the clinical trial group showed a significant decrease(p = 0.041), demonstrating the effectiveness of the training program. In the initial evaluation, no significant difference was found to be used as a biomarker when comparing salivary cortisol hormone levels, biosignals such as heart rate variability and sleep patterns in the subject group and the healthy control group. Discussion: This study demonstrates the effectiveness of the virtual reality‐based training program in depressed patients. The use of this platform may increase accessibility to diagnosis and treatment of depression in the future. Although there was no significant result for the biomarker of depression, the subscales of salivary cortisol analysis showed a tendency to be associated with depression score. Therefore, it is necessary to carry out studies with higher sensitivities in the future. Disclosure: Nothing to disclose.
