AB  - This study implemented a time-limited psychoeducational group with adolescents with physical disabilities and their families. Innovations of the group included its philosophical underpinnings in the social model of disability, the use of therapists with disabilities, a non-pathologizing orientation, and a family systems approach. The sample comprised four families, all of whom had sons with physical disabilities. The adolescent participants were between the ages of 11 and 16 and each had a different disability. The ethnically diverse families attended six discussion groups, facilitated by two professional counselors, each of whom had a physical disability. All participants completed quantitative (Acceptance of Disability Scale, Family Sense of Coherence Scale, Psychological Empowerment Scale, Adolescent Social Self-Efficacy Scale, and Adolescent Self-Perception Profile) and qualitative (Family Questionnaire) measures before and after the group program. A follow-up phone call was made one month post-group. The counselors also completed process logs. Results from the study helped clarify the issues and barriers relevant for these adolescents and their families and also highlighted the program's strengths and weaknesses. The recruitment process showed that many of these families are marginalized and isolated from each other and the disability community. Results indicated that parents of adolescents with disabilities have hopes for their children similar to those of parents of able-bodied children. Parents also expressed feelings of powerlessness and desires to shield their children from the pain of social isolation. These adolescents with physical disabilities struggled with social acceptance, particularly in groups, and depression. The program's power lay in the opportunity for these families to hear from and be heard by others who cope with similar issues. For the participants, this experience led to an increased sense of belonging to the disability community. These findings help to confirm recent research indicating that families with teens with disabilities are not wholly different from those with able-bodied teens. Finally, the findings suggest that the social model of disability, which provided the foundation of the program, is an appropriate and effective underpinning in the design of interventions for this population, as it focuses on belonging and empowerment in society. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
