AB  - Objective: To measure the impact of an online education/support group intervention on stigma, resilience, health literacy, and community participation among youth with spinal cord injury. Design(s): Pre-test/Post-test Single Group Design. Setting(s): Subjects participated remotely via computer/conference call with staff at a rehabilitation hospital. Participants (or Animals, Specimens, Cadavers): Subjects were ages 14-22 years old with SCI at any level and any gender. Exclusion criteria included Brain injury, cognitive/intellectual disability, or comorbid congenital/neurological conditions. Participants were recruited from the inpatient/outpatient population at the Shirley Ryan AbilityLab and through referrals from surrounding hospitals and community agencies. Intervention(s): Participants were instructed to watch an educational video at home followed by a 60 minute weekly guided discussion/social support conference call with participants and peer mentors. The duration was 5 weeks. Main Outcome Measure(s): Outcome measures were SCI QOL (Stigma and Resilience), RNLI, Demographic Information. Result(s): There were 4 participants in this study. The results from the RNLI and SCI-QOL subtests on resilience and stigma demonstrate no statistical significance with a paired t-test (p=0.55, p=0.09, p=0.64 respectively). Mean comparisons show trends for increased resilience, increased feelings of stigma, and decreased community participation. Participants report no change in educational, job, and sports/rec involvement, or understanding diagnosis. Two participants report taking public transportation compared to zero from initial. Two participants report no longer wanting to be involved in sports/rec compared to four at initial. All participants report seeking a job compared to three and three report knowing their desired career compared to two. Conclusion(s): Increased reports of resilience may be reflective of time spent in community during the study, but additional investigation is required before definitive conclusions may be drawn due to the limited sample size and absence of statistical significance. Author Disclosure(s): The authors have no conflicts to disclose. Key Words: Spinal Cord Injury, Youth, Quality of Life, Support GroupCopyright © 2019
