AB  - The article describes Alternatives to Aggression (ATA), a program for helping men end their aggression toward their wives or unmarried partners. Like most social problems, wife abuse appears to result from a mixture of psychological, family and social factors. There is not a consistent link between violent behavior and an approval of violence. Mental illness does not generally appear to be a factor in wife abuse. ATA consists of four ingredients: assertiveness training, systematic desensitization, cognitive restructuring and exploration of the social and personal roots of aggression. The model focuses on changing the man's cognitions and behaviors rather than intervening in the couple's system. A problem encountered in not having joint sessions is that even positive changes in the man might be resisted by family members. The number of members for each group was targeted at six, but has ranged from four to eight. Between sessions, members are assigned to complete an anger diary in which they describe an anger producing incident from the past, present or future and how they reacted or would react. Anger arousal can be lowered directly through the use of deep muscle relaxation training. Cognitive interventions are of potential benefit for eliminating aggression because of the mediating role played by cognitions in producing anger and stress. An awareness of their own victimization can increase their empathy for their partners.
