AB  - Interview & questionnaire data from 218 men with a history of partner abuse accepted for outpatient treatment at a family counseling agency, supplemented with data obtained from 107 of their female partners, suggest that personality traits & disorders among men who batter interact with the type of treatment they receive in reducing recidivism. The subjects were randomly assigned feminist-cognitive-behavioral or process-psychodynamic treatments. Results indicate that the feminist-cognitive-behavioral treatment helped reduce recidivism among men with antisocial traits, substance abuse potential, & hypomania, as they benefited from the structure of the group setting. Men with dependent traits had better success in the process-psychodynamic group treatment. It is concluded that treatments should be tailored to meet the specific needs & personalities of abusing men. 5 Tables, 1 Figure, 55 References. Adapted from the source document.
