AB  - Background: Psychomotor agitation (PMA) is a pathological condition characterized by increased arousal and elevated levels of verbal and motor activity [1]. This condition requires early identification, assessment and management in order to reduce the risk of escalation or the development of aggressive and violent behaviors [2]. About 10% of psychiatric emergencies are related to PMA [3]. Although current guidelines recommend second generation antipsychotics (SGA) as the first line treatment option, first generation antipsychotics (FGA) represents the most commonly used treatment in clinical practice [4]. Among FGAs, chlorpromazine is still a widely used option, because of its sedative effect and the ability to control aggressivity. However, clinical guidelines discourage the use of chlorpromazine as it has been associated with severe cardiovascular effects and erratic intramuscular absorption [5]. Within this frame, the aim of this study is to asses efficacy and tollerability of FGAs in the treatment of PMA. Methods: Subjects were retrospectively enrolled from the Psychiatric Inpatient Clinic of the University of Siena and from the community service inpatient unit (SPDC) at the Hospital â€œLe Scotteâ€� of Siena and then grouped based on the FGA that used to treat PMA (zuclopentixol, n= 7; promazine, n= 26; clotiapine, n= 21; chlorpromazine, n= 23). FGAs efficacy on PMA was assessed though the clinical global impressions (CGI) scale and the overt aggression scale (OAS), before and after FGAs administration. Vital signs (pulse rate, respiration rate, blood pressure, body temperature) and blood oxygen rate were measured after FGAs administration in order to assess FGAs tollerability. Also, we investigated neurological, cardiovascular and respiratory side effects after FGAs administration. Results: Of the 77 patients enrolled, 27 were diagnosed with a schizophrenia‐spectrum disorder, 28 with bipolar disorder, 7 with personality disorder, 6 with cognitive disorder, 1 with major depression and 1 with impulse control disorder. One‐way repeated measure ANOVA showed a significant decrease of OAS and CGI scores after FGAs administration but no significant difference between FGAs was detected. No patient developed serious adverse events. Mean vital signs and blood oxygen levels after FGAs administration remained within the normal range, with no significant difference among the different FGAs. Only 11,7% of subjects developed mild to moderate side effects following FGA administration: 1 subject treated with zuclopentixol developed dizziness and one mild hypotension; 1 subject on promazine and 2 subjects on clotiapine developed sedation. Of the patients treated with chlorpromazine, 4 developed sedation (mild in 3 patient and moderate in 1 patient) while only 1 subject showed cardiovascular side effects (mild hypotension). Conclusion: FGAs resulted a safe and effective option to treat PMA and aggression. Most interestingly, chlorpromazine, which has been previously associated with severe cardiovascular toxicity, did not induce any cardiovascular effect except mild hypotension in one patient. Further prospective, controlled and randomized studies with a larger sample are needed to confirm our findings.
