AB  - Introduction: Pregabalin (Lyrica) is a structural analog of GABA, similar to gabapentin. It is approved by the FDA for treatment of neuropathic pain, fibromyalgia, and adjunctive treatment of partial seizures. It is approved for the treatment of anxiety disorders in Europe. In the USA it is classified as a controlled substance and has yet to be approved for a psychiatric disorder. Preliminary studies indicate that pregabalin may have antidepressant properties when used to treat the anxiety disorders or medical disorders for which it is indicated. No published studies have addressed the use of pregabalin for treatment of Bipolar Disorder (BD). In the present open label study, not subsidized by the manufacturer, the investigators prospectively evaluated the efficacy of pregabalin as an adjunctive medication for a group of treatment refractory outpatients with BD. Method(s): Adolescent and adult outpatients in a private practice with any type of DSM-IV diagnosed BPD who were considered nonresponders or partial responders to standard medications for BD were treated with adjunctive pregabalin. The mood state at the time of initiation of pregabalin was documented and compared to the mood state after pregabalin treatment. A score of 1 to 3 on the Change Scale of the Clinical Global Impression-Bipolar Version Scale (CGI-BP) was considered a positive therapeutic response. All responders were treated with pregabalin for a minimum of two months. Demographics, substance abuse status, side effects, and adverse drug-drug interactions were documented. Result(s): Fifty-eight total patients were treated with adjunctive pregabalin. The average age was 47 (range 17-83). Forty-six (79%) were female. Twenty-four (41%) were rated as responders to pregabalin. Twelve (50%) of the responders have experienced a mood stabilizing effect for either mixed or rapid cycling symptoms; five (25%), an antimanic effect; and seven (29%), an antidepressant effect. The average dose for the responders is 72mg. The responders have taken pregabalin for an average of 12 months (range 2-18). Intolerable side effects were the most common reason for discontinuation of pregabalin in the nonresponder group (79%). Fifteen (44%) of the nonresponders discontinued because of overactivation; ten (29%), because of increased appetite and weight gain; and five (15%), due to lack of efficacy. No patient abused pregabalin (six of the total patients had a substance abuse history). All patients were taking an average of 3.3 other psychiatric medications and no adverse drug-drug interactions with pregabalin were noted. Discussion(s): This preliminary open study suggests pregabalin may be an effective adjunctive treatment for a significant number of patients with treatment resistant BD. It appears to have mood stabilizing, antimanic and antidepressant properties. The most common intolerable side effects for nonresponders were activation and increased appetite/weight. Pregabalin did not appear to cause adverse drug-drug interactions with other psychiatric medications, and it was not abused by any of the BD patients, even those with a history of substance abuse. Copyright © 2009 Blackwell Munksgaard.
