AB  - The influence of gender, ethnic, & geographic factors on risk for human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) infection among alcohol inpatients was examined through survey, review of medical data, & interview of 802 alcoholics at treatment centers in New York City & western NY. The 3.5% rate of self-reported HIV infection among the inpatients was substantially higher than that of national population samples & was similar to that of other high risk groups, eg, intravenous drug users & sexually transmitted disease clinic patients. Nonuse of condoms, injection of drugs, trading sex for drugs or money, & sexual relations with multiple nonprimary partners, were common & occurred in substantially higher frequency than in the general population. The highest levels of risk behavior were found among men, ethnic minorities, & New York City residents. 4 Tables, 41 References. Adapted from the source document.
