AB  - Elder abuse in Israel is a common phenomenon. Israeli legislation includes a number of laws that give welfare officials authority to intervene in defense of the elderly, including the Law of Legal Competence and Guardianship, 1962. Research has revealed that one of the main difficulties encountered by welfare officials in the process of initiating legal intervention for elderly persons relates to defining who is in need of defense according to the law. In addition, there are ethical, legal, and professional dilemmas, e.g., the contradiction between the need to protect the well-being of elderly persons and their right to personal autonomy. These difficulties and dilemmas arise mainly in cases of elderly people who do not have chronic or long-term mental or emotional disabilities, but are still exposed to abuse. Oftentimes, elderly persons are neither fully able to manage their own interests nor are they totally incompetent. Rather, their functioning lies between these two extremes, as many of them experience deterioration in various aspects of their health. The analysis of law and court rulings in the present article aims to propose a formula that balances between the rights of elderly persons to personal autonomy and society's responsibility to protect the elderly. An attempt is made to clarify the cases in which welfare officers should appoint a guardian for victims of elder abuse. In so doing, we seek to eliminate the obstacles that prevent the use of legal intervention, while also enabling welfare officers to use legal intervention in moderation, and in a way that conforms to the rights of elderly persons.
