AB  - Cocaine dependent Ss (mean age 35 yrs), 12 with and 15 without concurrent alcohol dependence disorder, were compared on measures of substance use, addiction severity, coping, and psychopathology taken before, during, and after a 12-session outpatient relapse prevention treatment program for cocaine dependence. Measures completed included the Addiction Severity Index, the Symptom Checklist 90-Revised, and the Coping Behaviors Inventory. At pre-treatment, the cocaine-alcohol (CA) group reported more frequent alcohol use and more severe alcohol and family/social problems compared to the cocaine-only (CO) group. By the end of treatment, both groups reported significantly fewer days of alcohol and cocaine use, with sustained reduction observed at 12 and 24 wks following treatment. On most of the addiction severity and psychiatric symptomatology scales, results indicate overall improvement as a function of time, however scores remained relatively 'worse' in the CA group. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
