AB  - The study is based on the evaluation of an initial, in-patient two to three-week detoxification and motivation treatment with a six-month-follow-up. Subsequent to this initial in-patient detoxification, 80 patients started a combined in-patient/out-patient alcoholism treatment. 30 patients entered a medication-supported out-patient therapy. 92 patients did not sign up for any form of further addiction treatment. The first part of the study considers whether the patients in the three 'treatment'-groups defined here differ with respect to socio-demographical variables, or alcoholism-specific characteristics. Counter to expectations, the groups of patients were comparable in most respects. In the second part of the study, the drinking behaviour of 24 patients of each group (matched according to predictor-variables of outcome) was studied after six months. In view of the relatively small number of cases, as well as the stringent matching procedure, only the in-patient/out-patient alcoholism treatment seemed to show a superior outcome compared to the other two modalities. However, patients without further treatment showed an outcome comparable to that of the patients of the minimal-intervention out-patient therapy group. It is possible that a merely general supportive out-patient minimal intervention does not represent a significant advantage over the 'spontaneous course' after an intensive, and qualified, detoxification and motivation treatment.
