AB  - Describes a behaviorally based parental assistance program targeted to low income and state benefit-dependent single parents. 77 mothers of children with behavior problems were assigned to either a treatment group or a waiting-list control group and completed pre- and postprogram self- and child-report measures. Findings reveal that the program significantly reduced the perceived number and intensity of Ss' child behavior problems; parental depression, inward irritability, and outward irritability; and the level of perceived child conduct problems, impulsivity, and anxiety. It significantly improved Ss' child management skills. Improvement in parental depression and irritability was maintained at 3 mo, and the reduction in child behavior problems was found at both 3- and 6-mo posttests. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
