AB  - Analyzed inpatient charts of patients enrolled in a schizophrenia clinic for analysis of substance use patterns and psychiatric hospitalizations. Ss were divided into groups based on operationally defined lifetime drug use histories. 16 Ss had used cocaine (COC) and other substances. 22 Ss had used substances excluding COC. 20 Ss had used no substances. COC-using Ss had significantly higher hospitalization rates than other substance-using or non-using Ss. COC-using Ss showed significantly higher rates of suicidal ideation after COC use compared with their own non-COC-associated hospitalizations or the other groups. The COC group also received higher neuroleptic doses by the 5th and 6th wks of hospitalization compared with their own non-COC-associated hospitalizations and with the other groups. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
