AB  - Psychiatric problems such as the subject, practice, & ideologies of psychiatric institutions are approached from the point of view that logical priority should be given to the 'global society' & its law rather than the individual, the family or the milieu. The psychopathological conflicts must be traced to their source & an order restored using the 'family sociorama' as an intermediary. Thereafter, the mental apparatus of the individual is able to be integrated under the dominance of laws of the social group. Differential social conditions for cultural transmission between successive generations of a given society are related to problems of social history; especially periodic breaks in interstructural connections at the core of the social system & the accelerating rhythm of history. Critical periods of history involve the mass occurrence of mental disturbance & the disruption of the progressive development of numerous individual biographies. This is seen as an effect of the structural hierarchy of those inherent laws which organize global social structure. At these times there is an inefficiency in normalizing 'symbolic castration' which is supposed to put the finishing touches on the oedipal drama & enable the individual to overcome his organic nature with his social nature accomplished by a rebirth of the individual which links his personal history with the history of the social group. Mental danger zones are seen where social laws weaken & a number of individuals grow up poorly socially defined & deprived of means adequate for the resolutions of contradictions inherent in human development. Beyond a certain threshhold of destructuring of the social group the growing number of failures in history is nevertheless part of the positive historical dialectic which ultimately brings about the restructuring of the entire social group based on new laws. This new coherence reduces to the level of accidents the number of transformations required for the socialization of new individuals. The psychopathological question of normalizing individuals in the lap of a given society at first appears logically inseparable from the historical question of the conditions necessary for the regulated reproduction of the entire society. In order to examine the link between mental disorders & historical crises, an analysis of socialization, psychiatry, & other institutions dealing with individuals who have strayed outside the law, & works of art peculiar to a particular historical transition should be considered. A strategic plan for interdisciplinary research is proposed which would focus on the axis of the freedom necessary for man's growing mastery over the laws that govern the development of his society & in the same way, his own development as a human being. (Tr by F. Darwin, Modified by M. Zeitlin).
