AB  - English consultant psychiatrists compared 29 patients (aged 22–62 yrs) nominated as suitable for treatment with a community treatment order with 49 matched controls (aged 23–74 yrs) not in need of compulsory treatment. The 2 groups did not differ in lifetime histories of psychiatric admissions, substance misuse, criminal charges, or dangerousness. Also, 65–72% of the Ss had a history of serious dangerousness; however, in the 12 mo before nomination, the nominated Ss were significantly less likely than the controls to have complied with psychiatric treatment, and more likely to have defaulted from follow-up. A psychiatrist's decision to recommend a patient for treatment with a community treatment order appears to depend on specific criteria related to the patient's recent past. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2018 APA, all rights reserved)
