AB  - RATIONALE: Hospitalization offers a "teachable moment" during which the risks of smoking can be highlighted, leaving smokers more receptive to cessation interventions. In July 2016, at an urban safety net hospital, we established an inpatient tobacco treatment consult (TTC) service composed of specialists who provide counseling, make recommendations for nicotine replacement therapy (NRT), and establish outpatient treatment plans. Despite alerts on the Electronic Medical Record (EMR) system, there has been incomplete adoption of this inpatient intervention, particularly for patients with other substance use disorders and psychiatric disease. We therefore sought to explore clinician and patient barriers and facilitators to adoption of inpatient tobacco treatment services. METHOD(S): We recruited a subset of hospitalized smokers with substance use disorders (n=20) and clinicians who see hospitalized smokers with substance use disorders (n=25). We developed semi-structured interview guides to probe impressions of patient-clinician communication. We audiotaped (clinicians only), transcribed, and coded interviews. Through iterative discussion, the full team reached consensus on the final themes represented in the data. RESULT(S): Virtually all clinicians acknowledged the importance of inpatient tobacco treatment. Clinicians, however, perceived that hospitalized patients with low health literacy, acute illness, other substance use disorders and psychiatric disease have little motivation to quit, and thus did not consistently offer inpatient smoking cessation treatment. Other clinician barriers to inpatient tobacco treatment included competing priorities, insufficient time, and preference for outpatient counseling. By contrast, patients reported being receptive to engaging in smoking cessation discussions and a desire to learn more about the available treatment options to help smoking cessation. Patient barriers to quitting and accepting pharmacotherapy included high levels of chronic stress, lack of knowledge and mistrust of medications. Both clinicians and patients welcomed a team-based approach to address smoking cessation during hospitalization. CONCLUSION(S): Clinicians view inpatient tobacco treatment services as an vital component of patient care, and patients also wish to explore strategies to help them quit smoking. Due to both clinician and patient barriers, hospitalization is a missed opportunity to counsel patients to quit smoking. Interventions to promote utilization of inpatient tobacco treatment services will need to address provider and patient-level barriers.
