AB  - An analysis of job-seeking patterns in terms of selected socpsychol'al characteristics, in an Introduction & 11 Chpt's. Introduction-states that the major purpose of the study is to spell out in greater detail the widely accepted findings about the job-seeking behavior of unemployed workers & to explore the relationship of soc-psychol'al & other factors. 450 bluecollar & 80 white-collar unemployed workers in Erie, Pa were interviewed from Aug-Oct, 1964. The sample was drawn from the files of the State Employment Service. In the tabulations a distinction was made between those employed workers who expected & received a call-back to their old jobs & those who were employed at new jobs. (1) History and Context of the Community's Economic Development-provides background on the econ environment of Erie County, which had been characterized by several yrs of high unemployment & then by a rather sharp recovery during 1964. (2) Socio-Economic Characteristics of Blue-Collar Workers-describes the SE characteristics of the workers in the sample (age, race, educ, Ru/Ur origins, marital status, individual income, N of dependents, nonwage income) & the relationship between some of these characteristics & the workers' re-employment status at the time they were interviewed. Information on the 1963 & 1964 unemployment experiences of the workers is included, together with current vs former wage rates of workers finding new jobs. (3) Job-Seeking Behavior of Blue-Collar Workers: Descriptive Findings-describes how soon the blue-collar workers started their search for jobs, what sources or techniques they used to find jobs, whether or not they expected to be called back to their old jobs, & how many Co's were checked in the process. Also examined are variations between M & F blue-collar workers & among skill levels. (4) Relationship of Job-Seeking Behavior to JobFinding Success-relates the following factors to the current employment status of blue-collar workers, all of whom had been unemployed during all or part of the approximately 18 months prior to the time they were interviewed: time taken to start job hunt, call-back expectations, wage expectations, jobs not applied for & job offers turned down, sources & techniques of job-seeking, extent of State Employment Service Activities in worker's job-seeking experience, N of Co's checked, & where new jobfinders first learned about their new jobs. (5) An Effective Index for Job-Seeking Techniques and a 'Reality' Test for Workers' Evaluations-analyzes the effectiveness of the techniques used by workers to find re-employment & develops an 'Effectiveness Index' that goes beyond the usual attempt to find out how re-employed workers obtained their new jobs in that it includes the job-seeking steps taken by still-unemployed workers as well. A reality test is developed to show, in terms of jobs found, how realistic various skill-levels were in evaluating & choosing the 'best way' to find a job. (6) Achievement, Motivation, Achievement Values, and Job-Interview Anxiety-attempts to determine whether explanations for (a) the job-seeking patterns of unemployed workers & (b) their job-finding success may be found in the domain of psychol. The 3 soc-psychol'al measures used are achievement motivation, achievement values, & job-interview anxiety. (7) The Re-Employment Status and Job-Seeking Behavior of Negro Blue-Collar Workers-consists of a number of comparisons between Negroes & the general sample, primarily re re-employment status & job-seeking behavior. A discussion of the econ position of Negroes in Erie is included. (8) Mobility Behavior and Attitudes of Blue-Collar Workers-discusses the mobility propensity of workers by identifying 'hard-core' immobile workers & contrasting their demographic, occup'al, & soc-psychol'al characteristics with those of other blue-collar workers. (9) White-Collar Workers' JobSeeking Behavior-reports the findings of interviews with one of the largest categories of white-collar workers in the files of the local Employment Service-office-machine operators. Since nearly 80% of this group were F's, comparisons in job-seeking behavior are presented between blue-collar & white-collar F's. (10) Manpower Development and Training Programs-analyzes the demand side of the job-seeking process, after a brief description of the structure of the pop & LF of Erie. Highlighted are the development of training in that area, the degree to which employers attempt to make manpower projections, & their recruitment methods. Data is based on personal interviews & a mail j to major employers. (11) Program and Research Implications-discusses the implications of the empirical results for programs, policies & further res dealing with the improvement of employment chances of workers. 3 Appendices: (A) Sampling Procedures, (B) Additional Data on Social-Psychological Aspects of Job-Seeking Behavior, & (C) Questions in Interview Schedule for Worker Sample. A joint name & subject Index. E. Weiman.
