AB  - Studied the changes of life quality of schizophrenic patients after family intervention and related factors. 213 schizophrenic patients in the Baoshan district, Shanghai, China, were randomly assigned into an intervention group (104 Ss) and a control group (109 Ss) in a 2-yr investigation. Ss in the intervention group received a 2-yr comprehensive family intervention, including mental hygiene education, drug instruction, family environmental intervention, and visitation or telephone counseling for coping with stressful events or avoiding relapse. The Quality of Life Questionnaire (QOL) (L. Song), the Disability Assessment Scale (DAS), and the Positive and Negative Symptoms Scale (PANSS), and the Self-Rating Anxiety Scale (SAS) were used for assessing Ss and their family members' quality of life. Ss' life quality data and rate of relapse were compared between groups. Objective and subjective life quality and the scores of the SAS, the PANSS, and the DAS were studied with Pearson's correlation analysis. Improvement of life quality of the family members and social function of schizophrenic Ss through comprehensive family intervention were discussed. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
