AB  - Limited research has been conducted exploring the criminogenic and psychosocial variables associated with treatment completion. There is a particular lack of research concerning the factors associated with early termination from, and engagement in treatment, for high risk, high need offenders. This paper explores the factors associated with treatment completion for 335 prisoners, characterised by high levels of treatment need and personality disorder, participating in a prison-based democratic therapeutic community. Psychosocial variables including cognitive, affective, behavioural and historical factors are examined to identify those facets which may ameliorate the tendency for offenders to withdraw from treatment prematurely and the impact of risk on treatment completion is explored. Conclusions are drawn regarding factors which may support the readiness for, and engagement in intervention for high risk and low risk offender populations, and the implications for treatment readiness in relation to risk are discussed. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
