AB  - Compares baseline characteristics and detoxification readmission rates (DRRs) of clients treated at outpatient acupuncture vs at short-term residential substance abuse detoxification programs. This was a retrospective cohort study using data on clients discharged from publicly funded detoxification programs in Boston between January 1993 and September 1994. Multivariate models were used to examine the effect on 6-mo DRRs of treatment at residential detoxification programs (used by 6,907 Ss) vs at outpatient acupuncture programs (used by 1,104 Ss) after adjusting for baseline differences. Notable demographic differences were found between the 2 client groups, including differences in education and prior mental health treatment. Acupuncture clients were less likely to be readmitted for detoxification within 6-mo (odds ratio 0.71, 95% confidence interval). Similar results were found when the analysis was performed on a subsample of Ss who were relatively similar in terms of baseline characteristics. Results suggest that acupuncture detoxification programs are a useful component of a substance abuse treatment system. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2017 APA, all rights reserved)
