AB  - Older adults hospitalized for nondisabling diagnoses can lose functional ability. Lack of exercise or physical activity during the acute illness and recovery may be contributory. This study evaluated whether increased exercise in hospital and afterward would shorten length of stay and improve physical and psychological function at 1 mo using a randomized controlled trial. Ss were 300 patients (mean age 78.2 yrs) with nondisabling medical and surgical diagnoses who were admitted to an acute care hospital. All patients had an expected length of stay of 5 or more days, were ambulatory before admission, and were not expected to die within 12 mo. A hospital-based general exercise program was administered to intervention patients along with encouragement to continue the program, self-administered, at home. The primary outcome was hospital length of stay. Secondary outcomes at 1 mo postdischarge included measures of physical function and other general health indicators. There was no significant difference in length of stay between treatment and control groups controlling for baseline characteristics and diagnoses. The intervention was associated with better function in instrumental activities of daily living at 1 mo but no change in perceived general health status and other measures of physical function. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
