AB  - Therapeutic interventions delivered to a number of families at the same time in a multi-family setting have been used for the last six decades in the treatment of a variety of psychiatric and medical disorders. For the last three decades, the Child and Adolescent Eating Disorders Service (CAEDS) at the Maudsley Hospital, London, has had extensive experience in treating severely ill patients with AN in an outpatient setting using Family Therapy-Anorexia (FT-AN) as the main therapeutic approach. The overarching goal of Multi-Family Therapy-Anorexia (MFT-AN) is to enhance the speed of change, enable rapid improvement of anorexic symptomatology, and secure physical safety brought about with adequate and regular weight gain as a necessary prerequisite for recovery from AN. The collaborative environment of multi-family therapy (MFT) is also an effective way of reducing the potential for the development of deleterious staff-patient relationships that can otherwise seriously hamper the progress of treatment. The delivery of the intensified four phases of FT-AN interventions in a multi-family setting helps families to overcome a sense of isolation and stigmatization. Families that are brought together in MFT share their experiences, learn by example, and create new and multiple perspectives permitting them to learn from one another. The MFT-AN group consists of five to seven families, all with a young person suffering with AN, atypical AN, or Avoidant Restrictive Food Intake Disorder (ARFID), and a therapeutic team. In MFT-AN at the Maudsley Hospital, 10 MFT days are offered over the course of nine months to one year and is described in this chapter. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
