AB  - Purpose: Among those with both lifetime PTSD and an alcohol and/or drug use disorder (A-DUD), at least one of the disorders is highly likely to be present in the past year despite almost universal treatment receipt. To better understand what may be contributing to this chronicity, we sought to characterize the clinical profiles of individuals with lifetime PTSD/A-DUD reporting one of six different treatment receipt patterns: No Treatment; Outpatient Mental Health (MH) Only; Outpatient + InpatientMH; SUD Treatment Only; SUD + Outpatient MH, and SUD + Outpatient + InpatientMH. Method(s): Six treatment receipt groups were identified via latent class analysis. Groups were compared on social stability indices, additional psychiatric comorbidities, and risk factors. Data: NESARC-III respondents who met criteria for both lifetime PTSD and A-DUD comprise the study sample (n = 1,349). Variables include lifetime and past-year diagnostic status for PTSD, AUD, and DUD, severity of PTSD and SUD (symptom counts), lifetime diagnostic status of eleven additional psychiatric disorders, suicidal ideation, suicide attempts, and indicators of social stability (e.g., past year homelessness, receipt of public assistance). Result(s): The three groups receiving some form of SUD treatment showed the greatest social instability. Those receiving SUD treatment had higher SUD severity than those receiving MH treatment only, but there was no difference in SUD severity among SUD treatment groups receiving inpatient or outpatient only. However, those receiving any inpatient treatment had greater PTSD severity than those receiving only outpatient. The two classes receiving inpatient treatment had similarly high rates of additional disorders and suicidal behaviors. The No Treatment group had the fewest comorbidities. Conclusion(s): There is marked heterogeneity among individuals with lifetime PTSD/A-DUD on key social instability factors and clinical presentation that tracks along expected lines with regards to patterns of treatment receipt. The complex clinical presentations of the majority of individuals with both lifetime PTSD and SUD and the persistence of comorbid disorders despite high rates of treatment engagement suggest that (1) interventions with demonstrated efficacy need to be widely disseminated across both addiction and mental health treatment settings, and (2) further research unpacking the complex underpinnings of these comorbidities may help guide treatment selection.
