AB  - Examined the use of coping skills training as a way to treat demoralization of parents with children who have severe handicaps. 36 parents of school-aged children (aged 4–16 yrs) with severe handicaps were randomly assigned to a treatment group and a waiting list control group. The intervention consisted of a series of 8 classes on self-monitoring, progressive muscle relaxation, use of relaxation as an active coping skill, and cognitive reframing. The treatment group improved significantly on measures of depression and anxiety. Results suggest that a psychoeducational approach to coping skills offered in the context of a support group can be an effective means of assisting parents. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
