AB  - Used the Learning Opportunities Coordination assessment scale to determine which environmental and managerial factors affected the social and personal development of 248 residents with mental handicaps living in 2 private sector and 10 public sector group homes. Management practices at 11 of the group homes assumed total control over basic individual life and leisure experiences such as cooking, cleaning, gardening, decorating, financial experience, and freedom of movement, thereby limiting the personal development and normalization of adults with mental handicaps. Results emphasized the overwhelming isolation and lack of community integration among the residents in the 11 group homes. One group home in the private sector, however, displayed a high level of community integration among 12 residents who, within 3 yrs, had been transformed from severely handicapped, institutionalized patients into useful community members, many of whom held jobs. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
