AB  - Background/Purpose: It is well documented, that women undergoing endocrine therapy for breast cancer (BC) experience multiple side effects. Younger women in particular are more vulnerable to hormonal changes and may be at greater risk of sexual and mental health issues. A significant number of women are interested in receiving care for these issues, however fewer than 20% get help. Furthermore, few cancer centers, have specific referral sources to address both sexual and mental health needs and unfortunately, these topics are not typically addressed in routine cancer care. To minimize sexual and mental health disparities in cancer care, we went to the community and brought BC survivors an educational pop-up series entitled: "Dare to Be Aware: My Mind, My Body." Methods: We developed a 3-h interactive workshop to explore sexual and mental health in young BC patients. The goals of the program were to 1) highlight the younger generation of those affected by BC; and 2) provide a safe place to discuss topics that are not typically addressed in cancer care. Result(s): The workshop was led by two expert clinicians (clinical psychologist/sex therapist and oncology advanced practice nurse) at three different community cancer support centers. Each workshop was limited to 15 participants to allow for small group discussion. The workshop included: 1) formal lectures pertaining to common sexual (body image, low libido, vaginal dryness and pain) and emotional (depression, anxiety, PTSD) sequelae of BC treatment and basic sex therapy (psychosexual education, libido enhancing) and medical interventions (pelvic health); 2) small group discussions to address knowledge and concerns associated with BC treatment; and 3) Q and A panel. Conclusions and Implications: The interactive workshops were well received, easy to implement, and generated candid and informative discussions. Community programs provide meaningful educational opportunities to address both sexual and mental health issues and serve to reduce disparities in cancer care.
