AB  - Background: The prevalence of older people with cognitive decline is increasing with the substantial, worldwide growth in the aging population. Cognitive impairment places older adults at high risk of functional disability in their dailylife activities and affects their quality of life. Study Aim: The aim of the research was to examine the effect of Tai Chi on cognitive functions, instrumental activities of daily living, and health-related quality of life (HRQOL) in community-dwelling older people with mild cognitive impairment (MCI) in Hong Kong. Design & Method: The study adopted a multi-site nonequivalent control-group pretest-posttest design. The study population consisted of 160 communitydwelling older people, aged 60 or above, with MCI. Four community elderly centers participated in the study; two centers were assigned for Tai Chi intervention and the other two centers were recruited for control. The intervention group (n=80) received training in the Yang-style simple form of Tai Chi, at a frequency of two lessons per week for 16 weeks. Each lesson lasted for 1 hour. The control group (n=80) had no treatment regime and joined different recreational activity groups in community centers as usual within the study period. Outcome measures included measures of global cognitive status, instrumental ADL and HRQOL. The Chinese version of the Mini-Mental State Examination (CMMSE) was used for global cognitive assessment. The Hong Kong Chinese version of Lawton's Instrumental Activities of Daily Living (IADL-CV) assessed the participants' instrumental ADL levels and the Chinese version of the Short Form-12 Health Survey-Standard 1 (SF-12) was employed to assess the participants' perceived health status. General Estimating Equations (GEE) was used to examine each of the outcome variables between the two groups across the two study time points (the baseline and at the end of study). Result: Data collection was completed on September 2016. 74 participants in the intervention group and 71 participants in the control group completed the study. The attrition rate was 7.5% and 11.25% for the intervention group and the control group respectively. No adverse events occurred during the Tai Chi training period. With adjustments for differences in age, education, marital status and living condition between the study groups, the findings revealed that the participants in the intervention group scored significantly better in the CMMSE test (p = 0.001), the instrumental ADL questionnaire (p = 0.004), the physical health component (PCS) (p = 0.036), the mental health component (MCS) (p = 0.014), as well as several subscales of SF-12, namely, the role-physical (RP) (p = 0.044), the bodily pain (BP) (p < 0.001) and the vitality (VT) (p =0.004) subscales, compared to participants in the control group. Conclusion: The research findings have provided empirical evidence to inform health care professionals about promoting Tai Chi in community settings, as it represents an affordable and safe strategy to enhance cognitive health and psychosocial wellbeing in older people. In the future, more rigorous study designs such as randomized control trials with larger sample sizes and longer interventional periods are suggested. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
