AB  - Recently, the issues of loneliness and social isolation among older people living in the community have been highlighted through television programmes, publications and campaign groups. Prevalence is age-related and is of most concern in the 'oldest old' group, where numbers are set to rise in the coming years. There is evidence to link a number of environmental, psychosocial and biomedical factors to loneliness and social isolation; research suggests that many of these are amenable to intervention in order to improve older people's lives. Initiatives largely include one-to-one interventions; group activities; or community engagement approaches. Community nurses are in a position to effect some of these changes in their areas of work.
