AB  - Investigated the efficacy of an outpatient cognitive behavioral group program for the rehabilitation of 34 22–67 yr old patients with chronic pain. The program aimed to increase Ss' skills for coping with chronic pain and its social, emotional, and physical consequences. Measures of pain intensity, mood, coping skills, physical disability, and medication consumption were obtained using the McGill Pain Questionnaire, the State-Trait Anxiety Inventory, and the Self-Rating Depression Scale. Significant improvements in mood, coping skills, physical disability, and analgesic consumption were found despite less clear-cut improvement in ratings of pain intensity. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
