PY  - 1987
DA  - 1987///
Y1  - 1987///
AB  - Many problems that the elderly experience may be defined in terms of loss of control over their environment. Loss of control has been found to be associated with impaired physical & mental health, decreased personal & social well-being, & increased rates of mortality. Conversely, the ability to successfully manipulate important aspects of the environment has been found to be associated with improved health & well-being. A further examination of this relationship is presented in a study of 63 residents at 3 retirement communities in Ohio, randomly selected & contacted by program administrators, & then randomly assigned to experimental (N = 29) & control conditions (N = 34). Married couples were assigned in pairs. Average age of the residents was 78.3, with 15 years of formal education, & an approximate annual income of $14,000. Forty-five (71%) were F; all were Caucasian; & 50 (79%) had had professional or white-collar jobs prior to retirement (or spouses in such occupations). A pretest-posttest control group design was employed. Participants in the experimental group received control enhancing interventions, consisting of biweekly educational classes for 10 weeks, 2 professional conferences, & triweekly physical fitness classes for 20 weeks. These interventions were designed to assist experimental group Ss to more adequately master their environment. Administrators were instructed to engage members of the control group in a variety of noncontrol-related group activities, including discussions of current events, card playing, & other scheduled activities at the respective facilities for a period of time equal to that of those in the treatment group. Baseline measures were gathered at the beginning of the study for all participants & the same measures collected 20 weeks later. Participants completed a self-administered, 35-item Likert-type index, which measured their level of personal control. They also completed an 82-item factor scale that measured their overall level of functioning or well-being. Self-reports regarding the number of times medical care was sought, number of days ill, number of days hospitalized, & amount of money spent for health care were also gathered. Finally, professional staff measured the muscular strength & flexibility of Ss, using the Krause-Weber Fitness Test. Pretest & posttest mean gain scores were calculated for all major variables & were examined, using an analysis of covariance design. By the conclusion of the study period, individuals who had received the control-enhancing interventions experienced significantly greater improvement in their level of perceived control than members of the control group. Although all other medical indicators were in the intended direction, no significant differences were found. The results suggest that declines in perceived control & well-being usually attributed to advancing age may actually be produced by environmental forces, the reaction of people to them, or both. Therefore, it appears possible to alter the perceptions people have regarding environmental factors or forces & to increase levels of control & well-being.
