AB  - People with mental retardation are less likely then people of normal intelligence to receive adequate psychotherapy services. Although adults with mild and moderate mental retardation experience the same types of emotional disturbance and have higher rates of emotional disturbance then the general population, they have been underserved by clinical psychologists. Among the many barriers to adequate services are clinical psychologists lack of training and negative attitudes about people with mental retardation. In this study 750 clinical psychologists were solicited by mail. Participants completed a questionnaire about clinical training, read a brief clinical vignette and rated their agreement with 17 attitude statements related to the use of psychotherapy for treating the client described in the vignette. A multigroup experimental design was utilized to determine the effect of client intellectual level (normal, borderline, mild or moderate mental retardation) on psychologists attitudes about the use of psychotherapy independent of symptoms and demographic characteristics. Results of this study indicate that although clinical psychologists have generally positive attitudes regarding the use of psychotherapy with people with mental retardation, attitudes towards the use of individual and group psychotherapy are significantly less positive for clients with moderate mental retardation. In addition, the results indicate that attitudes about the usefulness of psychotherapy for specific problem areas and attitudes about the client's ability to benefit from psychotherapy are significantly lower for the clients with moderate mental retardation. Overall, psychologists' attitudes were less favorable towards clients with lower levels of intellectual functioning confirming a negative response bias. The results of this study suggest that while clinical psychologists have fairly positive attitudes towards the use of psychotherapy for people with mental retardation, they have 