AB  - One hundred residents were asked to complete the Hostility and Direction of Hostility Questionnaire (HDHQ) at the time of their admission to the Ley Community in Oxfordshire, U.K. The Community is a fifty-eight bedded residential therapeutic centre for the treatment of severe drug and/or alcohol addiction. It derives its principles from the U.S.A. Synanon concept whereby change is encouraged through a hierarchical work structure combined with intense peer group involvement and group therapy among abstinent former drug users. Follow-up was obtained from 68 residents six months later. The group average score for total hostility on admission was 29.3 (s.d. 8.0) which showed a highly significant decrease (p<0.001) to 17.5 (s.d. 7.8) at follow-up. All the five HDHQ subscales also showed significant reductions over time. Females presented with higher total hostility scores than males on admission and their group hostility score showed a greater reduction at retest. The total hostility scores recorded from an untreated comparison group showed no significant statistical change over time. The results indicate that significant improvement in feelings of hostility took place during the first six months of treatment and, when compared to the scores of an untreated comparison group, suggest that the positive changes would not have been observed in the absence of therapy. © The Authors.
