AB  - Examined the treatment outcome of a substance abuse program for homeless mothers with young children in an 18-mo follow-up study. 149 Ss (mean age 29.8 yrs) were assigned to either a residential or nonresidential service center. Ss were interviewed at baseline and 6, 12, and 18 mo follow-ups to assess addiction severity, mental disorders, and housing patterns. Demographic data were also collected. Intervention involved group and individual treatments in a therapeutic community setting. Results show that although dropout rates were high, residential Ss had a lower dropout rate than nonresidential Ss. Ss in both groups improved in alcohol and drug problems and housing stability, regardless of the time spent in the program. These findings indicate that Ss successfully engage in treatment programs with provisions of placement in addition to participation in a therapeutic community. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
