AB  - Research Objectives: To investigate changes in depression, anxiety and quality of life (QOL) in a Hispanic, primarily immigrant and monolingual Spanish-speaking population at one-year post-surgery for intractable epilepsy.DesignRetrospective study. Individuals' pre-surgical depression, anxiety and QOL scores were compared with their one-year post-surgical scores. Established classifications of depression and anxiety severity (i.e., minimal, mild, moderate or severe) were used to analyze incidence of clinical depression and anxiety at one-year post-surgery. Setting(s): A comprehensive epilepsy treatment center in an urban, public health setting in Los Angeles, California. Participant(s): Adult, Hispanic, primarily immigrant, Spanish-speaking patients (n=47) undergoing surgery for intractable epilepsy between 2008 and 2014, and who completed pre-surgical and one-year post-surgical neuropsychological evaluation were included. Intervention(s): Resective surgery for intractable epilepsy.Main Outcome Measure(s)Beck Depression Inventory-II (BDI-II), Beck Anxiety Inventory (BAI) and the Quality of Life in Epilepsy-31 (QOLIE-31). Result(s): Paired sample t-tests revealed significant improvements at or beyond, the p <.05 level, on BDI-II, BAI and all QOLIE-31 subscale scores one-year post-surgery, when compared to pre-surgery. McNemar chi-square analyses revealed that the incidence of clinical depression (p =.006) and anxiety (p =.007) was significantly less frequent at 1-year post-surgery. Improvements were robust and unaffected by gender, education, or surgical hemisphere. Effect sizes were medium to large on the BDI-II and majority QOLIE-31 subscales, with small effects on the BAI and remaining QOLIE-31 subscales.Conclusion/DiscussionThis study documents the benefits of epilepsy surgery on depression, anxiety and QOL in a Hispanic, primarily immigrant, Spanish-speaking population of people with epilepsy (PWE) in the southwestern US. These results are useful for educating this unique and under-served population about the possible benefits of surgery for intractable epilepsy and can enhance pre-surgical counseling.
