AB  - At any age, smoking cessation can be very challenging, especially in the case of older adults who have been chronic smokers for decades. It is estimated that 10.5% of adults who are 65 years and older are chronic smokers, and in general, relapse rates for those who try to quit remain high. Dr. Lantz discusses the effects of habitual cigarette smoking among older adults, the value of support groups, counseling, and educational programs, and suggests possible methods for effective treatment. She emphasizes the importance of the role of the physician in this process.
