AB  - This study was a trial of an intervention program aimed to improve parental self-efficacy in the management of problem behaviors associated with Asperger syndrome. The intervention was compared across 2 formats, a 1-day workshop and 6 individual sessions, and also with a non-intervention control group. The participants included 45 mothers and 44 fathers of 6-12 yr old children diagnosed with Asperger syndrome. The results indicated that, compared with the control group, parents in both intervention groups reported fewer problem behaviors and increased self-efficacy following the interventions, at both 4 wks' and 3 mo follow-up. The results also showed a difference in self-efficacy between mothers and fathers, with mothers reporting a significantly greater increase in self-efficacy following intervention than fathers. There was no significant difference between the workshop format and the individual sessions. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
