AB  - Objectives: The goal of this session is to evaluate current practices of mental health and substance use disorder screening and interventions offered in Colorado's school-based health centers. Method(s): A 46-question electronic survey was disseminated through REDCap to the clinic managers or designees at 47 Colorado school-based health centers (SBHC). De-identified responses were pooled for descriptive analyses. Result(s): Of the 34 respondents (72% response rate), 92 percent of SBHC have a mental health clinician onsite: 1) master's-level therapist or counselor (79%); 2) bachelor's-level school counselor (8%); or 3) dual-trained substance use counselor (9%). The majority of SBHC (75%) screened all students who receive any SBHC services for mental health disorders, and 12 percent reported universal screening through the school. Validated mental health questionnaires (Patient Health Questionnaire [PHQ]-9, Generalized Anxiety Disorder) are the most commonly used screeners (83%), followed by clinician interview (17%). Standard screening includes depressive disorders (83%) and anxiety disorders (75%) and less commonly for ADHD (42%), PTSD (17%), and social anxiety (29%). SBHC mental health treatment options include individual therapy (71%), psychiatric medications (46%), group therapy (54%), case management (54%), and family therapy (46%). Similar to mental health screening, 73 percent of SBHC report some form of substance use disorder screening of all students who receive clinic services and 13 percent of schools report conducting universal screening throughout the school. Substance-specific screening measures (86%) and clinician interviews (14%) are most commonly used. Marijuana is the most commonly included substance (100%), followed by alcohol and tobacco (95%). Approximately two-thirds of SBHC offer Screening, Brief Intervention, and Referral to Treatment (SBIRT; 69%) or brief intervention (69%) and referral to a community-based treatment provider (78%). SBHC offer drug-specific counseling (35%) and pharmacotherapy for substance use disorders (9%). Conclusion(s): Even among the best equipped schools in the state, survey data indicate gaps in practices, most notably the lack of protocol for handling positive mental health and substance use disorder screenings, training in substance use disorders screening and treatment options, and school-based treatment options for mental health and substance use disorders. SC, SUD, PRECopyright © 2019
