AB  - Aims: To analyse the statistical report on outpatient treatment centres, which since 1980 has served several purposes: documentation of the characteristics of treatment centres, their patients, and the treatment processes used; treatment evaluation; quality assurance; analyses of trends; and the provision of a practical tool. Method(s): The statistics for 2006 are based on data from 267,496 clients (2005: 265,245 clients) in 743 outpatient treatment centres (2005: 749 treatment centres). The aggregated data on patients, treatment processes, and treatment outcomes were analysed according to two standardised data sets (the German core data sets). Result(s): Compared to 2005, the proportion of clients entering treatment for the first time decreased in all client groups. Most of the clients (55 %; 2005:54 %) obtained help because of an alcohol-use disorder (alcohol dependence or harmful use according to ICD-10 criteria). Disorders related to other substance use were treated as follows: opiates: 21 %, 2005: 22 %; cannabis: 13 %, 2005: 13 %; cocaine: 3 %, 2005: %; stimulants: 3 %, 2005: 2 %. There was a higher percentage of clients with opiate dependence than with opiate abuse (21 % vs. 3 %; 2005: 23 % vs. 4 %). However, the percentage of clients with a primary diagnosis of cannabis abuse was higher than with a primary diagnosis of cannabis dependence (2006: 28 % vs. 10 %; 2005: 31 % vs. 9 %). 42 % (2005: 43 %) of the alcohol clients, 24 % (2005: 23 %) of the opiate clients, and 44 % (2005: 45 %) of the cannabis clients completed their treatment as planned. Among those who complete their treatment as planned, a positive treatment outcome was achieved by 84% of the clients with an alcohol-use disorder, 71 % of those with an opiate-use disorder, and 80 % of those with a cannabis-use disorder; these percentages are identical to those for 2005. Conclusion(s): The statistical results are highly consistent across the two years with respect to clients' diagnoses, sociodemographic characteristics, and treatment outcomes.
