AB  - Background: Access to communication is a human right. For the individual with aphasia due to stroke, access to communication with family members, health care professionals and community service providers is limited due to the individual's difficulty speaking, understanding what is said, reading and writing. Training communication partners in the individual's social environment builds "communication ramps" that provide individuals with aphasia access to communicative activities and increased life participation. Existing interventions for families of people with aphasia include education, training and counseling programs. However, the literature still represents the speech-language pathologist as the expert in a directive role. Method(s): This presentation describes an innovative learner-centered communication training program for couples with aphasia affiliated with the March of Dimes Canada-Aphasia and Communication Disabilities Program (MODC-ACDP). Both one-on-one and semi-group formats have been studied previously. Results included more comprehensive outcomes than traditional programs. Positive changes were observed in communication as well as in emotions and marital relationships. By incorporating a well established adult learning model and making the learning process explicit, the adult learning approach teaches families a process for learning that can be used to develop creative solutions to communication challenges with, and eventually, without the speech language pathologist's direct involvement. Result(s): This presentation will highlight the results of a recent workshop format of the original learner-centered communication training program. This included a one day workshop and half day follow up session. This format was developed to provide introductory communication training to a larger number of families. The presentation will briefly review adult education principles, the Kolb's experiential learning model, the concept of "transformational learning" and the ways in which the model was incorporated in the communication training workshop for couples living with aphasia. Conclusion(s): Workshop results and benefits will be presented. Implications and future directions will be discussed.
