AB  - The members of the Alcoholic Anonymous have very high, but only partially met expectations towards the medical doctors. In search of the factors that determine the cooperation behaviour of the physicians, a sample of 49 medical professionals of the Swiss canton of Zurich was investigated on their perception of self-help groups as well as on their referrals of patients to the AA. It was found that most of the interviewed doctors knew AA and had already sent patients to them, mostly for aftercare. The majority of the doctors showed a critical, but positive self-evaluation of the experiences they made by cooperating with AA, and this self-evaluation turned out to be the most important determinant for the intensity of patient referrals. Strong associations do also exist between age and professional specialisation (general medicine vs. psychiatry) on one hand and perception of AA as well as cooperation behaviour on the other hand, whereas the form of treatment (inpatient vs. outpatient treatment) did not show to have a relevant impact on the perception as well as on the cooperation pattern.
