AB  - This non-experimental, retrospective, and quantitative study examined resiliency among children and adolescents from ages 9 to 18 years within a community mental health setting. The Vulnerability Index from the Resiliency Scales for Children and Adolescents – A Profile of Personal Strengths (RSCA) was used to analyze correlations among gender, age, academic success, socioeconomic status, and level of resilience. The total number of participants was 64, with 32 male and 32 female participants. Results showed a statistically significant difference in the Vulnerability Index for children, ages 9-14 with above average academic performance associated with lower vulnerability scores. However, for adolescents, the relationship was the reverse, with academic success correlated with greater vulnerability. Results indicated that males scored lower on vulnerability index scores than females. However, there was not a statistically significant difference between gender and academic success. Lastly, no statistically significant differences were found on academic success among those individuals at the poverty and non-poverty levels. This study may further the understanding of the levels of resilience among this particular population. This may be useful in guiding program development, curriculum design, and implementing additional supportive services for this group, including intervention strategies and treatment planning. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
