AB  - Introduction: Depression in older adults is common, persistent, and disabling. Major depressive disorder (MDD) and arthritis pain disorders co‐occur in 25‐40% of the population and are associated with reduced adherence to and effectiveness of pain management treatments. Biomarkers such as inflammatory cytokines and neurotrophic factors, including Brain Derived Neurotrophic Factor (BDNF) influence neural substrates in late‐life depression. Elevated levels of inflammatory cytokines have been linked to anhedonia, cognitive dysfunction, neurovegetative changes, and enhanced pain sensitivity which are clinical features of MDD. Antidepressant (ADT) treatments have been shown to reduce inflammatory cytokine levels and normalize decreased plasma BDNF levels in depressed individuals. Despite ADT advances, nearly two‐thirds of older adults treated for depression fail to achieve symptomatic and functional recovery. Barriers to effective pharmacologic treatment of geriatric depression include physiologic and financial burden of polypharmacy, medical comorbidities, elevated risk of iatrogenesis, and pharmacokinetic changes with aging. Non‐pharmacologic adjuncts to pharmacotherapy in elderly with MDD represent novel and important targets for interventions. One such non‐pharmacologic approach is Tai Chi, a mind‐body exercise that has demonstrated positive effects on muscular strength, reduced inflammatory biomarkers, and quality of life and is suitable for older adults with physical limitations due to chronic pain or gait impairment. A recent study by Lavretsky et al found that patients who combined participation in Tai Chi with ADT experienced significant improvement in MDD. We proposed that adjunctive Tai Chi exercise will benefit older adults with MDD and comorbid arthritis pain disorder. Methods: Primary objective: To compare the efficacy of 8 weeks of Tai Chi exercise adjunctive to existing ADT regimen vs. Mind‐Body Education (control) and ADT in elderly with MDD and comorbid arthritis pain. Secondary objective: To investigate the effect of Tai Chi exercise on inflammatory cytokines and depression biomarkers (BDNF). Study Design: Participants were randomized to receive Tai Chi (TC) or Mind‐Body Education Course, 2 hours per week for 8 weeks. Inclusion Criteria: ‐ Individuals age 60+ with DSM‐IV episode of non‐psychotic Major Depression (single episode or recurrent) confirmed by MINI (Mini‐International Neuropsychiatric Interview) ‐ Stable dose of ADT 30 days before enrollment ‐ Documented arthritis pain disorder ‐ 15 or higher score on the 17‐item SIGH‐D ‐ Cognitively intact (MMSE score at or above 26) ‐ Able to give informed consent ‐ Medically cleared to participate in study by referring physician Exclusion Criteria: ‐ History of psychotic disorders or dementia ‐ History of suicidal ideation or violent behavior or psychiatric hospitalization within 30 days of enrollment ‐ Currently participating in an exercise program ‐ History of substance abuse in the previous 6 months ‐ Primary neuropathic or non‐arthritis type pain diagnoses ‐ Medically unstable or severity of arthritis that limits participating in the study protocol Primary efficacy measure: Improvement in depressive symptoms as assessed by change from baseline to end of treatment in Structured Interview for Hamilton Depression Scale (SIGH‐D score). Secondary efficacy measure: Change in cytokines (Il‐1α, Il‐6, TNF‐α) and BDNF levels from baseline to end of treatment (week 8). Results: Participants were recruited from a geriatric medicine ambulatory clinic. Of 2,300 total patients, 615 had diagnoses of depression or arthritis. Of these, 124 were identified by chart review as meeting all study criteria. 24 participants were consented, and 6 participants failed the screening visit due to exclusion criteria.16 consented participants were then randomized in a 1:1 ratio. 60 minute TC sessions were conducted twice weekly by a certified instructor. Manual‐based education classes, delivered by trained personnel, served as the active comparator controlling for factors such as socialization and group support. Statistically significant reductions in SIGHD scores were noted for both groups at weeks 4 and 8, signifying improvement in depression severity in both groups at mid‐point and end of the study. No statistically significant differences were noted between groups in BDNF levels or Stroop (executive function) or arthritis measures (other secondary outcome measures). Statistically significant changes in the control group for IL‐1B (pro‐inflammatory cytokine) and Il‐4 (anti‐inflammatory cytokine) were noted. Conclusions: Depression comorbid with arthritis responds well to adjunctive treatment with Tai Chi. The impact of socialization and group interaction plays a significant role in depression outcomes. Further research in Tai Chi and other group interventions can add to the effective non‐pharmacologic treatment armamentarium for depression in older adults. (Table Presented).
