AB  - The article deals with a three-stage model of recovery for families affected by alcoholism. Family members have conformed their interactional patterns to the alcoholic's behavior. The treatment implication is that during recovery many of the survival skills of these families are rendered less effective and the family experiences far more chaos than it did during most of the active drinking. The first stage of family development is a time when boundaries and rules are established. Two sets of family rules must adjust and form a coherent pattern. When alcoholism occurs during this stage, a decision must be made to either challenge or accommodate the behavior. During the second stage, family life becomes more predictable and regulated as the family settles into living out the identity established in stage one. Therapeutic focus should be on daily functioning. During the third and final stage of the family life-cycle, the family must shift its focus from daily routines to future concerns. For the alcoholic family, stage three is marked by many losses, the most traumatic of which may be the loss of alcohol itself. In treatment, these families present with depression and anxiety without apparent cause. The clinician should present options that encourage the family to think flexibly and creatively.
