AB  - Purpose: Few studies have examined whether participants actually use skills taught in treatment sessions for Alcohol Use Disorder (during or after the treatment period), or the relationship of use of treatment skills with drinking outcome. In the current study, we tracked self-reported use of treatment skills during treatment and examined its association with drinking outcome among ServiceMembers and Veterans. Method(s): 23 Service Members or Veterans (21 male, Mage=36.9; 83% Caucasian) attended at least one of 15 treatment sessions (mean sessions attended = 12.3,SD = 3.69) in Alcohol Behavioral Couple Therapy-Military (ABCT-Mil). Every participant attended each session with a significant other, either a spouse/romantic partner, parent, sibling, or close friend). ABCT-Mil is a military-centric adaptation of an evidence-based ABCT protocol developed for civilian samples. Participants reported their use of treatment skills after sessions 5, 10, and 15. Data: The Use of Treatment Skills (UTS)measure (administered at sessions 5, 10, and 15) was created with 31 items, then categorized into 8 subcategories that reflect different types of CBT treatment skills, and one total UTS score. Skill categories included: drinker-focused alcohol coping skills interventions, drinker motivational enhancement interventions, partner-related interventions, couple therapy interventions, elective skills (e.g., depression, posttraumatic stress), general CBT skills, attending Alcoholics Anonymous, and taking medication. Total UTS and subscale scores were used. Daily drinking data were collected to yield our outcome variables, percent drinking days (PDD) and mean drinks per drinking day (MDPDD). Result(s): On average, the sample reported using 73% (SD = 14) of the treatment skills sometimes, often, or almost always (versus never/rarely) at session 5, 77%(SD = 17) of the treatment skills sometimes/often/almost always at session 10, and 77%(SD = 17) of the treatment skills sometimes/ often/almost always at follow-up. Repeated Measures ANOVA showed that participants' use of treatment skills reported at session 10 (F = 8.19,p = 0.015) and at session 15 (F = 4.92, p = 0.049) was associated with lower percent drinking days between sessions 5 and 15. Conclusion(s): Participants used a high percentage of treatment skills presented in ABCT-Mil, and use of skills was associated with less drinking during treatment at certain phases of treatment. We will also report differences in types of skills used, and patterns in use of treatment skills over time.
