AB  - Evaluation of a one semester (15 week) practicum course on social relationships offered at a Midwestern university to 14 people with psychiatric disability and 14 college undergraduates is described. The course combined social network and social skills techniques to help both undergraduates and people with psychiatric disability enhance their interpersonal skills and social ties. The course was structured to promote collaboration between participants in the classroom and direct application of knowledge in the community. Evaluation findings indicate that relative to a non-practicum control group, practicum participants' showed significant changes in the composition of their social networks, attitudes towards people with psychiatric disability, interpersonal self-efficacy and observational changes in social skills across the 15 week semester.
