AB  - This article looks at the growth and role of AA against the background of the prevailing alcoholism treatment system in different countries and at the influence of AA on treatment in general. It also discusses some impacts on AA as a social movement of contacts with the treatment system, and especially treatment based on the 12 steps. For the budding AA program in the US the importance of good contacts to professional alcohol treatment was obvious, even if individuals had had negative experiences and were critical. AA had to adopt its organization to the need of treatment in order to reach new members. AA was spread to most Anglo-Saxon and protestant countries and was reaching also into South-America before AA's relationship to professional treatment was seriously discussed within AA. This may be one of the reasons for the considerable variety in the early relations between AA and the treatment systems in different countries. The relationship between AA's growth and the treatment system in different countries is extremely complicated. A strong role of psychiatry within alcoholism treatment may have been a factor hindering the 'natural growth' of AA. The size of the alcohol specific treatment system does not seem per se to explain the early growth of AA. The present contacts between AA and professional treatment varies considerably. In some countries, AA is rather isolated from the treatment system, in others it has good contacts but is strictly autonomous and in still other countries the AA ideology has reached a position close to hegemony. Institutional treatment based on the 12 steps marks a new phase in the relation between AA and professional treatment. From the late 1970's and especially during the 1980's the treatment model has spread to many countries outside the US. The growth of the 12 step treatment has blurred the border between self-help and professional work within AA. The professionalization of AA challenges especially the 12th tradition. It seems possible to argue in favour of two viewpoints: The viewpoint that the 12 step institutional treatment means a special threat to AA as a self-help movement, and for the viewpoint that the 1980's has marked the golden age of AA, when its ideology was recognized in several countries as the one that responded to the demands of society, ideologically and financially.
