AB  - Compares a group of 32 psychiatrically ill postpartum women who were admitted to a Canadian general hospital psychiatric unit with their infants to a group of 26 psychiatrically ill postpartum women hospitalized on the same unit who refused admission of their infants. Ss admitted with their infants were more likely to be older, living with the infants' father, in a stable residence and job, in hospital for a longer time, and caring for their babies at 2 yr follow-up in contrast to the Ss who were admitted without their infants. The 2 groups were diagnostically different, with joint admission mothers likely to suffer from an affective psychotic illness, while the mothers without infants were more likely to suffer from personality disorder or substance abuse. The effects of mother–infant admission and some of the practical problems encountered are discussed. (French abstract) (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
