AB  - Objectives: At the conclusion of this presentation, participants will understand how the START (Systemic, Therapeutic, Assessment, Resources, Treatment) model and programs like it achieve their goals of decreasing emergency department visits and hospitalization. The presenters will review ideas critical to support systems, including building relationships and supportive services. Method(s): Principles of positive psychology are the underlying philosophical approaches of the START model. The START Team includes a director, clinical director (psychologist), medical director (psychiatrist), and master's level team of leaders and clinical coordinators. For each case, a coordinator is assigned and conducts intake and assessments, which feed into a START plan. Based on this plan, the coordinator will travel into the community regularly to perform outreach services to the individuals, facilitate needed medical and mental health assessments and treatments, and develop common goals with the community treatment team. A cross-system crisis plan is developed and shared with the system, with the START crisis outreach available 24 hours a day, 7 days a week. Data are collected on all individuals enrolled in the START program from a large online database, which is used for both quality improvement and research. Result(s): The data collected support the finding that enrollment in active START intervention decreases the rates of emergency department presentations and hospitalizations and reduces mental health symptoms as measured by the ABC model. For this presentation, several case examples will be provided to demonstrate the model and the psychiatrist's involvement. Principles and suggestions about the psychiatrist's involvement will be discussed that can be extrapolated to other practice settings to treat individuals with intellectual and developmental disabilities when START is not present in a region. Conclusion(s): The START model offers a comprehensive approach, including 3 levels of intervention: 1) primary level (eg, training to systems, building capacity); 2) secondary level (eg, consultation and training about an individual); and 3) tertiary level (eg, crisis response, resource center stay). Useful themes and findings from the START model will be discussed for the community psychiatrist to use in practice when the formal program is not available. For instance, the reframing of symptoms is being rooted in anxiety or trauma, with a focus on modulating psychosocial factors, avoiding polypharmacy, monitoring for side effects, and using more psychosocial interventions and focused, simpler medication regimens. ID, TREAT, CCCopyright © 2019
