AB  - Background: Major depressive disorder (MDD) is now considered the leading cause of disability worldwide [1] in part due to its high prevalence (which exceeds 300 million) and high rates of comorbidities, recurrence, chronicity and treatment-resistance. These indicate that MDD is treated suboptimally despite a multitude of effective interventions and well-regarded best-practice treatment guidelines. To improve the management of MDD, the nature and extent of 'gaps' in care pathways need to be understood. This project aimed to: 1 Identify the current 'treatment gaps' and patient needs along the care pathway, and determine the extent of these gaps (i.e. discrepancy between best- and current-practice). 2 Propose policy recommendation on how to improve the care pathway (i.e. minimise treatment gaps). Method(s): Care pathway analysis- The project working group agreed upon a set of relevant treatment gaps, a priori, based on the current gold standard 'stepped care' MDD management guidelines [2]. Data was gathered from a variety of sources pertaining to each treatment gap in each country (UK, Sweden, Germany, Italy, Portugal and Hungary). Consensus policy recommendations- Based on the care pathway analysis, a modified-Delphi approach was undertaken for attaining expert consensus on proposed recommendations [3]. A set of 35 possible recommendations was developed by a core group based on our previous results. A panel of 15 experts across European countries (including GP's, psychiatrists, people with lived experience) rated their views and suggestions across three survey rounds. Recommendations were accepted where >80% agreed the item was essential/important. Result(s): Care pathway analysis- Data integrated across data sources and countries suggested that: ~50% of depressive episodes are detected in healthcare services with an average delay to treatment of ~1-5 years from onset; at any one time ~25-50% of patients are treated with particularly low rates of psychological therapy; after treatment initiation, ~30-65% of patients seen < 3 months; only ~ 5-25% of patients can access secondary (psychiatric) services and limited/no data on access to specialist mood disorders services. Consensus policy recommendations- 28 recommendations to optimise care pathways included: 1. Enhancing depression detection (pathway entry) via improved information provision to patients, increased service availability, integrated self-management e-mental health tools with practice. 2. Improving treatment provision via e.g. decision-support tools, information provision to patients, encouraging patient preference, prescribing support tools (e.g. integrate electronic records, shared-care responsibilities), resources for psychological therapies. 3. Facilitating continuity of follow-up after treatment through self-management tools feeding back key information to clinicians, automatic appointment scheduling, measurement-based care, risk factor screening for intensity of follow-up required. 4. Increasing access to specialist care through enhanced specialism clinician training programs, optimising criteria for accepting psychiatric referrals, increased service resources, integrating specialists into primary care, systems for planning and executing transition between services. Conclusion(s): There are substantial and concerning treatment gaps in depression care across Europe, from the proportion of people not entering care pathways to those stagnating in primary care with impairing and persistent illness. A wide range of recommendations can be made to enhance care throughout the pathway. Conflict of interest Disclosure statement: Honorarium received from Lundbeck.Copyright © 2021
