AB  - A report of the effects of continuous, long-term group therapy on an 'open' group of M alcoholics (N=4-7), meeting with the same therapist over 11 yrs. S's were able to abstain from drinking for substantial periods, but experienced the same tensions which had provoked alcoholism. Marked difficulties in their functioning & interpersonal relationships continued, & reinforced original patterns of soc isolation. The supportive mechanism of group therapy developed a greater capacity for self-examination in the S's, & as interpersonal problems were resolved,& tensions decreased, S's demonstrated a better ability to relate to others & to the community. Their pattern of soc living gradually transformed; acceptance & support were found in community contacts, & alcohol & Alcoholics Anonymous assumed secondary importance. The change is attributed largely to emotional maturation & S's new capacity to compromise the ideal self with the reality of individual limitations. It was concluded that S's, despite the long duration of therapy, had utilized treatment constructively. J. Atkins.
