AB  - Tested a preventive mental health intervention with low income parent–child dyads among parents who were having problems with their children's behavior. 89 parents, of 96 children (aged 2–5 yrs), completed pre- and postintervention assessments. Families were assigned to a minimal treatment control group or a more extensive treatment experimental group. The more extensive intervention produced significantly more improvement in parents' ratings of children's symptoms of attention deficit and internalizing symptoms than did the control intervention. Both groups improved with respect to parents' ratings of children's oppositional symptoms. Change in parent ratings of children's behavior was correlated with change in blind observers' ratings of parents' behavior. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
