AB  - This article discusses a private-public partnership to deliver population-level integrated care to low-income seniors in Pennsylvania. In recent years, collaborative care models in primary care practices have improved the accessibility and quality of mental health care delivered in those settings. These models have led to better outcomes for a variety of patient populations. However, implementation of collaborative care models has largely been limited to closed health systems that serve a defined population of insured patients. In a joint effort, the University of Pennsylvania’s Department of Psychiatry and the state’s Department of Aging (DoA) have tapped some of their key resources to create such a program: SUSTAIN (SUpporting Seniors receiving Treatment And INtervention). SUSTAIN has two broad goals: to identify community dwelling elders at risk of poor health outcomes, including nursing home admission, and to support those patients and their primary care prescribers to manage their mental health care, consistent with evidence-based guidelines, across the state. SUSTAIN is an innovative partnership that combines the power of a large pharmacy database with a software-supported collaborative care program, both of which are now widely available, making this a model program that can be easily replicated. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
