AB  - Most of the tens of thousands of people with psychiatric illnesses live in the community. Unfortunately, the stigma to which they are subjected reduces their chances of rehabilitation and integration into society. Planning interventions to reduce this stigma requires knowledge and understanding about how the Israeli public conceives of these people. This article presents data from a study which was conducted among a representative sample of Israeli adults aged 21 and over (N = 1,583). When asked to describe an individual with a psychiatric illness, more than half of the participants cited strange behavior. Characteristics such as problems relating to reality, impulsive behavior, violence, and depression were cited by one-fifth of the participants for each variable, respectively. Psychiatric illnesses are perceived as having a genetic-chemical etiology on the one hand, and psychosocial etiology on the other. Although perceptions of manifestations of deviation from the norm vary, violence is often perceived as one the most salient manifestations. Regarding other parameters - education, intelligence, and ability to work - the public perceives people with psychiatric illness as being normative. The article briefly discusses the implications of the findings for interventions aimed at reducing stigmatization of people with psychiatric illness.
