AB  - Eating disorders (EDs) and alcohol use disorders frequently co-occur; however, there is a lack of integrated treatments. Twenty-four women were recruited for a study assessing feasibility of implementing in women with co-occurring EDs and substance use disorders (SUDs) the Women's Recovery Group, a gender-specific group therapy for SUDs. These analyses present baseline data on severity of alcohol and other drug use and SUD treatment history in order to better understand the need for integrated treatment in this population. Women admitted to ED residential treatment were included if they were >=18 years and had a co-occurring SUD. Participants were predominately white (96%), with mean age of 21 years (SD = 2.6). Baseline assessments included the AUDIT-C, Eating Disorders Examination Questionnaire (EDE-Q), and assessments of treatment service utilization and barriers to seeking treatment for SUDs. Alcohol was the most frequently used substance (68%), then cannabis (56%), and prescribed stimulants (20%). Participants reported an average of 9 days (SD = 9.8) of alcohol or drug use in the 30 days prior to admission, and 17% reported drinking 6 or more days/week in the year prior to admission. Mean AUDIT-C score was 6.3 (SD = 3.0; range 0- 12) and mean EDE-Q score was 4.3 (SD = 1.4). In the 30 days prior to admission, 12% reported attending an AA meeting and 28% were in individual therapy for substance use; 50% reported that problems related to alcohol or drug use led to their current treatment admission. The most common barriers to seeking SUD treatment (Likert scale: 0 = Not important; 4 = Very important) were: "My eating problems were a higher priority" (M = 2.4; SD = 0.9), "I thought I could handle it on my own" (M = 2.3, SD = 0.8), and "I liked getting drunk/high" (M = 2.3, SD = 0.9). Motivation to remain in SUD treatment was rated slightly above average (M = 56.5, SD = 33.4; range 0-100). Women with co-occurring EDs and SUDs who enter ED treatment are a special population whose alcohol and drug use disorders are often under-treated. Integrated treatment addressing alcohol and other drug use in the context of the eating disorder, delivered in the psychiatric services setting, may prove efficacious for this population. Increasing motivation and addressing barriers to treatment may be important factors in an integrated treatment for EDs and SUDs.
