AB  - Cigarette smoking is a known risk factor for many life-threatening diseases, particularly in the elderly, who are already at high risk for stroke and cardiovascular compromise. The long-term care setting may provide an optimal environment to encourage and support smoking cessation and abstinence in this population group. A number of interventions can be instituted by health care providers in order to reduce the incidence of smoking among the elderly in long-term care, and this article reviews several approaches to smoking cessation that can be implemented at minimum cost and time to the facilities. Reducing smoking rates in long-term care residents may potentially improve the quality of life for these people, decreasing the morbidity and mortality associated with tobacco use.
