AB  - Presents some explanations for the doubling of the rate of suicide in young adults since 1950. The diagnoses and population groups whose high rates of suicide contribute most of this increase are detailed. The factors leading to suicide are presented for each group, and preventative interventions are developed from the analyses. Mood disorder, schizophrenia, personality disorders, substance abuse, and medical illness are examined in terms of risk factors. Suicide among Native Americans, ghetto youth, prisoners, soldiers, and college students is discussed. It is suggested that institutions such as prisons, colleges, and military bases need to adopt the prevention programs developed by psychiatric hospitals to deal with suicide. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
