AB  - Background: Pulmonary Arterial Hypertension (PAH) is a frequent complication of adult congenital heart disease, and is typically due to increased pulmonary blood flow through a left to right shunt. Chronic loading of the pulmonary circulation leads to increased pulmonary vascular resistance and shunt reversal, known as Eisenmenger Syndrome (ES). Patients with congenital heart disease associated PAH (CHD-PAH) and ES are classified as WHO Group I, and therefore benefit from treatment with selective pulmonary artery vasodilator therapy. The addition of a prostacyclin in this group can be challenging, due to complex medication delivery systems, side effect profiles, or concomitant diagnose. In the pivotal GRIPHON trial patients with ES were excluded, and to date there is no data with regards to oral prostacyclin treatment in this group. We present the first cohort of four patients with ES that were successfully initiated on selexipag, an oral selective IP prostacyclin-receptor agonist. Method(s): This is a single center retrospective review of four patients with ES. Each patient had a NYHA functional class of III symptoms. Patients were started on selexipag 200 mcg twice daily and titrated weekly to their highest tolerated dose. Patients were followed at our outpatient PAH center and their symptoms recorded in the EHR. Result(s): A total of 4 patients with ES were treated with an age range between 19-65 years old. Each patient underwent an initial right heart catheterization with an average mean PAP of 54mmHg. Three of the patients had associated developmental delays such as Down syndrome and cerebral palsy, making parenteral initiation of a prostacyclin difficult. They were successfully started on selexipag with improvement of their functional classification from NYHA III to NYHA II at a 3 month follow up. Discussion(s): Selxipag is an oral prostacyclin-receptor agonist approved for the treatment of patients with PAH WHO Group I, functional class II-III symptoms. In a recently completed multi centered phase 3 study (GRIPHON), selexipag was shown to reduce death and hospitalization due to PAH. The addition of selexipag in ES population is a promising alternative to parenteral or inhaled therapies. In our center, selexipag was successfully initiated in 4 patients with ES with a demonstrated improvement in functional status on short-term follow up. Conclusion(s): This review highlights the successful initiation of selxipag in patients with ES, in whom parenteral therapy via complex medical delivery systems is not an option due associated medical comorbidities such as Down syndrome or Cerebral palsy. [Table Presented].
