AB  - This paper analyses the immediate and short-term impacts of super cyclone of 1999 on different aspects of life and livelihood of women, who are considered as one of the vulnerable sections of a society. The different aspects of impact assessment include health, nutrition, drinking water availability, sanitation and other activities of women centering households management; the extent of psychological trauma and other post traumatic stress disorders reflected in life processes of women; disruption in socio-cultural practices and loss of asset, income and employment. The coping responses of different category of women in the aftermath of super cyclone are also analyzed. The study is based on a field survey conducted in 13 villages of two worst affected coastal districts namely Jagatsinghpur and Cuttack of Orissa. A stratified random sample constituting 112 women was taken by dividing women population into 11 strata. The super cyclone has devastated the socio-economic and cultural life of women with unbelievable impacts on psychic and other life processes of women not only for its super cyclonic nature but also for their less preparedness and acquaintances to such an unexpected tragedy. It is suggested to strengthen cyclone warning system in Orissa as it was found that all the cyclone-warning systems were not operational just before the cyclone. The evacuation efficiency should be raised to avoid heavy loss of life. Awareness camps, community contingency plan and gender specific training should be provided to women to defend themselves during the disasters. R&D efforts should be enhanced for better understanding of the effects of disasters and to suggest pragmatic action plan for effective prevention, preparedness, relief, rehabilitation and reconstruction.
