AB  - Substance abuse persists as one of the most challenging problems in this country. The vocational intent of this study was to provide empirical data in the rehabilitation of community-based substance abusers. This study investigated the relationship between selected demographics, employment, treatment program type, and work interference level as measured by the MMPI-2. Low self-esteem (LSE) and work interference level (WRK) were examined. Age, race, and gender explained 5% of the variance in work interference. ANOVA results (n = 404) showed significant differences in work interference level by age group as defined by Super's (1957) age/stage theory and between treatment program groups. Work interference level and low self-esteem were found to be highly correlated (r =.77). Employed and unemployed substance abusers (n = 79) differed significantly on work interference level. Other findings reported work interference also related to anxiety (r =.79) and depression (r =.76). Conclusions were drawn with regard to the need for treatment matching, individual assessment, and viewing addiction ecologically as a psychosocial as well as an individual problem. Further research was suggested in the application of the rehabilitation process to incarcerated substance abusers in a community corrections environment. An Individualized Substance-Abuse Treatment Plan was proposed as an intervention in the treatment of substance abusers. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
