AB  - Examined the usefulness of the General Health Questionnaire (GHQ) in screening a student population for psychological disturbance, using 1,279 1st-yr undergraduates. Two matched groups of potentially vulnerable Ss (n = 52) were identified. One group was left to its own resources; the other was offered psychotherapeutic intervention, the effects of which were measured by the number of consultations with general practitioners, type of treatment, and rate of withdrawal from the university. Results show that although Ss in the intervention group had fewer consultations, lower GHQ scores at follow-up, and fewer withdrawals from the university, none of the differences achieved statistical significance due to the small numbers involved. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2018 APA, all rights reserved)
