AB  - Purpose: Various Internet-based or mobile-based therapeutic interventions for substance use problems have developed to address the treatment gap.We developed a web-based relapse prevention program based on an existing face-to-face program for Japanese drug users and conducted a pilot randomized controlled trial (RCT). However, we did not find statistically significant effects of the program on abstinence. This study was a secondary data analysis of a pilot RCT with consideration for the severity of drug dependence, which explored subgroups with large effects of the program. Method(s): Outpatients with a diagnosis of drug abuse/dependence were recruited from six psychiatric hospitals in Japan. The participants (n = 48) were randomly assigned to an eight-week, six-session web-based relapse prevention program (intervention: n = 23) or a web-based self-monitoring only (control: n = 25). We tested efficacy on abstinence during the intervention for 56 days as a primary outcome in a subgroup divided by the total term of outpatient treatment (long-term: >=3 years vs. short-term: < 3 years). We included 44 participants who provided complete data of daily drug use or abstinence during the intervention to this secondary analysis (intervention: n = 19, control: n = 25). We compared the consecutive abstinence duration fromthe primary abused drug between the intervention and the control using a t-test and calculated the effect sizes (Cohen's d). Resuts: In the subgroup with short-term outpatient treatment, the intervention group (n = 10, mean = 56.0, SD = 0.0) significantly maintained more prolonged abstinence than the control group (n = 12, mean = 42.3, SD = 19.2; t = 2.46, p = 0.03, Cohen's d = 0.96). Conclusion(s): This web-based program was more effective in outpatients who received treatment for a relatively short term. Patients who needed long-term treatmentmight have complicated problems other than drug use and bemore suitable for intensive treatment. This study suggests that we need to focus on patients who can receive benefits fromthe web-based program when we provide the program as an alternative treatment and should recruit patients who will havemore significant effects in a future RCT.
