AB  - This study evaluated treatment outcome at a public substance abuse treatment center. A variation of the quasi-experimental, nonequivalent control group design enabled the assessment of 65 participants in an intensive, 21-day treatment group and 126 participants in a comparison (detox) no-treatment group. Treatment effectiveness was conceptualized as the satisfaction of expectations of those affected by substance abuse and its treatment, ranging from participants and families to employers to society at large. The key psychosocial constructs used included (1) physical/psychological health and subjective well-being; and (2) social role/functioning (including employment, legal, marital/family/significant others). The results indicated that psychosocial factors improve during traditional treatment, even though they are not the focus of treatment. Suggestions are provided for including components of treatment directly addressing psychosocial changes. In addition, the role of social workers in substance abuse treatment is explored including a discussion of the relevance of social workers' assumptions, training, and values that enable social workers to have a unique perspective in substance abuse treatment. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
