AB  - Since de-institutionalization in the 1980s, New York State has struggled to maximize human potential while minimizing costs to meet the needs of the seriously and persistently mentally ill. Outcome research aims to clarify the level of care needed to assist such individuals in meeting their goals. This dissertation examined the relationship between diagnosis, supports and outcome. 825 closed charts were reviewed from Intensive Psychiatric Rehabilitation Treatment (IPRT) (N = 577) and Clinic (N = 248) programs at three service sites in one agency in New York City. Each of the three IPRT programs was assessed for fidelity to the Boston University Psychiatric Rehabilitation model. The relationship between client-centered variable, housing, and employment outcomes were: (1) Factors assessing work on program entry or exit predicted positive vocational outcome. (2) Independent residential status on program entry, program type, employment status on program exit and goals reached by discharge predicted independent residential outcome. (3) Detailed clinical description, including primary diagnosis, personality and cognitive disorders, substance abuse history, diagnosis and length of sobriety, as well as hospitalization frequency, recency, and age of onset did not predict housing or vocational outcome. (4) Neither general or vocational support use predicted employment or housing outcome. (5) Goal statements made on entry and goal assessment forms were not predictive of goal outcomes. (6) Participants receiving IPRT did not reach their goals at a higher rate than those receiving Clinic treatment alone. Clinic groups entered treatment with signs of significantly greater community functioning, some of which were predictive of outcomes. IPRT participants were significantly less likely to live independently on program entry and discharge than their Clinic peers, calling into question treatment effects for residential outcome. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
