AB  - Objective(s): To investigate the prevalence of comorbid conditions and age related conditions among those aging with spinal cord injury and disease. To compare these data to current Medicare data on adults over 65 and those with disabilities. To determine the association of age and time since injury or disease onset to these rates of prevalence. Design(s): Cross-sectional survey based on subjects interviews. Setting(s): 11 NIDILRR SCI Model Systems centers and two Rehabilitation Research Training Centers (RRTC) on aging with SCI and disability in 10 different geographical areas. General community setting. Participant(s): 117 persons with SCI/D (traumatic and nontraumatic). Inclusive sample. Ages 45-85. Intervention(s): N/A. Main Outcome Measure(s): Comorbidities and Age Related Conditions Questionnaire - during phone interviews subjects were asked about the presence of a number of comorbidities and other conditions associated with aging and spinal cord injury. This measure was developed based on items used by Medicare to assess the presence of comorbidities in the elderly population. Additional questions were added associated with aging with SCI. Result(s): An analysis of prevalence of co-morbidities in this sample shows the highest self-reported prevalence for the conditions arthritis (40%), hypertension (39%), hyperlipidemia (29%), overweight (27%), depression 24%and diabetes (15%). When comparing those with SCI over 65 years of age with a Medicare sample, several conditions show higher rates among those with SCI: hypertension (63% versus 61%); atrial fibrillation (26% versus 9%); arthritis (53% vs. 31%); osteoporosis (16% vs. 8%); depression (21% vs. 12%); and diabetes (32% vs. 28%). These data need to be interpreted with caution since only 19 members of our sample were 65 years old or older. Two conditions differed as a function of years since injury, atrial fibrillation (p<.05) and osteoporosis (p<.05).Age related conditions with the highest prevalence were significant joint pain (60%), sleep difficulties (58%), significant muscle pain (49%), UTIs (46%), falls (44%) and Bowel Incontinence (44%). Prevalence rates did not differ between those younger and older than 65. The only condition to differ as a function of years since injury was bladder incontinence. Data were analyzed using Chi-square tests and ANOVAs. Conclusion(s): Current data suggests higher rates of comorbid conditions among those with SCI/D when comparing to an elderly normal population and age related conditions among those with aging with SCI were also significantly higher than the normal population. These findings support the need to assess these conditions among those with SCI/D to target early preventive and treatment strategies to address these issues.
